SUMMARY:Rust-BPF updates
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Alexei Starovoitov · https://lpc.events/event/20
 /contributions/2439/\n\nSupport for all of Rust required rethinking of how
  the verifier processes instructions. Stack liveness\, SCEV\, indirect cal
 ls are known building blocks while more fundamental rewrite is still neces
 sary. The talk will cover completed and upcoming work areas in the verifie
 r\, LLVM\, GCC\, rustc\, libbpf.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2439/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2393@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103000
SUMMARY:Security Features status update
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Justin Stitt\, Kees Cook · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2393/\n\nAnother year of work is behind us\, wi
 th lots of progress across GCC\, Clang\, and Rust to provide the Linux ker
 nel with a variety of security features. Let's review and discuss where we
  are with parity between toolchains\, approaches to solving open problems\
 , and exploring new features.\n\nParity reached since last year:\n- Variou
 s little behavioral corner-case bug fixes\n\nIn progress:\n- Overflow Beha
 vior Types (needed in GCC)\n- forward-edge CFI (GCC KCFI at v14)\n- covera
 ge-sanitizer stack-depth tracking (needed in GCC)\n\nStalled / needs atten
 tion:\n- -fbounds-safety language extension (slow in Clang)\n- __strong ty
 pedef (needs design finalized)\n- Link Time Optimization for GCC kernel su
 pport\n- backward-edge CFI (x86 CET shadow stack\, kernel mode)
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2393/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2345@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
SUMMARY:Untangling convoluted performance regression
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Jan Kara · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2345/\n\nIn this talk I will speak about a performance regres
 sion in DB2 backup speed reported by one of SUSE's customer last year. Due
  to various reasons the analysis was rather convoluted so I will go throug
 h the dead ends we have explored as well as leads which eventually allowed
  us to track down and fix the problem. Overall we demonstrate on a practic
 al example how various tools for analyzing IO related performance regressi
 ons can be used.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2345/
END:VEVENT
