BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2493@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T101000
SUMMARY:Aspects of Dependable Linux Systems
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Kate Stewart\, Philipp Ahmann · 
 https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2493/\n\nIn regulated industries
 \, Linux is widely used due to its strong software capabilities in areas s
 uch as dependability\, reliability\, and robustness. These industries foll
 ow best practices in terms of processes for requirements\, design\, verifi
 cation\, and change management. These processes are defined in standards t
 hat are typically not accessible to the open source kernel community.\n\nH
 owever\, since these standards represent best practices\, they can be inco
 rporated into structured development environments like the Linux kernel ev
 en without the knowledge of such standards. The kernel development process
  is trusted in critical infrastructure systems as it already covers many p
 rocess elements directly or indirectly.\n\nThe purpose of this session is 
 to initiate a discussion on what is currently available and what may be mi
 ssing in order to enhance the dependability and robustness of Linux kernel
 -based systems. How can the artifacts be connected? Where are the appropri
 ate places to maintain them? And who is the best responsible for each elem
 ent of the development lifecycle?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2493/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-session-277@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Nova GPU & DRM Rust Workshop
LOCATION:Club B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:https://lpc.events/event/20/sessions/277/\n\nThis workshop will
  center on Nova\, the upstream Rust-based kernel driver for NVIDIA GPUs\, 
 and on Rust in the DRM subsystem in general.\n\nOn the Nova side\, discuss
 ion topics will include the design and evolution of the firmware APIs expo
 sed by the GPU System Processor (GSP)\, in particular the new GMC APIs\, a
 s well as user-space submission interfaces\, compute APIs\, and interactio
 ns with the core kernel (device / driver APIs\; locking and lifetimes\; me
 mory management APIs). Beyond Nova\, the workshop will cover the shared Ru
 st DRM infrastructure — device / driver core and initialization\, TTM/GE
 M\, GPUVM\, job submission and scheduling — and how to properly tie thes
 e components into driver lifecycle design.\n\nPotential key participants a
 re members of the Nova team at NVIDIA and Red Hat\, contributors from the 
 DRM and Rust-for-Linux communities\, and developers of parallel Rust drive
 r efforts such as Tyr (Arm\, Collabora\, Google)\, the Asahi AGX driver\, 
 and rvkms.\n\nThe workshop aims to keep Nova and the Rust DRM infrastructu
 re closely tied to the needs of the graphics / compute stack in Linux\, an
 d to foster collaboration around shared challenges in GPU driver design.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/sessions/277/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2635@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
SUMMARY:AF_XDP BoF
LOCATION:Club D\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Björn Töpel · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2635/\n\nAF_XDP has grown considerably in function
 ality and hardware support\, but recent netdev discussions have exposed ga
 ps in semantics\, queue lifecycle\, offloads\, and cross-driver testing. T
 his BoF is intended to align on the main pain points and identify concrete
  next steps.\n\nTopics can\, e.g.\, be:\n\n * AF_XDP semantics: copy vs ze
 ro-copy\, multi-buffer\, UMEM constraints\n * TX/RX metadata and offloads\
 n * Queue ownership\, RSS contexts\, netkit queue leasing\n * Driver capab
 ility discovery and consistency\n * xskxceiver gaps and AF_XDP hardware co
 mpliance testing\n * Running AF_XDP tests in drivers/net/hw / NIPA CI\n * 
 Open issues and next steps
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2635/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2627@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100100
SUMMARY:Android MC Intro
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2627/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2627/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2450@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T102000
SUMMARY:Performance Tools and Prompts
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Brendan Gregg 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2450/\n\nA new era is upon pe
 rformance engineering where we ask AI to do much of our analysis and think
 ing: we run "performance prompts" (and skills and agents) instead of "perf
 ormance tools." These can analyze all sources\, including flame graphs\, b
 cc and bpftrace tools (eBPF)\, Ftrace\, PMCs\, and MSRs\; they can also pr
 opose new metrics and tools. How well does it currently work\, and what do
 es this mean for the future of system monitoring and observability\, inclu
 ding for flame graphs and eBPF tools. There are many questions to ponder (
 more questions than answers)\, including: Should one day we include prompt
 s\, skills\, and agents in the kernel tree\, just as we do performance too
 ls? This talk discusses questions and challenges that we will all likely b
 e facing\, and new opportunities\, including example performance prompts.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2450/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2556@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100100
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T101500
SUMMARY:Vendor hooks in Android kernels? Why? What do vendors do with them?
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Todd Kjos · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribu
 tions/2556/\n\nAndroid’s Generic Kernel Image (GKI) enforces a single bi
 nary kernel\, restricting Android ecosystem partners from modifying core k
 ernel code. To allow partner-specific optimizations\, Google introduced "v
 endor hooks"\, based on Linux tracepoints\, to act as in-kernel callback r
 egistration points. Partners use loadable kernel modules to register handl
 ers for these hooks to implement partner-specific features.\n\nBut what ar
 e they actually used for?\n\nIn this session we'll discuss the motivation 
 behind providing vendor hooks and some of the common optimization patterns
  implemented by partners across various kernel subsystems including schedu
 ler and mm. The discussion will be based on the actual usage of these hook
 s across major Android partners.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2556/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2424@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T100500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
SUMMARY:A Compressed RAM Service
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Gregory Price · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2424/\n\nCompressed RAM (where hardwa
 re offloads compression) presents a particularly novel problem for the ker
 nel: the device fundamentally lies about its true capacity - while the ker
 nel is written to assume any `struct page` it can get will always be backe
 d by real capacity.\n\nUnlike zswap/zram - these devices provide cacheline
 /byte access to compressed memory\, their memory can remain page-table map
 ped and page-cache present without generate faults on read-access (no COW\
 , no software decompression step etc).\n\nLets discuss what it would take 
 to formalize support for a compressed RAM service that otherwise "looks li
 ke" normal memory (migratable\, mappable\, page-cache-able - but maybe not
  directly writable).\n\nAll but one feature required to achieve support al
 ready exists in the kernel (or has existed at one time).\n\n 1. Anon Memor
 y support: Page Table Write Protection (COW\, KSM)\n 2. Page Cache support
 : Clean Cache (clean page eligible only)\n 3. Reclaim and Demotion\n 4. Me
 mory Ballooning (dynamic sizing)\n 5. Free Page Reporting (stale data trim
 ming)\n 6. Memory Tiering (Hotness / NUMA Balancing)\n 7. Strictly control
 led NUMA memory allocation (private nodes)\n\nI will present a tested comp
 ressed ram service (mm/cram.c) that achieves near-native performance to DR
 AM under TAOBench and FIO benchmarks\, and has been tested under most in-t
 ree filesystems for pagecache correctness.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2424/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2540@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T101500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104000
SUMMARY:Towards Program Verification of the Linux Kernel Library XArray
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Corinn Tiffany · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2540/\n\nXArray is a data structure used in man
 y Linux kernel components\, most notably the page cache. Its API contracts
  are not precisely documented\, making it challenging to understand caller
  obligations and which invariants any changes to the implementation must m
 aintain over the structure. Since its integration into the Linux source tr
 ee in 2019\, errors in the use of the library have caused bugs such as mem
 ory leaks [1\, 2]\, and the library code itself has suffered from bugs lik
 e race conditions and null pointer dereferences [3\, 4]. Recent case studi
 es [5\, 6] suggest that applying program verification to Linux kernel sour
 ce code is a promising means to make requirements structured\, explicit\, 
 and machine-checkable against the implementation.\nThis talk presents prog
 ress verifying the core load and store APIs of the XArray library. So far\
 , we have verified the xa_load() function and its callees\, and are workin
 g on the verification of xa_store(). We will discuss how the structured sp
 ecification of the original library can be used to build confidence in the
  Rust re-implementation that is under active development [7]. By formalizi
 ng and checking the API’s precise contracts\, we can validate the parity
  of the Rust port’s behavior in critical kernel clients.\n\n[1]  Matthew
  Wilcox. “mm/huge_memory: Fix xarray node memory leak.” url: https://g
 it.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?h=v6.19&
 id=69a37a8ba1b4\n\n[2] Yang Yang. “swap_state: update shadow_nodes for a
 nonymous page.” url: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/tor
 valds/linux.git/commit/?h=v6.19&id=5649d113ffce\n\n[3] Matthew Wilcox. “
 XArray: Disallow sibling entries of nodes.” url: https://git.kernel.org/
 pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/commit/?h=v6.19&id=63b1898fffc
 d\n\n[4] Matthew Wilcox. XArray: Fix xas_create_range() when multi-order e
 ntry present. url: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/3e3c658055c002
 900982513e289398a1aad4a488\n\n[5] Julia Lawall\, Keisuke Nishimura\, and
  Jean-Pierre Lozi. 2024. Should We Balance? Towards Formal Verification of
  the Linux Kernel Scheduler. SAS 2024.\n\n[6] Julia Lawall\, Keisuke Nis
 himura\, and Jean-Pierre Lozi. 2025. Understanding Linux Kernel Code throu
 gh Formal Verification: A Case Study of the Task-Scheduler Function select
 _idle_core. OLIVIERFEST '25.\n\n[7] Daniel Gomez\, "rxarray\," commit 0ae6
 dd57e31d\, Linux kernel development tree. url:\nhttps://git.kernel.org/pub
 /scm/linux/kernel/git/da.gomez/linux.git/commit/?id=0ae6dd57e31d9e436ee935
 288767f53e118db8c0
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2540/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2565@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T101500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103000
SUMMARY:Kernel Lock Contention Hotspots Causing Frame Drops on Android Main
 line
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Barry Song\, Bo Zhang\, Hongru Zhang · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2565/\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nAndroid 
 UI rendering is latency-sensitive — at 90Hz\, each frame has only ~11ms 
 time budget. When a UI-critical thread (e.g.\, RenderThread) is blocked in
  the kernel\, the frame is likely to be dropped.\n\nWe profiled 40 popular
  Android applications on a Pixel 6 (kernel 6.18.0-mainline\, 90Hz refresh 
 rate)\, using ftrace lock_contention events correlated with Perfetto Frame
 Timeline data\, to confirm causal links between lock contention and droppe
 d frames.\n\nThe results show that approximately 14% of all dropped frames
  are causally attributable to kernel lock contention. The contention conce
 ntrates in 5 subsystems that account for over 90% of all lock-caused frame
  drops.\n\nWe would like to present these findings and discuss optimizatio
 n directions with the community.\n\n## Top 5 Problems\n\n### 1. Mali GPU D
 river\n\nThe Mali GPU kernel driver uses multiple locks in its internal pa
 ths such as job submission and scheduling\, and the RenderThread is observ
 ed to be occasionally blocked on these locks\, causing frame drops.\n\n**T
 o discuss:** What optimization approaches are available for reducing lock 
 contention in the Mali GPU driver? Is this a known issue?\n\n### 2. mmap_l
 ock\n\nSeveral mm paths contend on the same per-process mmap_lock on both 
 write side (e.g.\, mmap\, mprotect) and read side (page faults).\n\nRFC PA
 TCH: https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260804095135.45897-1-zhanghongru@xiaom
 i.com/\n\n**To discuss:** We have frame-drop causation data showing mmap_l
 ock contention still impacts Android UI performance on 6.18. How effective
  are current mitigations in practice? What remains to be addressed?\n\n###
  3. ZRAM Compression\n\nWe observed priority inversion in the ZRAM swap-in
  path with durations exceeding 20ms. Threads such as RenderThread\, Blueto
 oth\, and SystemUI are observed to be blocked on this lock.\n\nRFC PATCH: 
 https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260805005545.66112-1-baohua@kernel.org/\n\n*
 *To discuss:** Possible approaches to avoid priority inversion in the ZRAM
  swap-in path\n\n### 4. Binder IPC Allocator\n\nWe observed lock contentio
 n in the Binder buffer allocation path with durations exceeding 8ms. Threa
 ds such as RenderThread\, SystemUI\, and app main threads are observed to 
 be blocked on this lock.\n\nRFC PATCH: https://lore.kernel.org/all/2026080
 5152752.1924434-1-zhangbo56@xiaomi.com/\n\n**To discuss:** Is this a known
  issue? What approaches can reduce lock contention in the Binder allocatio
 n path?\n\n### 5. cgroup threadgroup rwsem\n\nWe observed lock contention 
 in the process fork and exit paths (cgroup_threadgroup_rwsem) with duratio
 ns exceeding 11ms. The RenderThread is observed to be blocked on this lock
 .\n\n**To discuss:** Is this a known issue? What approaches can reduce loc
 k contention on cgroup_threadgroup_rwsem?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2565/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2576@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T102000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
SUMMARY:Unified collection of kernel bug reports
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · John Harrison 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2576/\n\n**Debugging end user
  kernel issues is a problem**\n\nReporting bugs is hard - important inform
 ation is scattered across many log files\, crash files\, sysfs entries\, e
 tc. Most end users don't know where or how to file a bug. They might not e
 ven know that a crash has happened (just a glitch on the screen or a log e
 ntry somewhere).\n\nWhen a bug is reported\, triage can be difficult. Like
 ly only one report but is it really a one-off issue\, never to be seen aga
 in? Was all the important info included? Were the logs collected but two h
 ours after the bug occurred and have wrapped? ...\n\nBugs can cascade - a 
 non-fatal issue can sometimes lead to a fatal issue later in time. A timel
 ine of reports from a single system/boot is needed to identify cause/effec
 t relationships. Bugs can also cross subsystem boundaries and a bug logged
  against driver X might really have been caused by module Y but team Y mig
 ht never get to know about it because team X didn't pass it on properly (o
 r at all).\n\n**Solution: Remove the user**\n\nAutomatically generate a du
 mp file - a daemon can ensure it captures all important information (e.g. 
 devcoredump and other sysfs entries plus dmesg\, syslog\, etc. plus timeli
 ne info such as boot time\, hashed processor id\, etc.) and immediately at
  the time of the bug occurrance. Daemon then sends the bug report a server
 .\n\nFirst level server can be local\, but ultimately bugs are sent to a g
 lobal server. Server does first step triage - build timeline of bug report
 s with the same session id\, check for known instability issues earlier in
  the timeline\, match new bug to existing entries\, etc. It would have plu
 gins to help analyse driver specific data blocks (e.g. devcoredump files) 
 for customised triage. Sends notification to driver/module owner when a ne
 w bug is identified and provides database for investigating bugs - develop
 ers can see how many times a given bug has really occurred\, what platform
 s/hardware/kernels are affected\, etc.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2576/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2438@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
SUMMARY:Support large-size arguments and Rust based exception handling
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Yonghong Song · https://lpc.events/event/20/cont
 ributions/2438/\n\nLarge-size Arguments\n--------------------\n\nCurrent b
 pf verifier will reject a function if one of its arguments\nis a union/str
 uct or more than one register size (except __int128 type).\nSuch limitatio
 n forces users to work around codes to fit bpf prog\nrequirement\, but suc
 h limitation does not exists for other languages\,\nlike normal C\, rust\,
  etc. Without such limitation\, users will be able\nto write more elegant 
 codes.\n\nRust Based Exception Handling\n-----------------------------\n\n
 Current C based bpf prog do not have language level exception\nhandling. T
 he bpf echosystem added bpf_throw() kfunc to allow\nexception which does a
 rchitecture unwinding and allows some\ncode at main prog level.\n\nRust bp
 f supports more flexible exception handling at language\nlevel. It allows 
 bpf callbacks at individual function exception\nlevel\, e.g. to release ce
 rtain references etc. At each function\nlevel\, it is possible there are m
 ultiple possible exception\nhandling\, e.g.\, A -> B -> C. If C tiggered e
 xception\, bpf\ncallbacks can be called for C\, then B\, then A.\n\nIn any
  case\, Rust can provide lots of flexibility when something\nwrong during 
 verification.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2438/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2391@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
SUMMARY:Compiler-Based Context Analysis for Compile-Time Lock Safety
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Marco Elver · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2391/\n\nCompiler-Based Context Analysis is a language exten
 sion which enables statically checking that required contexts are active (
 or inactive) by acquiring and releasing user-definable “context locks”
 . An obvious application of this feature is lock-safety checking for the k
 ernel's various synchronization primitives\, verifying at compile time tha
 t locking rules are not violated. This session will begin with a brief ove
 rview of this new infrastructure that relies on Clang's Thread Safety Anal
 ysis (-Wthread-safety)\, but the focus will be on how developers can use i
 t to annotate locking requirements to catch concurrency bugs before they e
 ver make it into a kernel binary.\n\nCurrently\, the analysis is opt-in by
  default and requires declaring which modules and subsystems should be ana
 lyzed\, as enabling it tree-wide currently results in numerous false posit
 ive warnings. In the remainder of the talk\, we will discuss strategies an
 d best practices on broadening Context Analysis coverage across the entire
  kernel tree.\n\nReference: https://docs.kernel.org/dev-tools/context-anal
 ysis.html
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2391/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2561@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
SUMMARY:RT tasks in sched-ext support
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Aiqun Yu\, Tengfei Fan · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2561/\n\nThe upstream Linux kernel explicitly excludes 
 real-time (RT) tasks from the sched-ext extensible scheduler framework\, r
 estricting sched-ext BPF schedulers to only manage SCHED_NORMAL/SCHED_BATC
 H/SCHED_IDLE tasks. However\, Android's production workloads present a fun
 damentally different reality: many performance-critical scenarios — incl
 uding audio pipelines\, camera capture\, display composition\, and game re
 ndering — depend on tight co-scheduling of RT and non-RT tasks to meet l
 atency and throughput targets simultaneously. Optimizing only the CFS port
 ion of the scheduler in isolation leaves significant headroom on the table
 .\n\nA concrete example is pipeline scheduling identifying related task ch
 ains and placing critical tasks onto specific pipeline CPUs to improve loc
 ality and predictability. The problem is that RT tasks remain outside sche
 d-ext control — even when sched-ext builds and protects such a pipeline\
 , an uncoordinated RT task can land on a pipeline CPU\, break the intended
  task-to-core layout\, and degrade performance. The same blind spot appear
 s in load tracking\, where per-CPU utilization and frequency selection mus
 t account for RT load that a BPF scheduler managing only fair-class\ntasks
  cannot see.\n\nThis talk explores the gap between upstream's conservative
  stance and Android's practical needs — including why upstream currently
  draws this line — surveys the combined RT+sched-ext scheduling scenario
 s that matter most on Android\, and discusses what vendor hook infrastruct
 ure is currently required from the Android Common Kernel (ACK) to bridge t
 his gap safely and\nmaintainably in the short term. Beyond that immediate 
 step\, the session aims to align customers\, Google\, and Qualcomm\, and t
 o open a discussion with the upstream community on a possible long-term di
 rection in mainline.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2561/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2468@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110500
SUMMARY:Unordered I/O (UIO) support and P2P paths
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Arun George · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2468/\n\nThe PCIe Unordered I/O (UIO) f
 eature (introduced in v6.1) relaxes the strict ordering rules of the PCIe 
 fabric\, providing benefits such as the avoidance of head-of-line (HOL) bl
 ocking. CXL v3.2 specification incorporates P2P UIO access into the HDM sp
 ace\, enabling the peer access from non-CXL capable accelerators (e.g.\, G
 PUs) over the PCIe bus.\n\nEnabling UIO in the Linux kernel involves certa
 in considerations:\n - Coherency management while allowing CXL HDM space f
 or both normal and P2P accesses (Ex: coupling with back-invalidation).\n -
  Managing a UIO capable P2P route between a provider (e.g.\, CXL HDM) and 
 requester (e.g\, PCIe GPU).\n\nThis session will discuss:\n - Enumeration 
 of UIO within the PCI and CXL layers.\n - CXL-side aspects of a P2P UIO ca
 pable memory region (including HDM-DB).\n - PCI driver design for UIO and 
 associated P2P-DMA considerations.\n - Potential race conditions during en
 umeration and the mapping P2P routes.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2468/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2550@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T103500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T105500
SUMMARY:What Bugs Matter? Continuous Linux Kernel Bug Monitoring
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Yuan Tan · htt
 ps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2550/\n\nLinux kernel bug reports v
 ary widely in quality: some are duplicates\nor false positives\, while oth
 ers describe exploitable vulnerabilities\nor issues with little practical 
 impact. Maintainers still need to\ndetermine what is real\, what matters\,
  and what deserves attention\nfirst.\n\nAt the same time\, bug findings ar
 e increasingly fragmented across\ndifferent tools and workflows\, includin
 g fuzzers\, static analyzers\,\nhuman analysis\, AI-assisted tools\, and p
 atch-review systems. For\nexample\, Sashiko may uncover pre-existing bugs 
 while reviewing\npatches\, but those findings can remain scattered across 
 individual\nreview reports rather than being collected and tracked in one 
 place.\n\nWe are developing a continuous Linux kernel bug monitoring and t
 riage\nplatform. The platform aggregates findings from multiple sources\, 
 maps\nthem to kernel subsystems\, and gives maintainers a view of currentl
 y\nobserved bugs affecting the code they maintain. For each finding\, it\n
 attempts to generate and execute a reproducer on a KASAN-enabled\nkernel t
 o help validate the report. It also assesses potential impact.\nFor exampl
 e\, whether a bug may enable privilege escalation or has\nlimited practica
 l impact. Confirmed bugs can also include AI-generated\nanalysis and draft
  patches to help reduce the effort required to\nunderstand and fix the iss
 ue.\n\nBuilding on the [mailing-list discussion][1]\, we would like to exp
 lore\nwhat actionable kernel bug observability should look like. How shoul
 d\nheterogeneous bug signals be validated\, prioritized\, and presented to
 \nsubsystem maintainers? How can automated analysis help distinguish\nimpo
 rtant bugs from noise without creating yet another stream of\nlow-quality 
 reports?\n\n  [1]: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260830115546.3942129-1-yu
 antan098@gmail.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2550/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2344@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:The slab allocator sheaves post-mortem
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Vlastimil Babka · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2344/\n\nSheaves are a new percpu caching layer for th
 e Linux kernel's slab allocator (specifically\, its only remaining impleme
 ntation\, SLUB). To some extent it's a return to the former SLAB implement
 ation's percpu arrays (callled magazines in the original Bonwick's paper)\
 , but avoiding the pitfalls that the SLAB implementation had\, thus attemp
 ting to get the best of both SLAB and SLUB approaches.\n\nIn 6.18 sheaves 
 were merged and enabled for maple node and VMA caches. Later  in 7.0 they 
 were enabled for all caches and the original cpu slabs and cpu partial sla
 bs caching layer was removed. This talk will discuss the new implementatio
 n\, explain the tradeoffs involved\, the challenges and performance regres
 sion reports encountered on the way. We'll also look at the lessons learne
 d\, and ongoing/future work that the sheaves caching has enabled.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2344/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2492@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:Common pitfalls and errors\, when using Linux in Safety Application
 s
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Igor Stoppa · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2492/\n\nSafety-oriented use of the Linux Kernel p
 resents a class of obstacles that are normally absent from other types of 
 use\, and therefore can be easily overlooked. Sometimes what would normall
 y constitute a strength can actually turn into a weakness. Or aspects that
  are peculiar of creation of physical products (e.g. cars\, robots) can pr
 esent unexpected challenges.\nThis talk wants to raise awareness about the
 se uncommon aspects.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2492/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2640@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:Attested TLS for Confidential AI Agents: Insights and Lessons Learn
 t from High- and Critical-severity CVEs on Early Attestation
LOCATION:Club D\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Muhammad Usama Sardar · https://lp
 c.events/event/20/contributions/2640/\n\nWe aim to bootstrap some discussi
 ons on the intersections of the following topics:\n\n - Agentic AI\n - Con
 fidential Computing (MC on this already exists)\n - Attestation\n - Attest
 ed TLS\n\nTo initiate discussion\, we will present a few slides to introdu
 ce the above\, then pose some open questions. We very much welcome present
 ations by attendees on related topics. If you are interested in presenting
 \, please email me with the title and expected time.\n\nWe will share insi
 ghts from the several CVEs and GitHub Security Advisories (GHSAs) on Early
  Attestation. In particular\, we will share our discovered critical-severi
 ty CVEs\, such as [CVE-2026-92701][1]\, [CVE-2026-92702][2]\, [CVE-2026-10
 0835][3]\, each of CVSS 9.1\, and other [critical- and high-severity secur
 ity concerns][4] against early attestation\, and discuss with the communit
 y whether early attestation is really necessary.\n\n  [1]: https://www.cve
 .org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-92701\n  [2]: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=
 CVE-2026-92702\n  [3]: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-100835\n 
  [4]: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-intra-handshake-fail/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2640/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2557@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T104500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
SUMMARY:BPF FD Loader: Integrity-Verified BPF on Android
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Siddharth Nayyar · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2557/\n\nOn modern Android devices\, eBPF loading is strictly
  confined to early boot by a privileged loader alongside SELinux lockdown 
 of BPF_PROG_LOAD. While this protects the kernel attack surface\, it leave
 s the platform fundamentally rigid: platform services and hardware partner
 s cannot deploy or activate BPF programs on demand. Furthermore\, the appr
 oach of individual cryptographic signature verification fails in a decentr
 alised client ecosystem comprising several SoC vendors\, OEMs\, and partne
 rs\, where managing runtime key-rings in the Generic Kernel Image (GKI) im
 poses an unsustainable operational burden.\n\nTo address this\, we introdu
 ce the BPF FD Loader driver in Android. Inspired by finit_module()\, it al
 lows the kernel to load BPF programs directly from verified file descripto
 rs rather than untrusted userspace memory.\n\nBecause BPF lacks an in-kern
 el linker\, our solution leverages upstream BPF Light Skeletons. At runtim
 e\, an authorised userspace daemon simply passes an open file descriptor o
 f this ELF to the Android driver via ioctl(). The kernel driver reads the 
 backing file directly before executing the loader\, verifying that it orig
 inates from an authenticated\, read-only partition sealed by Android Verif
 ied Boot (AVB) and dm-verity.\n\nIn this talk\, we present the end-to-end 
 architecture and discuss our roadmap towards standardising file-based BPF 
 loading in the Android ecosystem.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2557/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2547@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T105500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T111500
SUMMARY:Detecting Unhealthy Kernels at Scale
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Sam Crossley ·
  https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2547/\n\nThis talk explores Met
 a's approaches to identifying production issues rooted in the Linux kernel
 . Our investigation process\, spanning detection\, correlation\, and deep 
 triage\, relies on a unified framework of telemetry and monitoring tools. 
 A common challenge arising in hyperscale kernel releases is that of robust
 ly comparing kernel performance across lifecycle phases\, despite vast var
 iations in deployment size. Our findings highlight the efficacy of combini
 ng flexible data aggregation with statistically grounded analysis\, to str
 eamline and automate the release process.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2547/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2408@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:Extending the BPF Type Format to support more languages
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Alan Maguire · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2408/\n\nNow that kernel modules can be built in Rust [1]\,[2]\, 
 it seems timely to look at programming language constructs outside of C to
  assess their applicability to BTF representation. Suggestions include\n\n
 - slices/"fat pointers" with type and size\n- tagged unions\n- generics\n-
  interfaces\n- lifetime tags\n\nRough suggestions will be made for how to 
 represent these as BTF kinds to trigger discussion.\n\n[1] https://docs.ke
 rnel.org/rust/index.html\n[2] https://lwn.net/Articles/991719/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2408/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2394@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:BPF support in the GNU Toolchain
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Cupertino Miranda\, David Faust\, Jose E. M
 archesi\, Vineet Gupta · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2394/\
 n\nIn this activity we will first provide a very brief update on the statu
 s of the port of GNU binutils and GCC to the BPF target\, with emphasis on
  the level of support for extant BPF programs and the kernel BPF selftests
 . Then we will address a set of particular issues for which we need feedba
 ck and/or consensus from the BPF kernel and clang/LLVM hackers.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2394/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2564@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T111500
SUMMARY:CPU Power Regression Testing with Wattson
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · William McVicker · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2564/\n\nWattson is a trace based power estimation tool desig
 ned around perfetto to estimate CPU power consumption on ARM64 devices usi
 ng a statistical per-SoC model. With support of both the Pixel 6 (gs101) a
 nd SM8750 upstream\, you can now use the Wattson tool to detect CPU power 
 regressions in the Linux kernel. This talk dives into how Google is using 
 Wattson to catch CPU power regressions including:\n - What is needed to en
 able testing.\n - Walk-through of the metrics collected and how to use Wat
 tson.\n - Examples of regressions Wattson has caught.\n - Expanding into o
 ther subsystems like GPU and TPU/NPU power estimates.\n\nUltimately\, we
 ’d like to connect with the Linux community to see who wants to collabor
 ate on establishing CPU power regression testing upstream.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2564/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2521@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T110500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T112500
SUMMARY:CXL Performance Monitoring - Status and Outlook
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Robert Richter · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2521/\n\nThe CXL specification defin
 es a Component Performance Monitoring Unit (CPMU) register interface for p
 erformance monitoring of CXL devices. The Linux kernel includes a CPMU dri
 ver that exposes an interface to collect hardware events from CXL memory d
 evices through the perf subsystem.\n\nThe current driver supports poll-bas
 ed event counting using perf stat \, providing events such as clock ticks\
 , DDR CAS read/write counts\, and CXL M2S/S2M protocol events. However\, i
 nterrupt-based sampling is not implemented — the CPMU capability registe
 r defines interrupt support in the CXL spec\, but the kernel driver does n
 ot utilize it. As a result\, perf top and other sampling-based tools are n
 ot available.\n\nThis talk gives an overview of the current CPMU driver im
 plementation\, its usage with the perf tool\, and the limitations encounte
 red. We also look at how CPMUs relate to standard DRAM profiling with unco
 re PMUs and EDAC. We discuss adding interrupt support and extending CPMU d
 iscovery beyond CXL endpoint devices to root ports and switch ports.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2521/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2559@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T111500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:Modernizing Android USB: Userspace AOA\, Multi-UDC\, Type-C\, and F
 ramework APIs
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · George Chan\, Neill Kapron · https://lpc.events/
 event/20/contributions/2559/\n\nThe Android USB stack was originally archi
 tected around the constraints of early smartphones. Initially designed pri
 marily for phones with a single USB Micro-B device port\, the stack lacks 
 the modern API surface required for advanced USB applications. While incre
 mental changes have been implemented out of necessity\, a larger refactori
 ng is required to better support modern USB features like Type-C and multi
 ple-UDC topologies.\n\nThis presentation will highlight some of the limita
 tions of the existing frameworks\, provide an update on current projects\,
  and solicit feedback on the new architecture plans and API surfaces.\n\nT
 opics for Discussion:\n\n - Userspace AOA via function_fs: Deprecating the
  out-of-tree kernel AOA driver and moving protocol handling entirely to us
 erspace.\n - Upstream Type-C Integration: Hooking the framework directly i
 nto the Type-C connector class\, allowing applications to query cable capa
 bilities and provide safe role-swap hints to the TCPC.\n - Multi-UDC & Per
 -Port Configuration: Refactoring the framework to break the single-UDC ass
 umption\, allowing for independent per-port configurations on laptop-class
  devices.\n - Modular Function Provider Patterns: Replacing legacy broadca
 st mechanisms with strict provider patterns and robust APIs with proper er
 ror handling.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2559/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2548@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T111500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T113000
SUMMARY:We don't know what we're looking for until we find what we're looki
 ng for
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · David Dai\, Sha
 keel Butt · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2548/\n\nWhat start
 ed out as an investigation for potential isolation issues in the fleet led
  us down into a few different rabbit holes. We started with trying to find
  patterns where the kernel lock holders are unable to run\, causing other 
 tasks to wait on said locks. We'll discuss ways to monitor said patterns a
 cross the fleet at a broader scale and then zoom into some interesting cas
 e studies to see what we found including:\n\n- OOM killer prints to the co
 nsole while holding a lock\n- Act of monitoring causes isolation issues in
  the fleet for sensitive services\n- Hard partitioning from CPUSETS causes
  lock holder unable to get CPU time\n- Chain of lock waiters -> Lock holde
 r sleeping on synchronize_rcu -> CPU holding up grace period\n\nAlso we'll
  discuss how to expand on monitoring for scheduling issues and/or issues i
 n the fleet that causes stalls that prevent system wide progress
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2548/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2436@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
SUMMARY:Slaying the BPF Verifier: Safe & Static Kernel Extensions Using Rus
 t
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Aniket Gattani · https://lpc.events/event/20/con
 tributions/2436/\n\nWhile the BPF verifier ensures kernel safety\, its rig
 id static rules heavily bottleneck the development of complex kernel exten
 sions. Last year’s LPC introduced [Rex][1] (Rust Extensions) to address 
 these gaps. By leveraging Rust\, Rex aims to guarantee memory and type saf
 ety at compile time rather than relying on the restrictive verifier. While
  this initial iteration utilized runtime protection to manage panics and a
 ddress the halting problem\, it resulted in **considerable** architectural
  and performance trade-offs.\n\nThis talk introduces the revamped\, static
 ally compiled **panic-free** Rex. By fully leaning into Rust’s static ty
 ping\, borrow checker\, and a dedicated compiler pass\, this new architect
 ure closely mirrors the verifier’s safety profile but at compile time. I
 t empowers developers to build large-scale kernel programs using dynamic l
 oops\, complex data structures\, and natural programming patterns.\n\nTo p
 rove this model's viability\, we will present a successful Proof of Concep
 t integrating the new Rex with the [ghOSt][2] scheduler. We will walk thro
 ugh the implementation details\, demonstrating how Rex handles complex [sc
 heduling logic][3] safely and efficiently entirely in Rust.\n\nUltimately\
 , we seek to engage the community in a dialogue regarding the robustness o
 f this architectural framework and the strategic roadmap for upstream inte
 gration. We are eager to assess the broader interest in the evolved Rex an
 d solicit feedback on potential refinements to further optimize our model.
 \n\n  [1]: https://lpc.events/event/19/contributions/2190/\n  [2]: https:/
 /research.google/pubs/ghost-fast-and-flexible-user-space-delegation-of-lin
 ux-scheduling/\n  [3]: https://lpc.events/event/19/contributions/2034/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2436/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2399@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
SUMMARY:Are your types the same as mine?
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Kris Van Hees · https://lpc.events/event/2
 0/contributions/2399/\n\nThis session is aimed at discussing the benefits 
 (and requirements) of having a unified type representation that can be use
 d in tracing tools to facilitate type management\, type compatibility chec
 ks\, etc.  With type info from the kernel\, type info from userspace\, and
  types that may be defined in tracing scripts\, the hoops that one may nee
 d to jump through to make it all work as a single environment where cross-
 stack tracing can be done are a bit excessive.\n\nLet's discuss ongoing ef
 forts to work on this\, and look at the needs\, wants\, etc to help drive 
 this in the right direction where a single type management system can sati
 sfy all requirements\, and avoid duplication of effort\, and painful conve
 rsions between systems.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2399/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2351@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124500
SUMMARY:Modern Developments with NFS in Linux
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Jeffrey Layton · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2351/\n\nThe Linux Kernel's NFS server and client has b
 een undergoing a lot of changes recently. This talk will cover some of the
  latest developments in the Linux NFS Client and Server in the last few ye
 ars. Including:\n\n- Dynamic threading\n- New iomodes (buffered\, direct a
 nd dontcache)\n- Directory delegations\n- POSIX ACLs for NFSv4\n- Signed f
 ilehandles\n- Delegated timestamps\n- Multigrain timestamps\n- Netlink upc
 alls
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2351/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2425@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122500
SUMMARY:Using Platform-provided Hints for Hot Page Detection and Promotion
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Bharata Bhasker Rao ·
  https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2425/\n\nHardware platforms con
 tinue to expose useful and actionable memory access information to the OS 
 in various ways. Sources of such information include CPU-level instruction
 /op sampling mechanisms (like AMD IBS and ARM SPE)\, PMU-based precise sam
 pling (like Intel PEBS)\, and device-side facilities such as the CXL Hotne
 ss Monitoring Unit (HMU). These platform-provided hints can be used by sub
 -systems like NUMA balancing\, reclaim and hot page promotion in tiered me
 mory systems to make better placement decisions\, especially on systems wi
 th CXL-attached or accelerator memory where the cost of a bad placement is
  high.\n\nThe IBS Memory Profiler [1] on future AMD processors is one conc
 rete example of such a hint source. It is a second\, lightweight IBS insta
 nce on the chip\, dedicated to memory access profiling\, and provides per-
 access information (virtual/physical address\,  source/target NUMA node) a
 t low overhead. This talk intends to share the experience of working with 
 the IBS Memory Profiler and to look at the ways and challenges in making s
 uch a hardware source usable by the kernel in general.\n\nFor any such pla
 tform-provided hint to be useful\, a sub-system that can collate and act o
 n hotness information from multiple sources in a producer-agnostic manner 
 is needed. The pghot subsystem [2] is being developed for this purpose. It
  moves the hot page promotion engine out of NUMA balancing into a sub-syst
 em of its own and exposes a uniform interface so that multiple producers (
 NUMA hint faults\, AMD IBS\, CXL HMU\, etc.) can feed into a single promot
 ion policy. pghot has been discussed at LPC 2023 [3]\, LSF/MM/BPF 2025 [4]
  and LPC 2025\, and is a recurring topic on the biweekly Linux Memory Hotn
 ess and Promotion community call. In this talk\, pghot is treated as the e
 nabling base infrastructure and not as the main subject.\n\nThe talk will 
 also touch upon the producer-side ABI that pghot should commit to\, where 
 filtering and aging of the access information should reside\, the coexiste
 nce with NUMA balancing-driven sampling on capable hardware\, and a set of
  workloads and metrics for fairly comparing different hotness sources.\n\n
 [1] AMD IBS Memory Profiler documentation: https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/
 69205_1.00_AMD64_IBS_PUB\n[2] pghot v7 patchset: https://lore.kernel.org/l
 inux-mm/20260504060924.344313-1-bharata@amd.com/\n[3] Using hardware hints
  for optimal page placement\, LPC 2023: https://lpc.events/event/17/contri
 butions/1513/\n[4] Unifying sources of page hotness information\, LSF/MM/B
 PF 2025: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/20250319124552.0000344a@huawei.c
 om/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2425/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2541@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122500
SUMMARY:Scaling the Design Definition
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Chuck Wolber · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2541/\n\nThe fundamental unit of design is a requ
 irement (i.e. "testable expectation"). All forms of design can be expresse
 d as a (directed acyclic) knowledge graph of requirements. A software proj
 ect that documents its design in this manner can derive both source code a
 nd automated test from the same idea. Without this\, there is no guarantee
  that test and implementation reliably reflects the same idea.\n\nIn virtu
 ally all open-source projects\, documenting the design to this level of de
 tail is rarely (if ever) considered. While the benefits are easy to unders
 tand\, retrofitting a design on an existing project and then keeping it up
  to date is a barrier to entry that few are willing to consider.\n\nIn thi
 s troubleshooting session\, Chuck will describe the problem in more detail
 \, answer relevant background questions\, and solicit ideas for potential 
 approaches to retrofitting designs and keeping them updated.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2541/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2641@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124500
SUMMARY:Live Update BoF
LOCATION:Club D\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Pratyush Yadav · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2641/\n\nThe primary topic of discussion will be 
 LUO/KHO ABI and what type of backwards and forwards compatibility should b
 e guarantee for the ABI. There are different opinions from various parts o
 f the community and this would be a chance to bring out all views and come
  to an agreement. More context can be found at these mailing list threads:
  [[0]](https://lore.kernel.org/kexec/2vxzik5f311e.fsf@kernel.org/#r) [[1]]
 (https://lore.kernel.org/kexec/20260904160009.GV4157646@nvidia.com/#r).\n\
 nOther list of potential topics\, time permitting:\n\n- TMPFS preservation
  [[2]](https://lore.kernel.org/kexec/20260923224408.3745689-1-pratyush@ker
 nel.org/T/#u) [[3]](https://lore.kernel.org/kexec/20260901180713.4185641-1
 -dmatlack@google.com/T/#u).\n- Getting rid of scratch/bootmem areas.\n- KH
 O enablement on different architectures like Loongarch or PowerPC.\n- Maki
 ng KHO work with crash reservations and KASAN.\n\nThis list is open to sug
 gestions.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2641/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2560@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T121500
SUMMARY:Space sharing between super and data partitions on Android system
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Yangtao Li · https://lpc.events/event/20/contrib
 utions/2560/\n\nThe Android Super partition relies on static reserved spac
 e to guarantee OTA upgrade capability. However\, this fixed allocation met
 hod possesses an inherent defect: if the reserved space is too large\, it 
 permanently occupies flash memory and cannot be utilized by the data parti
 tion\; if it is too small\, OTA upgrades will fail due to insufficient spa
 ce. To address the industry pain points of static partition isolation and 
 low flash utilization\, we propose a space-sharing mechanism between the s
 uper and data partitions. Distinct from traditional reserved space schemes
 \, this technology enables space sharing between the two partitions. The c
 ore idea is that in daily usage scenarios\, idle physical blocks within th
 e Super partition are shared with the data partition to expand the storage
  space available to users\; when the super partition requires expansion fo
 r OTA upgrades or system maintenance\, it can dynamically requisition idle
  physical blocks from the data partition without causing any data corrupti
 on throughout the process.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2560/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2549@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122000
SUMMARY:Following the bread crumbs: Tracing packets through the kernel with
  per-skb BPF metadata
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Jakub Sitnicki 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2549/\n\nOperating a network 
 at scale\, we routinely need to answer a deceptively simple question: what
  path did *this* packet take through the kernel\, and where did it get dro
 pped? On our edge\, a single packet can cross several network namespaces\,
  get encapsulated in GRE or IPIP\, be encrypted with IPsec\, and pass thro
 ugh multiple nftables chains before it leaves the box. Tagging the payload
  the way we tag L7 requests is not an option here: it's opaque\, encrypted
 \, and rewritten along the way.\n\nWe'll walk through our attempt to build
  an `mtr`-like tool that\, instead of a list of hosts\, shows the *kernel 
 waypoints* a packet hits — interfaces\, netns boundaries\, tunnel encap/
 decap\, IPsec encrypt/decrypt\, nftables verdicts\, and drops — each wit
 h a timestamp.\n\nWe started with [Retis](https://github.com/retis-org/ret
 is)\, Red Hat's eBPF-based packet tracer\, which already does most of the 
 heavy lifting. We'll cover what worked out of the box and where we hit its
  limits:\n\n- It correlates events by hashing the skb data area (`skb->hea
 d`)\; we found\n  tracking by `skb` address to be both cheaper (~15% fewer
  trigger events on a busy box) and a better fit.\n- The "tcpdump model" mi
 smatch: Retis filters at every probe\, capturing many\n  flows. For a trac
 eroute-like UX we instead want to *lock on* to a packet once\,\n  then hav
 e downstream probes fire purely on identity.\n\nThis is where the observab
 ility problem becomes a kernel interface problem. To follow a packet acros
 s transformations\, and to let BPF programs annotate a packet at one hook 
 and read it back at another\, we need per-packet state that lives and dies
  with the `sk_buff`. The BPF-map "side-stash" works today but is costly: t
 o avoid leaking entries it has to hook the `consume_skb` release path\, wh
 ich fires for every one of the hundreds of thousands of packets passing th
 rough the system each second\, even when we only care about a handful of t
 hem. So we started an upstream effort to add a better building block: **a 
 BPF metadata buffer embedded in an skb extension chunk**.\n\nWe'll update 
 the audience on where that work has moved since we [last presented](https:
 //netdevconf.info/0x1A/sessions/talk/thrice-the-charm-an-skb-extension-for
 -bpf-metadata.html) it at Netdev 0x1A in July\, and on the challenges we f
 aced wiring Retis up to use it.\n\nWe'd like to hear whether others have f
 elt the same gap in production\, and if not\, how they observe a packet's 
 journey through their systems today.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2549/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2562@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T121500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
SUMMARY:Android GBL + Dynamic Partitions 2.0
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Daniel Zheng · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2562/\n\nHi I'm here to discuss the developments in android GBL +
  CF support\, and talk about android dynamic partitions 2.0 feature (resiz
 able super partition) to accommodate large OS updates.\n\n- Quick GBL + Cu
 ttlefish recap\n- Cuttlefish default booting off GBL\n- New android_esp pa
 rtition\n- Fastbootd deprecation\n- u-boot implementation\n\nAndroid Dynam
 ic Partitions 2.0 allow for dynamic resizing of super partition vs. userda
 ta during OTA to support larger os updates. We will cover the different so
 lutions proposed and in plans of getting adopted.\n\n- Samsung F2FS patche
 s to support dynamic data partition\n- bootloader support\n- liblp support
  in GBL
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2562/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2386@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124000
SUMMARY:Crash Dumps at a Bare-Metal Cloud Provider - Capture\, Dedup\, and 
 AI-Assisted Analysis.
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Serapheim Dimit
 ropoulos · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2386/\n\nCoreWeave i
 s a bare-metal cloud provider. A typical machine in our fleet has at least
  2TB of RAM\, and customers tend to run close to that limit. That has two 
 immediate consequences: the crash-kernel memory reservation matters a lot 
 (every GB we reserve is a GB the customer doesn't get)\, and when a machin
 e panics the resulting crash dump is huge. Because it's so big\, the downt
 ime during a crash\, and how soon we can hand the machine back to the cust
 omer\, ends up gated almost entirely on makedumpfile. At the fleet level i
 t gets even more challenging as bugs triggered by a bad kernel upgrade or 
 some fancy customer workload lead to getting tons of crash dumps at once a
 nd all of them are due to the same bug. Finally there’s us\, a lean team
  of 2 kernel engineers at CoreWeave that have to look at all the incoming 
 crash dumps. We lean on AI a lot\, at least for triaging failures and knoc
 king out the easy RCAs. That field has progressed and gotten more accurate
  over time but we still get the occasional confident hallucination.\n\nOur
  ideas and projects so far:\n\n- (Project in-progress at the time of submi
 ssion) Fingerprint the failure before we even try to capture a dump. Compu
 te a cheap signature of the crash from within the crash kernel\, compare i
 t against what we've already seen\, and if it's a known one\, skip collect
 ion altogether. (This probably needs a network connection out of the crash
  kernel to check against a central store\, and we're not sure yet how pain
 ful that is to pull off)\n\n- (Idea) Decouple capturing the crashed memory
  from turning it into a proper dump. Instead of running makedumpfile to co
 mpletion before we reboot\, grab the crashed memory (maybe with some very 
 light filtering)\, reboot early to give the machine back\, and do the heav
 y transform/filter later. (There may be Kexec HandOver-related discussion 
 here).\n- (Idea) An alternative on the above that we're weighing is to ski
 p the general-purpose dump entirely and use drgn+sdb to take ultra-filtere
 d dumps on the spot\, driven by a preconfigured list of data structures we
  care about.\n\n- (Project in-progress at the time of submission) Use out-
 of-band kernel introspection from the DPU to grab ultra-filtered dumps the
  moment we know a panic happened\, without even waiting for the crash kern
 el to come up (we got drgn+sdb on the DPU reading host memory over RDMA).\
 n\n- (Implemented) For the AI triaging/RCA side\, hand the agent a Docker 
 container preloaded with the crash dump plus matching debug info\, the ker
 nel source for that exact version\, and some hints on where to look for ex
 isting issues (bug trackers\, the mailing list\, etc.). We also drop in a 
 small agents.md whose main job is to keep the agent from spinning in endle
 ss loops.\n\nTopics we'd like to discuss:\n\n- How do folks capture dumps 
 on multi-TB hosts at fleet scale? Full vs. aggressively filtered\, local N
 VMe vs. a network target\, and what capture times are people living with? 
 And how do you settle on the crash-kernel memory reservation?\n\n- Is anyo
 ne else deduplicating crash dumps? If so\, what crash-signature/dedup sche
 mes are you using\, and is there any appetite for converging on a shared\,
  drgn-based triage library instead of everyone rolling their own?\n\n- How
  are people using AI tools for crash dump analysis\, and which setups have
  worked best? How do you keep the confident hallucinations in check?\n\n- 
 Is anyone else doing out-of-band introspection of production hosts (DPU\, 
 BMC\, PCIe P2P\, RDMA)? How do you handle it\, and do you use drgn?\n\n- H
 ow do you keep historical crash dump data around? Jira and/or database plu
 s Grafana? Something else?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2386/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2518@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T122500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T125000
SUMMARY:DAMON (Data Attributes Monitoring/Operations Engine)-based {C\,G\,X
 }PU [un]attached NUMA Pages Migration
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · SJ Park · https://lpc
 .events/event/20/contributions/2518/\n\nIn the last LPC\, we introduced a 
 plan to extend DAMON (Data Access MONitor) for migrating pages around NUMA
  nodes based on their access pattern.  Based on on/offline feedback\, we c
 ontinued discussions and development in the upstream community.\n\nAs a re
 sult of the collaborations\, we made a concrete plan and a roadmap for the
  goal\, including support of extensions for h/w features such as AMD IBS\,
  Intel PEBS and ARM SPE. Major stakeholders agreed on the roadmap that cur
 rently planned to deliver the first working version by mid 2027. By the ti
 me\, it will no longer be called DAMON (Data Access MONitor) but DAMON (Da
 ta Attributes Monitoring/Operation eNgine).\n\nIn this session\, we will i
 ntroduce the evolved design of the in-kernel framework and user interface.
  We will share the entire roadmap with the expected timeline and the statu
 s as of the time of the session. We will discuss pros and cons of the desi
 gns and possible adjustments on roadmap\, timeline\, and priorities of sho
 rt term future works.\n\nQuestions we will discuss include but not limited
  to below:\n\n- What is the plan and the current status of DAMON for NUMA 
 system memory optimizations?\n  - Does the plan makes sense?\n  - Any of p
 lanned work items need [de]prioritizations?\n    - Monitoring extension fo
 r AMD IBS\, Intel PEBS\, ARM SPE\, and/or page faults.\n    - DAMOS integr
 ation with new monitoring framework.\n- Can we make it just works without 
 any user inputs?\n- Is there a plan to use it in real products?\n- Will th
 e classical page table accessed bit based access monitoring be deprecated?
   What is the plan and the status?\n- What is the current and future test 
 plan?\n- Myths and truths of DAMON for NUMA and general use cases.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2518/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2432@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
SUMMARY:blitmus: Litmus-testing the eBPF memory model on real hardware
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Puranjay Mohan · https://lpc.events/event/20/con
 tributions/2432/\n\nThe eBPF instruction set has quietly grown a concurren
 cy surface: load_acquire/store_release instructions\, arena atomics now JI
 T-compiled across x86\, arm64\, riscv and powerpc\, resilient queued spinl
 ocks\, and lock-free ring buffers. eBPF programs routinely run concurrentl
 y across CPUs and increasingly coordinate through shared memory. Yet unlik
 e the Linux kernel — which has a formal\, herd7-executable memory model 
 (LKMM) and the klitmus7 tool to exercise it — eBPF has no formal memory 
 model and no way to check that the ordering a program requests actually su
 rvives the verifier and each architecture's JIT.\n\nThis talk presents **b
 litmus** (https://github.com/puranjaymohan/blitmus)\, a tool that converts
  standard LKMM litmus tests into eBPF programs and runs them on real hardw
 are. Each process of a litmus test becomes a BPF program pinned to a speci
 fic CPU\; a userspace harness races them across millions of iterations\, h
 istograms the outcomes\, and compares them against the test's exists claus
 e and expected result — the same methodology klitmus7 and herd7 use\, bu
 t exercising the BPF runtime end to end. Because the same litmus test can 
 be run unchanged on every architecture\, blitmus can surface JIT ordering 
 bugs (e.g. a missing barrier for cmpxchg) that are otherwise silent and ca
 tastrophic for concurrent BPF code.\n\nI will demo the tool converting and
  running the LKMM litmus suite through BPF\, walk through the results on a
 arch64 and x86-64 (including weak behaviors observed for store-buffering a
 nd a Result: Never test that blitmus flags as violated)\, and use that cas
 e to discuss tool fidelity: how faithfully a batched\, test-run-based harn
 ess models true multi-CPU races\, and where the current barrier/interleavi
 ng strategy needs to improve.\n\nI would like to use the session to discus
 s next steps with the community:\n\n(1) growing the harness to cover BPF-s
 pecific primitives — arena spinlocks\, rqspinlock\, ring buffer reserve/
 commit ordering\, per-CPU maps\;\n\n(2) a proper litmus parser and cross-a
 rchitecture CI (qemu on riscv/ppc/s390/loongarch) wired into selftests/bpf
  as a memory-model conformance suite\;
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2432/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2395@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
SUMMARY:Towards real-time ABI compatibility assurance and smarter module ve
 rsioning with libctf
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Nick Alcock · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2395/\n\nThe CTFv4 extension of the CTF file format into a s
 uperset of BTF is nearly complete.  Once it's working\, what can we do wit
 h systemwide type information for all C programs and the kernel?\n\nOne po
 ssibility we explore in this talk is to use some simple linker extensions 
 to provide real-time\, linear-time ABI checking in ld.so to determine for 
 every running program whether any of the C libraries it uses has changed i
 ncompatibly since the program was linked in a way that breaks the program\
 , without linking ld.so with libctf or requiring it to do anything more di
 fficult than a few equality comparisons and strcmps.\n\nThe end goal in us
 erspace is to be able to see something like this\n\n    # emacs\n    warni
 ng: libxml.so.2.13.9: incompatible symbol: linked against\n      xmlDocPtr
  xmlReadMemory (const char *\, int\, const char *\, const char *\, int)\n 
    but running against\n      xmlDocPtr xmlReadMemory (const char *\, int\
 , const char *\, const char *)\n\n(a contrived example: xmlReadMemory has 
 not actually broken ABI. ld.so invokes a helper program that uses libctf t
 o print that output\, but does not itself need to use libctf.)\n\nIt also 
 seems likely to me that the same infrastructure that implements the above 
 can be reused to implement a better modversions without the manifold pain 
 points of the current implementation.\n\nThis is only partly written so fa
 r: we explore the design of this scheme in part so that the audience can s
 pot any serious errors before it causes widespread breakage\, and to see i
 f people can suggest improvements to the general design. (I'm certain that
  modversions will add exciting extra wrinkles to this.)
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2395/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2491@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T125500
SUMMARY:Defining Linux Kernel Requirements and Test Specifications out of t
 he Kernel Tree
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Gabriele Paoloni\, Kate Stewart 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2491/\n\nFollowing the [objec
 tions][1] in having requirements and even code documentation traceable to 
 Kernel testing as optional part of the Linux Kernel development process\, 
 the ELISA project decided to start defining requirements and test specific
 ations in a separate repository.\nThis session will present the current st
 atus for the requirements and test specifications framework and traceabili
 ty to upstream code and test case implementations.\n\n  [1]: https://lore.
 kernel.org/all/2026021207-hatchery-spore-2800@gregkh/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2491/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2558@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124500
SUMMARY:Generic Boot Loader on Android platforms
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Naina Mehta · https://lpc.events/event/20/contri
 butions/2558/\n\nBootloaders play a vital role in the Android boot process
 \, but the ecosystem has long been fragmented across silicon vendors and O
 EM-specific implementations. To address this\, Google introduced the Gener
 ic Boot Loader (GBL)\, a Rust-based EFI application in AOSP\, with the goa
 l of standardizing Android boot logic.\nThis session focuses on how Qualco
 mm is adapting its boot chain to integrate GBL while preserving existing f
 irmware and platform capabilities. It describes the Qualcomm-specific chan
 ges required to expose core UEFI services such as block I/O\, memory alloc
 ation\, RNG\, etc. to GBL\, along with the firmware adjustments needed to 
 present these services in a clean and consistent way. The session also cov
 ers Qualcomm’s implementation of device tree selection and fixups throug
 h GBL-defined interfaces\, as well as the handling of Android-critical boo
 t data including kernel command line and bootconfig handoff.\nIn addition\
 , the talk discusses Qualcomm’s support for GBL-specific UEFI protocols 
 that enable Android requirements such as Verified Boot and Fastboot\, and 
 how these fit into the existing Qualcomm boot flow. Overall\, the session 
 provides a practical look at the platform changes needed to bring GBL into
  the Qualcomm boot chain and move Android boot architecture closer to a mo
 re maintainable\, upstream-aligned model.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2558/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2546@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
SUMMARY:Breakpoints in drgn
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Linux System Monitoring and Observability MC · Omar Sandoval 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2546/\n\nBreakpoint support f
 or drgn is currently underway (https://github.com/osandov/drgn/issues/626)
 . Support is planned for:\n\n* KGDB\n* Linux kernel QEMU guests\n* Live ke
 rnels in production (a sequel to my [2023 LPC session](https://lwn.net/Art
 icles/952942/)).\n* Userspace processes (ptrace)\n\nEach of these targets 
 has its challenges and deficiencies. I will discuss these problems as well
  as next steps.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2546/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2353@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Modernizing Kernel Boot Options: Resolving cmdline and Bootconfig D
 iscrepancies
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Masami Hiramatsu · https://lpc.events/ev
 ent/20/contributions/2353/\n\nThe current Linux kernel command-line subsys
 tem is very simple and easy to define\, but it seems to have several probl
 ems\, such as inconsistent API naming\, drivers and the kernel sharing the
  same command-line options\, and discrepancies between documentation and c
 ommand-line option definitions. Furthermore\, some options require special
  handling and cannot be supported by Bootconfig\, an extension of kernel c
 ommand-line options\, but there is no way to indicate which options these 
 are.\n\nThis session will present ideas for solving these problems\, or di
 scuss whether to continue as is.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2353/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2566@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T124500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
SUMMARY:Actionable Strategies to mitigate memory footprint on 16kb kernels
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Juan Yescas\, Kalesh Singh · https://lpc.events/
 event/20/contributions/2566/\n\nWith the release of Android 17\, partners 
 are required to support the **16 KB developer option** on devices meeting 
 specific hardware requirements\, such as CPU compatibility and memory conf
 igurations exceeding 8 GiB. While this transition significantly improves s
 ystem performance and unlocks hardware efficiencies\, **Android partners**
  frequently observe a noticeable memory footprint increase\, and they ask 
 **"How can we reduce this memory footprint?"**. This regression is primari
 ly driven by internal fragmentation\, mapping more memory than what is nee
 ded and page-granularity alignment constraints across both userspace and k
 ernel allocations.\n\nThis presentation provides a concrete\, engineering-
 focused recipes for Partners to reduce the memory footprint in:\n\n**Users
 pace**\n\n - Fileback mappings\n - Anonymous Memory\n - Shmem memory\n\n**
 Kerne memory**\n\n - Kernel modules\n - CMA memory usage\n - Buffer alloca
 tions in drivers
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2566/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2516@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T125000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131000
SUMMARY:XMFS: A Cross-node Express Memory File System
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Yifan Qiao · https://
 lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2516/\n\n**Cross-node Memory as a Linux 
 Storage Tier：Exploring POSIX-based Shared Memory**\n\n**Background & Mot
 ivation**\nMemory-semantic interconnects such as CXL 3.0 and Huawei United
  Bus make remote memory directly addressable. This raises a question for L
 inux: should cross-node memory become another storage tier that can be exp
 osed through existing POSIX filesystem interfaces?\n\n**Our Exploration: X
 MFS**\nTo explore this question\, we built XMFS\, an in-kernel prototype t
 hat exports cross-node shared memory through a standard POSIX filesystem i
 nterface. Initial experiments with metadata-intensive workloads and contai
 ner image sharing suggest the approach is practical.\n\n**Engineering Chal
 lenges Encountered**\nDuring development we repeatedly encountered four ar
 eas where existing kernel abstractions are either missing or insufficient:
 \n• Metadata synchronization: Current VFS and filesystem metadata manage
 ment assume a single kernel instance coordinating namespace and inode upda
 tes. What generic kernel mechanisms are needed to efficiently synchronize 
 metadata across multiple kernels without introducing centralized bottlenec
 ks?\n• Software cache coherence: The Linux page cache and MM subsystem r
 ely on hardware cache coherence within a machine. When memory is shared ac
 ross nodes without hardware coherence\, should software cache-coherence po
 licies live inside individual filesystems\, the MM subsystem\, or new gene
 ric kernel infrastructure?\n• Integrating hybrid transports: Linux curre
 ntly exposes memory-semantic interconnects and RDMA through different subs
 ystems and programming models. How should these be integrated so that file
 systems can transparently use both without exposing transport-specific beh
 avior to applications?\n• Capacity management & Tiering: Linux already p
 rovides memory tiering\, page migration\, and NUMA-aware memory management
 . Should cross-node shared memory participate in these mechanisms\, and wh
 at interfaces are needed between filesystems and MM to support it?\n\n**To
 pics for Discussion**\nRather than presenting XMFS as a finished filesyste
 m\, we hope to discuss whether Linux should treat cross-node memory as a f
 irst-class storage tier\, which abstractions belong in VFS/MM rather than 
 individual filesystems\, and what a practical upstream path could look lik
 e. We believe these questions will become increasingly relevant as memory-
 semantic interconnects become more widely available.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2516/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2431@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Adding KASAN support for JIT-compiled BPF Programs
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Alexis Lothoré · https://lpc.events/event/20/co
 ntributions/2431/\n\nKASAN (Kernel Address Sanitizer) is a powerful develo
 per tool for detecting\nuse-after-free and out-of-bounds memory accesses i
 n the kernel code.\nHowever\, not all memory accesses performed by the ker
 nel are covered by\nKASAN monitoring. BPF programs are a major example: wh
 en they are\ntranslated by the in-kernel JIT compiler\, the kernel directl
 y emits new\nnative instructions that then escape KASAN instrumentation. C
 losing this\ngap would shed some light on potential bugs in the eBPF verif
 ier or JIT\ncompilers that would be hard to investigate otherwise.\n\nThis
  talk will present the current effort funded by the eBPF Foundation\naimin
 g to introduce KASAN support for eBPF programs: we will discuss the\nmain 
 architectural points\, going over how the JIT compiler emits calls to\n`__
 asan_*()` functions before each memory access\, discussing the register\ns
 ave/restore strategy\, and the interactions with the BPF verifier.\n\nAs a
  first draft of this work has been introduced at the LSFMMBPF\nconference 
 2026 (Zagreb\, Croatia)\, and as many revisions have been sent and\ndiscus
 sed on the BPF mailing list since then\, this talk will also act as an\nup
 date\, highlighting the main changes since the first draft\, as well as th
 e\nremaining difficulties and issues to solve.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2431/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2397@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Adding C library wrappers for all Linux syscalls - The Return
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Carlos O'Donell\, Florian Weimer · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2397/\n\nAt Linux Plumbers Conference 2
 025 in Tokyo I gave part 1 of this talk\, this year I want to revisit prog
 ress made against the use cases presented last year including progress mad
 e on raw futex.\n\nIn review we'll look at more applications and if we we 
 are able to solve their most thorny calls to syscall with actual libc wrap
 pers.\n\nI'll include a more formal review of what is currently missing in
  glibc\, collecting the list of syscalls for x86_64 and comparing to the k
 ernel.\n\nI will close again with a call to action for always having sysca
 lls available as C library calls even if they could be used behind the bac
 k of the implementation.\n\nI will propose a formal mentoring program. If 
 you want to get a syscall wrapper into glibc I'll work with you to make it
  happen.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2397/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2490@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T132500
SUMMARY:From Manual Argument to Machine-Readable Graph: Toward a Safety SBO
 M for Linux
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Nicole Pappler · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2490/\n\nA safety case is the structured argume
 nt that a system is acceptably safe: claims\, the evidence supporting them
 \, and the assumptions under which the argument holds. Building and mainta
 ining one is largely manual\, and keeping it consistent as a design evolve
 s is harder still.\nSPDX 3.1's Functional Safety profile changes the start
 ing point by giving the safety case a machine-readable form. It models req
 uirements and their refinement\, verification activities\, pass/fail evalu
 ations\, evidence\, and assumptions of use—the same structure safety pra
 ctitioners already reason about\, now expressed as a graph that tools can 
 produce\, exchange\, and check.\nThis talk introduces the profile and its 
 data model: what each element means\, and how claims link to the evidence 
 that supports them and the assumptions they depend on. We show how this re
 presentation can be used to generate a safety SBOM for Linux—a safety ca
 se generated and recomputed from project content rather than assembled by 
 hand—building on the work of the ELISA Architecture working group and it
 s current efforts on requirements. We argue this graph-based approach is t
 he foundation for an automated handshake between upstream evidence and dow
 nstream safety arguments\, enabling the kind of contract-style consumption
  of Linux components that safety-critical projects need.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2490/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2593@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131500
SUMMARY:16KB page cache and mTHP on Android
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Barry Song\, Bo Zhang\, nanzhe zhao · https://lp
 c.events/event/20/contributions/2593/\n\nThis topic proposes exploring the
  possibility of using 16 KB for both\npage cache and mTHP on Android.\n\nC
 ollaborating with Kalesh Singh\, Ryan Roberts\, David Hildenbrand and\noth
 ers\, Xiaomi is exploring the use of 16 KB large folios for both page\ncac
 he and anonymous memory.\n\nWe have posted an RFC patchset for large folio
  support in F2FS\, which\nhas been working well on Android devices so far:
 \n\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260622160830.324455-1-zhaonanzhe@xiaomi
 .com/\n\nIn this discussion\, we would like to present the data we have co
 llected\nusing 16 KB page cache and mTHP\, and discuss the following topic
 s:\n\n1. Pros and cons of using 16 KB large folios with 16 KB base pages.\
 n\n2. Memory fragmentation and direct reclaim issues observed with 16 KB\n
    large folios\, along with potential compaction optimizations for a\n   
 lightweight mechanism targeting 16 KB folios only.\n\n3. Potential Scudo a
 nd other component optimizations to reduce memory\n   footprint while usin
 g 16 KB as the management granularity. These\n   optimizations could also 
 be beneficial when using 16 KB as the base\n   page size.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2593/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2517@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:DAXFS: What Does Shared-Memory Storage Need from DAX\, VFS\, and CX
 L Memory Management?
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Device and Specific Purpose Memory MC · Cong Wang · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2517/\n\nWhen multiple kernels or CXL-con
 nected hosts share byte-addressable memory\, every existing filesystem opt
 ion pays a copy per participant: tmpfs replicates content N times\, erofs 
 and fscache keep a private page cache per kernel. To ground the discussion
  we bring DAXFS\, a prototype filesystem that runs directly on DAX memory 
 with no block layer: one shared namespace\, a cooperative page cache in th
 e shared region\, and mounts backed by a raw physical range or a dma-buf\,
  so GPUs reach file data zero-copy through the backing buffer. Unlike famf
 s\, where a userspace master preallocates files and distributes metadata t
 o read-only clients out of band\, DAXFS is masterless: metadata lives in t
 he shared region itself and any participant can create files and COW-write
  pages concurrently through lock-free CAS updates\, no metadata server\, n
 o out-of-band log.\n\nThe killer use case we are building toward is checkp
 oint and restore of AI agent sandboxes. Thousands of short-lived agent ins
 tances share one mapping of the base rootfs and model weights\, with writa
 ble state as COW overlay pages in shared memory. Today the image carries a
  single shared overlay\; giving each sandbox its own sealable overlay woul
 d make checkpoint a seal operation and restore (or forking N agents from o
 ne checkpoint) just a map: no gigabytes serialized through a block device\
 , cold start drops from minutes to seconds.\n\nBuilding it surfaced gaps b
 igger than one filesystem\, and we want the MC to weigh in:\n\n* DAXFS byp
 asses fs/dax entirely (memremap plus raw PFN mappings) because fs/dax assu
 mes a filesystem privately owns its dax_device. How do shared multi-writer
  DAX mappings become supported\, not a special case?\n\n* Is a filesystem 
 the right ABI for pooled memory carved into named shareable objects\, or s
 hould this be device-dax\, a sparse memfd (per the DCD discussions)\, or g
 uest_memfd-shaped?\n\n* DAXFS mounts from an imported dma-buf today\; expo
 rting per-file extents as dma-bufs is the natural next step. What are the 
 lifetime and coherence rules when the exporter is a filesystem over shared
  device memory\, with P2P DMA targeting it?\n\n* Who owns allocation when 
 N hosts share a Dynamic Capacity Device\, and how does DCD extent release 
 interact with live filesystem data?\n\n* Where is the line between memory 
 core MM manages and memory a cross-kernel service manages\, and how do hot
 ness tracking and migration see pages no single kernel owns?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2517/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2630@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T131500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Compatibility for 4KB Applications on Large Page-Sized Systems
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Android MC · Frederick Mayle\, Kalesh Singh · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2630/\n\nAs the ecosystem shifts toward larger 
 page sizes for enhanced performance\, preserving functionality for legacy 
 applications remains a significant challenge. This talk will introduce a p
 er-process 4KB compatibility mode on native 16KB kernel. Primarily for leg
 acy Android applications\, this approach intentionally prioritizes functio
 nal correctness over memory efficiency\, allowing legacy software to run s
 eamlessly while ensuring native 16KB applications experience zero compatib
 ility overhead.\n\nWe plan to discuss to the design\, current validation s
 tatus\, and the subsystem integration work required to bridge the gap betw
 een 4KB legacy user spaces and 16KB native environments\; and outstanding 
 challenges of ensuring consistency and correctness in a mixed-mode e4KB PT
 Es --> 16KB Folio environment
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2630/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2542@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T132500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T133000
SUMMARY:Wrap up
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Safe Systems with Linux MC · Kate Stewart\, Philipp Ahmann · 
 https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2542/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2542/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2442@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
SUMMARY:BPF Kernel Verifier meets GCC: A saga of arranged marriage
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Vineet Gupta · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2442/\n\nBPF Verifier has troubles grok'ing GCC generated code (h
 aving evolved with LLVM over time). This talk is about my recent work on B
 PF GCC and the kernel verifier and efforts to make them like each other mo
 re.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2442/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2396@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
SUMMARY:Advancing PGO\, AutoFDO\, and Propeller in the Linux Kernel
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Rong Xu · https://lpc.events/event/20/cont
 ributions/2396/\n\nAutoFDO and Propeller are upstream and PGO is available
  out of tree\, but none of them supports kernel modules\, which run 4–5%
  of kernel cycles on Google's servers and carry performance-sensitive vend
 or drivers on Android. Part 1 presents per-module profiles for all three b
 ehind one Kbuild interface\, and a path to upstream PGO by reusing compile
 r-rt's profile code. Part 2 shows why function-level coverage misleads onc
 e functions are split\, and introduces line-level attribution and Cycles p
 er Byte (CPB) to measure how well a kernel's hot code is laid out.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2396/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2346@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
SUMMARY:Nova — Building an NVIDIA GPU Driver in Rust Upstream
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Danilo Krummrich\, John Hubbard · https:
 //lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2346/\n\nNova is an open-source NVIDIA
  GPU driver being developed entirely upstream from day one — and in Rust
 . This talk presents the current status and roadmap of the project\, descr
 ibes the upstream development process\, and dives into the driver's archit
 ecture and how Rust shapes it.\n\nOn the technical side\, we present Nova'
 s architecture: the split into nova-core\, nova-drm\, vGPU support\, and f
 wctl\, the rationale behind this decomposition\, and how the components in
 teract across bare-metal and virtualized environments. We follow up with i
 mplementation details showing how Rust's type system and ownership model h
 elp enforce the boundaries between these components at compile time in the
  context of the driver model and the DRM subsystem — and show why the DR
 M subsystem provides a challenge in this regard.\n\nFinally\, we examine t
 he Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) architecture common in GPU drivers\, w
 here multiple generations of hardware must be supported through composable
  abstraction layers. We discuss how Rust's trait system and generics provi
 de stronger compositional guarantees than C when building and maintaining 
 these layered abstractions.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2346/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2578@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T152000
SUMMARY:Linux Crypto API is dead\, long live Linux Crypto API v2?
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Ignat Korchagin · https://lpc.e
 vents/event/20/contributions/2578/\n\nLinux Crypto API has recently come u
 nder fire due to a series of discovered vulnerabilities\, like [Copy Fail]
 [1] and friends. This resulted in a drastic response from some maintainers
 : deprecation of the [Linux Crypto API userspace interface][2]. However\, 
 it feels like cracking a nut with a sledgehammer\, as some vulnerabilities
 \, like [Dirty Frag][3]\, do not even use the userspace interface directly
  for the exploit.\n\nDoing crypto in the kernel on behalf of userspace cod
 e may bring some security and efficiency advantages:\n\n- the key material
  is not exposed to the process address space anymore\, which can save the 
 key from very common memory bugs (remember Heartbleed?)\n- abstracting the
  key material as a kernel object (e.g.\, a file descriptor) allows sharing
  the key usage across unprivileged applications without giving those appli
 cations access to the key material (software HSM/KMS use case\, cloud work
 loads\, K8s)\n- the kernel is best positioned to abstract modern cryptogra
 phic hardware and allow applications to utilize it and reap the performanc
 e benefits\n- highly-constrained embedded systems may have fewer dependenc
 ies as they don't have to pull extra crypto libraries such as OpenSSL or g
 crypt into their firmware\n\nOne of the reasons quoted for the deprecation
  is that the userspace Linux Crypto API interface is not efficient anyway 
 and there are very few users. But maybe there are few users\, because it i
 s not very efficient and apparently insecure? What if instead of disabling
  it we improve it or even redesign? For example:\n\n- replace the splice()
 -based zero-copy interface (which was targeted by the vulnerabilities in t
 he first place and is not truly zero-copy anyway) with a modern primitive 
 such as io_uring\n- fix the limitation of authenticated encryption being a
 ble to process only ~64k of data\n- replace the confusing network-based AF
 _ALG protocol with something simpler\, such as plain device files/sysfs\n-
  use eBPF to expose Linux Crypto API to userspace in a more constrained an
 d controllable manner\n- limit the number of algorithms exposable to users
 pace – only general-purpose algorithms should be exposed\n\nThe goal of 
 this session is to reach a shared view on whether the userspace Linux Cryp
 to API is worth saving and\, if so\, what a v2 should look like.\n\n[1]: h
 ttps://copy.fail/\n[2]: https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/20260430011544.3182
 3-1-ebiggers@kernel.org/\n[3]: https://github.com/v4bel/dirtyfrag
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2578/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2419@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
SUMMARY:Reducing kernel stack memory (on arm64)
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Will Deacon · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2419/\n\nThere have been a bunch of attempts [1] 
 to reduce the memory consumed by kernel stacks for x86 and arm64\, largely
  based around the idea of dynamically growing the kernel stack allocation 
 based on page faults. This poses what appear to be insurmountable challeng
 es\, as it introduces complexity into the architecture exception entry cod
 e (which needs to be able to transition cleanly to a new stack) but also m
 eans that the kernel must be able to allocate memory from any context at a
 ll.\n\nI would like to discuss and explore alternatives to dynamic stack a
 llocation based on some initial rework I have started of the arm64 excepti
 on entry code[2]. Specifically:\n\n- Revisiting the decision to move from 
 8k to 16k stacks\n- Changing the kernel stack size at boot time\n- Changin
 g the kernel stack size per task\n- Controlling the kernel stack size from
  userspace\n\nI think it would also be useful to talk briefly about the st
 icking points with dynamic kernel stacks and whether there is anything tha
 t can be carried forward from that work.\n\nMy motivation is based on Andr
 oid vendors having various out-of-tree implementations of the dynamic kern
 el stack series and I would like to see if there are alternatives that we 
 can provide upstream so that we can all stop carrying the broken stuff aro
 und in our pockets.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260424191456.2679
 717-1-stevensd@google.com/\n[2] https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kerne
 l/git/will/linux.git/log/?h=overflow-stack
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2419/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2381@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
SUMMARY:Debugging CPU isolation / nohz_full
LOCATION:Club B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Frederic Weisbecker · https://lpc.
 events/event/20/contributions/2381/\n\nInstalling a CPU isolated workload 
 for extreme low-latency expectations can be challenging. Requirements and 
 settings have been recently documented upstream but practice is another st
 ory. Let's discuss that around a live example.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2381/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2522@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T152500
SUMMARY:Secure vIOMMU architecture and implementation challenges
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Suravee Suthikulpanit\, Vasant Hegde · ht
 tps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2522/\n\nModern cloud computing in
 creasingly relies on confidential virtual machines (VMs) - isolated enviro
 nments\nwhere sensitive workloads run securely\, even from the underlying 
 hypervisor. A key challenge in this space\nis enabling these confidential 
 VMs to safely communicate with hardware devices without exposing their dat
 a\nto the host system or hypervisor.\n\nVirtual IOMMU (vIOMMU) is an exten
 ded feature of AMD's IOMMU that allows IOMMU hardware to directly access\n
 and process virtual machine data structures. Building on this foundation\,
  AMD's Secure vIOMMU extends\nvIOMMU capabilities to support SEV-TIO (Secu
 re Encrypted Virtualization - Trusted IO) device assignment to\nconfidenti
 al virtual machines. Specifically\, it enables secure communication channe
 ls between confidential\nvirtual machine and IOMMU hardware enabling relia
 ble management of device DMAs including guest TLBs without\nhypervisor int
 ervention.\n\nThis presentation introduces the Secure vIOMMU architecture 
 and explains how its components work together\n-- including the IOMMU\, AM
 D's Security Processor\, the hypervisor\, Virtual Machine Manager (VMM) an
 d\nconfidential VMs. We will also cover supported operational modes (vTOM 
 and Guest Page Table) for secure\ndata transmission between Trusted I/O de
 vices and confidential VM.\n\nFinally\, we will discuss the interface chal
 lenges that arise across various kernel modules including IOMMU\,\nccp\, K
 VM\, SEV-TIO\, etc\, and explore potential approaches to address them.\n\n
 SEV-TIO specification: https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/58271_0.91\nSecure v
 IOMMU initial code: https://github.com/AMDESE/linux-iommu/tree/sviommu/tsm
 0407_v619_v0
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2522/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2401@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
SUMMARY:Optimizing Gaming Performance and Power Efficiency with Intel LPMD
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Gaming on Linux MC · Srinivas Pandruvada · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2401/\n\nGaming performance remains critical for u
 ser experience\, but power efficiency has become equally important\, parti
 cularly for battery-powered devices. Also improves thermal management and 
 reduce thermal throttling. LPMD offers a solution for enhancing energy eff
 iciency by dynamically selecting optimal processor configurations and powe
 r slider settings based on CPU and GPU utilization.\nThis session demonstr
 ates how Intel LPMD can be configured to improve energy efficiency during 
 gaming workloads while maintaining acceptable performance levels. Will pre
 sent some performance metrics showing improvements in power consumption an
 d thermal behavior. The discussion will cover LPMD configuration\, impleme
 ntation considerations\, and get some suggestions from gaming community.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2401/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2585@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T152500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
SUMMARY:Early Attestation is Very Harmful. Why is it Required?
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Muhammad Usama Sardar · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2585/\n\n**Abstract**:\nThis talk is a 
 follow-up of LPC'25\, where we introduced the security problem to the comm
 unity for a suitable approach of attested TLS protocols. We would like to 
 present updates to the work since LPC'25 and discuss open problems with th
 e community so that the feedback can be accommodated in the standardizatio
 n in the IETF. In particular\, we share our discovered critical-severity C
 VEs\, such as [CVE-2026-92701][1]\, [CVE-2026-92702][2]\, [CVE-2026-100835
 ][3]\, each of CVSS 9.1\, and other [critical- and high-severity security 
 concerns][4] against early attestation\, and ask the community whether ear
 ly attestation is really necessary.\n\n**Discussion**:\nWe would like to d
 iscuss:\n\n- Section 7 of [this work][5].\n- [Questions][6] raised by the 
 IETF community\n\n**Background**:\nTransport Layer Security (TLS) is a wid
 ely used protocol for secure channel establishment. However\, it lacks an 
 inherent mechanism for validating the security state of the workload and i
 ts platform. To address this\, remote attestation can be integrated into T
 LS\, which is named attested TLS protocol.\n\nAs presented in LPC'25\, the
 re are two key approaches for this integration\, namely early (intra-hands
 hake) and standard (post-handshake) attestation. We also present insights 
 from formal verification using the state-of-the-art symbolic security anal
 ysis tool ProVerif that led to the discovery of the critical- and high-sev
 erity CVEs.\n\nCurrent project partners include CoreWeave\, University of 
 Namur\, Stevens Institute of Technology\, Shanghai Guan An Information Tec
 hnology\, Switch\, Oxford University\, Sureshot Labs\, PHI-OMEGA\, HashClo
 ak Inc\, Stoffel Labs Inc\, EMILIA Protocol\, ESILV\, Birmingham City Univ
 ersity\, Verily\, Arm\, Linaro\, Siemens\, Huawei\, Intuit\, Axis\, Bonn-R
 hein-Sieg University of Applied Sciences\, and Barkhausen Institut. By thi
 s talk\, we hope to inspire more open-source contributors to this project.
 \n\nThe attendees will gain technical insights into the latest development
 s of standardization of attested TLS protocols in the IETF\, and will be a
 ble to provide feedback on the standardization effort of attested TLS.\n\n
 **Benefits to the ecosystem**\nOur thorough formal security analysis shows
  that early attestation is potentially vulnerable to replay\, relay\, dive
 rsion\, and misbinding attacks. Our analysis shows that:\n\n 1. Early atte
 station adds unnecessary complexity in the handshake.\n 2. Early attestati
 on results in several security and privacy problems\, such as those captur
 ed in published CVEs.\n 3. All use cases of early attestation can be handl
 ed with standard attestation while offering better security properties.\n\
 nBecause of critical-severity CVEs and fundamental architecture problems i
 n early attestation\, we believe early attestation is very harmful to the 
 TEE attestation ecosystem. We would like to discuss with the community whe
 ther it is necessary.\n\n  [1]: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-
 92701\n  [2]: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-92702\n  [3]: http
 s://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-100835\n  [4]: https://datatracker.i
 etf.org/doc/draft-intra-handshake-fail/\n  [5]: https://www.researchgate.n
 et/publication/414529199_EarlyAttestationBleed_Three_Critical-severity_Vul
 nerabilities_of_CVSS_90_in_Confidential_Computing\n  [6]: https://www.ietf
 .org/archive/id/draft-intra-handshake-fail-54.html#section-17.2.1
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2585/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2624@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T152500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
SUMMARY:IOMMU Page Table Observability and Reclamation
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Logan Odell\, Pranjal Shrivastava · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2624/\n\nThe current Linux memory man
 agement framework lacks the mechanism for tracking IOMMU page table (IOPT)
  consumption across sparse virtualization workloads. By utilizing the IOMM
 UFD and VFIO frameworks\, hypervisors dynamically map and unmap massive IO
 VA regions\, often leaving intermediate page table directories stranded an
 d invisible to the core  kernel. This session talks about the current stat
 e of IOPT observability and attempts to propose new features and enhanceme
 nts for IOPT memory reclamation.\n\nThe session proposes a design for an I
 OMMU Page Table Shrinker that allows lockless and lazy reclamation of stra
 nded IOPT memory\, specifically by the intermediate tables while employing
  the generic_pt to keep the shrinker arch-agnostic. We invite a discussion
  to explore strategies for lockless tracking\, page table branch severance
 \, and lifecycle management using RCU state machines to ensure that the co
 re kernel can harvest stranded memory under pressure without penalizing th
 e IOMMU mapping paths.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2624/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2447@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
SUMMARY:Evolution of Value Tracking in the BPF Verifier
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Shung-Hsi Yu · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2447/\n\nWhile safety and performance get most of the credit for 
 BPF's success\, a safe and fast program that couldn't do anything interest
 ing would be rather useless. Much of the flexibility that makes BPF progra
 ms interesting today (e.g. indexing into a map value at a computed offset\
 , walking packet data\, reading a stack array at a variable index) was act
 ually not there at the start.\n\nIn this talk\, we'll give a thousand-foot
  overview that goes from the initial tracking of constant/unknown all the 
 way to cnum\, covering topics like scalar IDs\, sub-register bounds\, boun
 ds syncing\, as well as value-tracking bugs along the way.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2447/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2392@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
SUMMARY:Upgrading Runtime Leaks to Compile-Time Errors: Adopting Clang’s 
 require_explicit_initialization
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Ian Rogers · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2392/\n\nThe Linux kernel makes heavy use of aggregate struct
  initializers to configure subsystem interfaces\, driver registries\, and 
 object lifecycles (such as struct kobj_type and struct device_type). Howev
 er\, omitting mandatory struct members—such as a kobject's release callb
 ack—may result in silent memory leaks or deferred runtime panics.\n\nCla
 ng offers a static alternative attribute ‘require_explicit_initializatio
 n’\, which triggers the -Wuninitialized-explicit-init warning if a desig
 nated field is left uninitialized during aggregate construction . This tal
 k is proposed as a collaborative discussion to brainstorm how we can adopt
  this safety mechanism in kernel headers (e.g.\, via a macro like __requir
 ed_init) . We will discuss the candidate structures that would benefit mos
 t\, evaluate the engineering friction of enforcing this across cross-tree 
 refactors\, and address compiler parity issues—specifically how to handl
 e GCC fallback and whether a similar diagnostic can be proposed for GCC.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2392/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2421@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T155000
SUMMARY:Better Anon (Swap) Readahead
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Kairui Song · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2421/\n\nWhen handling swap page faults\, the ker
 nel often don't reads just a single page. For legacy HDDs\, an entire clus
 ter is read in. For SSDs\, we check the VMA and page tables to see if near
 by pages are likely to be needed soon\, using a basic statistic-driven heu
 ristic. For compressed RAM (like zram\, and potentially soon zswap)\, trad
 itional readahead is skipped entirely. Meanwhile\, enabling (m)THP swap-in
  currently acts as kind of a speculative fetching.\n\nThe current implemen
 tation is not really ideal. For example\, why can't we extend (m)THP suppo
 rt to SSDs by merging readahead detection with (m)THP lookaround? Why can'
 t we enable a lightweight readahead for compressed RAM as well? We also ne
 ed a smarter heuristic\, where workingset shadow entries could provide a m
 uch better hint than basic per-VMA statistics?\n\nWith so much active deve
 lopment in the swap subsystem\, the goal of this session is to consolidate
  these scattered paths\, and explore whether the swap readahead mechanism 
 can share common\, useful infrastructure.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2421/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2400@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
SUMMARY:GPU Job Scheduling: Current Development
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Gaming on Linux MC · Daniel Almeida\, Philipp Stanner · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2400/\n\nThe Linux kernel is responsi
 ble for managing the dependency graph of userspace's compute and graphics 
 shaders. Moreover\, it handles the GPU load balancing and tries to guarant
 ee forward progress and deadlock resistance.\n\nHistorically\, most graphi
 cs drivers have used drm_sched\, a problematic legacy code base\, for thes
 e tasks. More recently\, Rust drivers are ramping up their own infrastruct
 ure to achieve the same\, while trying to learn from the mistakes of the p
 ast and to leverage the features of the Rust programming language for robu
 stness and reliability. Notably\, engineers at Collabora and Red Hat colla
 boratively develop a successor in Rust for drm_sched\, drm::JobQueue.\n\nT
 his talk briefly informs about the aforementioned developments and then op
 ens room for discussions which may help the developers (who are – in par
 t – concerned with the compute aspect of GPUs) understand the wider comm
 unity's pains and requirements\, such as from the graphics and video game 
 parties. Topics that could be discussed are\, for example:\n\n- Future of 
 GPU scheduling: more control is handed over to the GPU's firmware\, making
  it difficult or impossible for the kernel to prioritize real time applica
 tions such as games.\n- What are the major issues in the Linux kernel rega
 rding stability\, e.g.\, crashing video games?\n- One reason why drm_sched
  is in bad shape is that super-priority was given to performance\, for exa
 mple by omitting locks. Fixing some of these missing locks has been reject
 ed\, due to reported massive performance regressions. Would video game dev
 elopers argue that ~100% reliability is worth significant sacrifices with 
 frames per second?\n- Used hardware: Which GPUs and drivers are most impor
 tant for gaming on Linux\, and which would the community desire to be supp
 orted better the most?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2400/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2347@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Long-term latency monitoring of real-time Linux systems
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Jan Altenberg · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2347/\n\nWith the PREEMPT_RT configuration being merged 
 for the 6.12 release a milestone of a twenty years lasting journey was rea
 ched: Linux officially became an RTOS! During that time many technical iss
 ues have been resolved and many features have been added that made Linux e
 ven better even for non real-time users. A specific challenge which had to
  be tackled was testing and proving the real-time behavior. While for clas
 sical RTOSes traditionally a path analysis was carried out\, this is close
  to impossible for a modern operating system such as Linux - not just beca
 use of the complexity of the software: Modern processors do come with a lo
 t of performance with the price of being non-deterministic due to several 
 levels of caches\, speculation engines and other techniques. As a result t
 he real-time behavior of modern systems has to be evaluated. This is why t
 he OSADL QA Farm was born\, doing comparable measurements on a huge variet
 y of systems collecting long-term data to prove stability in the field. Bu
 t even after 20 years of operation work is not done yet. Linux is evolving
  rapidly and the test scenarios (also for real-time) have to adopt. Apart 
 from that Open Source RTOSes are also approaching small processors. This i
 s why the OSADL QA Farm was recently extended with Zephyr tests.\nThis pre
 sentation gives an overview on best practices for evaluating the real-time
  behavior of Linux (and other) systems\, sharing the experience from the O
 SADL QA Farm. It also wants to serve as a basis for discussion on how meas
 urements shall be carried out in future and how data can be efficiently sh
 ared.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2347/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2591@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160500
SUMMARY:Extending BPF Signing into an IMA Integrity Lifecycle
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Hongming Qiu · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2591/\n\neBPF programs enter the kernel as privi
 leged extensions and\, once admitted\, become part of the system’s trust
 ed computing base. Prior LPC discussions established BPF signing as a way 
 to track the provenance of these programs\, while deliberately separating 
 signature verification from the policy that consumes it [1\,2]. IMA was id
 entified as one possible consumer through LSM integration\, but its enforc
 ement semantics were left open. Follow-up discussion highlighted unresolve
 d issues involving dynamically generated programs\, trusted loaders\, and 
 applications such as Cilium and bpftrace.\n\nWe developed IMA-BPF as a con
 crete continuation of this work. IMA-BPF connects BPF signatures to IMA po
 licy\, measurement\, appraisal\, and TPM-backed attestation. It preserves 
 signer provenance for programs created by signed loaders\, allowing reside
 nt programs to be re-evaluated after the original loader disappears. We al
 so introduce reappraisal and purge mechanisms\, allowing programs that no 
 longer satisfy current policy or trust state to be detached and removed th
 rough BPF’s link and reference based lifetime mechanisms.\n\nThis talk w
 ill explain how IMA-BPF extends BPF signing into an integrity lifecycle\, 
 evaluated through Cilium vulnerability remediation. We hope to discuss thr
 ee areas: the practicality of adopting signing across real BPF workloads\;
  how the BPF ecosystem should standardize provenance for dynamically gener
 ated programs\, such as providing common reference tooling in different la
 nguages\; and how our initial reappraisal and revocation policies and mech
 anisms should be refined. Although our prototype demonstrates this lifecyc
 le end to end\, we hope to use the discussion to improve the design and id
 entify a practical path toward upstream adoption.\n\nReferences\n\n1. KP S
 ingh\, BPF Signing and IMA Integration\, https://lpc.events/event/16/contr
 ibutions/1357/\n2. KP Singh\, Discussion: What’s Next for BPF Signing\, 
 https://lpc.events/event/19/contributions/2167/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2591/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2382@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Rust for Linux Office Hours
LOCATION:Club B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Alice Ryhl\, Andreas Hindborg\, Ben
 no Lossin\, Boqun Feng\, Danilo Krummrich\, Gary Guo\, Miguel Ojeda · htt
 ps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2382/\n\nA Rust-related BoF to work
  together on several topics\, to answer questions or resolve pain points f
 rom attendees\, to get to know people interested in Rust and Rust for Linu
 x and generally to have some more time for discussion on top of the Rust M
 C.\n\nThe list of topics will be developed closer to the conference (and m
 ore may be added during the conference too)\, but some examples of potenti
 al topics would be:\n\n  - Review and discussion of particular patch serie
 s.\n  - Prototyping of a small project\, e.g. implementing a kernel module
 .\n  - Providing assistance with `pin-init` and Klint.\n\nPlease feel free
  to join!
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2382/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2520@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
SUMMARY:IOMMUFD Support for Microsoft Hypervisor Hosts
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Jacob Pan · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2520/\n\nDevice assignment in Microsoft Hypervisor (/dev/mshv
 ) host environments has requirements beyond existing KVM-oriented workflow
 s\, including direct HWPT attachment for assigning a device to a guest whi
 le preserving existing Stage-2 page tables\, and VMM-assigned virtual devi
 ce information to facilitate hypercall-based device operations.\n\nWe plan
  to publish an RFC before LPC describing the proposed iommufd/VFIO plumbin
 g for MSHV device assignment. At LPC\, we would like to discuss the remain
 ing design questions and potential follow-on work\, including alignment wi
 th Xen and how the same model could support future use cases such as TDISP
  or page table sharing in general.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2520/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2416@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T155000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
SUMMARY:DMA-buf cgroup accounting for proxy allocators: attributing buffers
  to the right consumer
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Albert Esteve\, T.J. Mercier · 
 https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2416/\n\nOn embedded and automot
 ive Linux systems\, a single daemon often allocates DMA-buf memory on beha
 lf of other clients. Today\, most of that memory is invisible to cgroup ac
 counting\, only system-heap buffers can carry a charge via __GFP_ACCOUNT\,
  and even then it lands on the allocator’s cgroup rather than the app th
 at requested the buffer.\n\nThe misattribution has real consequences on an
 y system that uses cgroup memory limits to drive reclaim or enforce resour
 ce budgets\, as untracked buffers silently skew pressure signals. For exam
 ple\, on Android\, where app memory limits depend on per-app accounting\; 
 or on automotive systems\, where strict Freedom From Interference (FFI) is
  a key requirement and a central allocator absorbing the charge of its cli
 ents across domains breaks any meaningful resource isolation argument.\n\n
 In this session we will discuss the design space for fixing the proxy-allo
 cator problem\, presenting the context from our proposal in upstream. Shou
 ld the charge be attributed at allocation time\, or before export time? Sh
 ould the allocator identify the target via a pidfd (natural for approaches
  like Binder\, where the allocator already knows the sender_pid) or a cgro
 upfd? What happens with buffers that may belong to different cgroups acros
 s their lifetime?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2416/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2440@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Proof-Carrying Verification for eBPF
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Martin Fink · https://lpc.events/event/20/contri
 butions/2440/\n\n## Abstract\n\neBPF provides a safe way to extend the ker
 nel functionality. To ensure safety\, the kernel verifies the memory safet
 y and termination of eBPF programs and is thus the security boundary for e
 BPF. It must accept untrusted programs from userspace and determine\, unde
 r strict time and memory limits\, whether they are safe to execute.\n\nTod
 ay\, the verifier symbolically executes program paths\, tracks abstract re
 gister and memory states\, and uses state pruning and repeated loop explor
 ation to establish safety. Keeping both proof discovery and proof checking
  in the kernel has the important advantage that the kernel does not need t
 o trust the compiler or any userspace component.\n\nThe cost of this desig
 n is that the kernel has to employ a sophisticated analysis capable of rec
 onstructing invariants from eBPF bytecode. Compilers may already discover 
 source-level relationships and loop invariants\, but these facts are not n
 ormally conveyed to the kernel. When the verifier cannot recover invariant
 s\, it must conservatively reject the program. At the same time\, the comp
 lexity of the analysis makes it difficult to audit and maintain this secur
 ity boundary\, and verifier bugs can result in malicious programs being ac
 cepted. Verification failures can also be difficult to predict and relate 
 back to the source program.\n\nWe are exploring a proof-carrying verificat
 ion architecture that separates proof generation from proof checking. An u
 ntrusted userspace proof generator analyzes the program\, discovers invari
 ants\, and uses an SMT solver to discharge safety obligations. If successf
 ul\, it emits a certificate containing the invariants and proof steps need
 ed to validate the program. If an obligation cannot be proved\, the tool c
 an use compiler metadata and solver output to distinguish concrete counter
 examples from unsupported reasoning or missing invariants\, and produce so
 urce-oriented diagnostics.\n\nInside the kernel\, a domain-specific checke
 r validates the program and certificate together. The certificate supplies
  facts such as basic-block and loop invariants\, pointer bounds\, and rela
 tionships between program values. The checker does not search for invarian
 ts or invoke an SMT solver. It validates each claimed state transition and
  entailment using a restricted set of eBPF-specific reasoning rules.\n\nFo
 r example\, a loop certificate may provide an invariant relating an induct
 ion variable\, a packet pointer\, and data_end. The checker verifies that 
 the invariant holds on entry\, is preserved across the back edge\, and is 
 strong enough to justify each memory access. It does not need to infer the
  invariant or repeatedly explore the loop.\n\nWe have built a preliminary 
 end-to-end prototype that generates and checks certificates for a subset o
 f eBPF programs\, including examples with control flow\, loops\, scalar re
 lationships\, and memory-safety obligations. Our early experiments indicat
 e that certificate checking can perform particularly well on larger progra
 ms for which abstract interpretation requires extensive path exploration\,
  state merging\, or repeated loop analysis. In these cases\, the checker b
 enefits from being given the required invariants directly and can avoid mu
 ch of the search performed by the existing verifier. These results are sti
 ll preliminary and currently apply only to the supported subset. At LPC\, 
 we will present the supported feature set\, representative examples\, and 
 an initial comparison of verification behavior and runtime\, together with
  the cases where the approach does not yet apply.\n\nA central design prob
 lem is defining the safety policy shared by the proof generator and checke
 r. This policy must cover instructions\, helper calls\, kfuncs\, program c
 ontexts\, pointer accesses\, and kernel-managed resources\, while evolving
  with eBPF and its kernel interfaces. Its format\, maintenance model\, and
  relationship to existing verifier semantics are part of the research ques
 tion.\n\nThe talk will present the architecture\, certificate language\, p
 rototype\, and initial results. We will also discuss certificate size\, ma
 lformed or adversarial certificates\, unsupported features\, policy evolut
 ion\, verifier transformations\, and the relationship between checked byte
 code and JIT semantics.\n\nWe seek feedback from the eBPF community on whe
 ther this is a useful and technically plausible way to structure safety ch
 ecking\, which parts of current verifier semantics are hardest to express 
 as explicit proof obligations\, and whether a maintainable shared safety p
 olicy is possible.\n\n## Relation to Prior Work\n\nProof-carrying verifica
 tion for eBPF was previously explored by Exoverifier\, which separates pro
 of generation from proof checking and emits general proof objects based on
  a Lean formalization of eBPF semantics. More recently\, VEP proposed an a
 nnotation-guided toolchain with userspace verification\, a specialized com
 piler\, and a lightweight bytecode-level proof checker. BCF takes a hybrid
  approach: it retains the kernel verifier’s abstract interpretation\, wh
 ile using machine-checkable proofs generated in userspace to guide abstrac
 tion refinement and improve precision.\n\nOur work explores another point 
 in this design space. Rather than relying on general theorem-prover object
 s\, source annotations\, or refinement of the existing verifier analysis\,
  we are designing a restricted certificate language around eBPF-specific i
 nvariants and state transitions. The checker accepts only a fixed set of r
 easoning steps\, with explicit limits on certificate structure and checkin
 g work. Our preliminary prototype suggests that this model is sufficient f
 or a useful subset of programs while remaining closely aligned with curren
 t eBPF concepts. The talk will compare these approaches and discuss which 
 parts of eBPF verification fit restricted certificate checking and which m
 ay require richer reasoning.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2440/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2398@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Kage: device driver isolation in the Linux kernel with LFI
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Zachary Yedidia · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2398/\n\nDrivers in Linux run with full kernel privilege
 s and account for a significant share of exploitable vulnerabilities. Kage
  is an experimental kernel subsystem that runs kernel modules inside of in
 -kernel sandboxes using LLVM's Lightweight Fault Isolation (LFI) feature. 
 Kaged drivers still run in the same hardware privilege level and address s
 pace as the rest of the kernel\, but are compiled such that control-flow a
 nd memory accesses are confined to a subset of virtual memory. This keeps 
 transition costs cheap and reduces the engineering effort needed to retrof
 it isolation onto existing drivers. LFI is designed to provide secure isol
 ation even for arbitrarily malicious code through the use of a machine cod
 e verifier that keeps the compiler out of the trusted code base.\n\nThis t
 alk will walk through a prototype version of Kage\, which can load LFI-bui
 lt kernel modules into an isolated portion of the address space\, and demo
 nstrate how drivers can be ported to run in sandboxes. Kage uses BTF type 
 information to automatically check call signatures and replace kernel poin
 ters with opaque handles on calls between the kernel and the module. The c
 urrent prototype can sandbox simple computational kernel modules\, and is 
 expected to grow in capabilities in order to handle MMIO\, buffer sharing\
 , DMA (via the IOMMU)\, and interrupt routing\, with the end goal of being
  able to isolate real\, complex drivers\, such as USB\, network\, or possi
 bly even GPU drivers.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2398/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2403@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:EPP-boost: Per-core util-based performance boosting in AMD p-state 
 driver
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Gaming on Linux MC · David Vernet · https://lpc.events/event/
 20/contributions/2403/\n\nI've observed that on the Steam Deck\, changes t
 o the AMD p-state driver can have a significant impact on tail latencies a
 nd stale frame numbers. I've submitted an RFC upstream which proposes a pe
 r-CPU EPP boost heuristic: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260728073150.5496
 4-1-void@manifault.com/.\n\nWe should discuss gaps in the current cpufreq 
 / AMD p-state driver\, and how to best address frequency scaling for gamin
 g workloads in general.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2403/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2592@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Attestation-Based Storage Provisioning for Linux: Towards a Standar
 d Model
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Nandakumar Raghavan · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2592/\n\nLinux has no standard model for 
 provisioning and unlocking encrypted persistent storage at boot in environ
 ments where the underlying platform cannot be fully trusted. Today\, every
  deployment stack solves this independently: custom boot agents\, post-pro
 visioning scripts\, and platform-specific unlock logic. The mechanism exis
 ts — LUKS2 tokens and the cryptsetup plugin interface — but the workfl
 ow around it does not. Every team that wants attestation-backed storage un
 locking ends up writing the same three things from scratch: a script to re
 gister token metadata at provisioning time\, a plugin to perform attestati
 on and key retrieval at boot\, and custom logic to handle failure cases. N
 one of this is reusable across platforms or deployable through standard Li
 nux tooling.\n\nThis session proposes that the LUKS2 token mechanism and t
 he cryptsetup plugin interface should become the standard interoperability
  contract between Linux storage tooling and external attestation-aware key
  services. In this model\, a Key Broker Service holds encryption keys and 
 releases them only after the system proves its identity through remote att
 estation. The LUKS2 header carries the token metadata that tells systemd-c
 ryptsetup which plugin to invoke at boot\, making the storage layer indepe
 ndent of any specific attestation technology\, hardware platform\, or key 
 service implementation. The provisioning side is handled by systemd-repart
 \, which registers the token metadata in the LUKS2 header at first-boot 
 — no separate import step\, no custom scripts.\n\nFor this model to work
  reliably end-to-end\, three specific gaps need to close. First\, the cryp
 tsetup plugin error contract is undefined: today systemd-cryptsetup silent
 ly falls through to passphrase unlock when a plugin fails\, with no distin
 ction between permanent attestation failure and transient network unavaila
 bility. A KBS plugin that fails because attestation was rejected should be
 have very differently from one that timed out waiting for the network — 
 but there is currently no standard way to express that. Second\, the LUKS2
  token JSON schema beyond type and keyslots is unspecified: KBS-specific m
 etadata such as service endpoint URLs is stored in private fields with no 
 agreed naming\, making provisioning tools and unlock plugins non-interoper
 able without bilateral coordination. Third\, there are no packaging or ini
 trd inclusion guidelines for token plugins\, leaving distributions without
  a clear answer on how to ship and discover them at boot.\n\nThe session w
 ill walk through the current state of the code in systemd-cryptsetup and s
 ystemd-repart\, identify precisely where these gaps exist today\, and open
  a discussion on what standardisation is needed. The goal is agreement on 
 reusable\, upstream building blocks for attestation-driven storage provisi
 oning — so that every platform and deployment stack building on Linux do
 es not have to solve this problem independently.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2592/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2415@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:Page fault locking
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Matthew Wilcox · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2415/\n\nIn 2023 we introduced per-VMA locks t
 o solve contention and priority inversion on the mmap_lock for multithread
 ed programs.  While successful\, the initial implementation did not solve 
 every case of contention and some workloads can still demonstrate problems
 .  This session will discuss ways of fixing the remaining problems.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2415/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2625@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T161000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T163000
SUMMARY:arm-smmu contig hint support and performance impact
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Prakash Gupta\, Vijayanand Jitta · https:
 //lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2625/\n\nThis talk covers ARM SMMU con
 tiguous-hint support in Linux stage-1 page tables and the ARM-SMMU PMU sup
 port used to measure its impact. The work enables larger effective mapping
 s\, including 64K regions built from 16 adjacent 4K entries\, and extends 
 IOMMU map/unmap support for mixed and larger mapping sizes. In parallel\, 
 ARM-SMMU PMU integration exposes TBU and TCU counters through perf\, inclu
 ding access\, TLB allocate/read/write\, cache-hit\, and page-table-walk ev
 ents\, with StreamID-based filtering for workload attribution.\n\nWe prese
 nt page-table behavior observed in tracing alongside translation-side meas
 urements from PMU counters. Early measurements show measurable gains from 
 mixed 4K/64K mappings with contiguous hint enabled: lower TLB allocation p
 ressure and lower elapsed time versus a 4K-only baseline.\n\nThe talk also
  highlights different pmu counters used\, invalidation effects\, and how w
 e can evaluate real SMMU translation performance.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2625/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2445@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
SUMMARY:Safely Optimizing eBPF for Hardware and Workloads
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · YUSHENG ZHENG\, Hao Sun · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2445/\n\neBPF users pay for verified safety with perfo
 rmance. Across 27 microbenchmarks\, we find that eBPF code runs 1.7–2.1x
  slower than the same code compiled natively. Most of the time is lost in 
 the JIT\, which translates one instruction at a time and leaves many hardw
 are features unused. The JIT also does not adapt to the workload\, such as
  stable map entries or branches. This is by design: the eBPF instruction s
 et is designed for verification\, not execution\, and the kernel must trus
 t the JIT and keeps it simple to keep the trusted computing base (TCB) sma
 ll\, so optimizing it is harder than optimizing a userspace JIT. We ask: h
 ow can we get the speed back without growing the TCB much?\n\nWe start wit
 h the hardware. We propose verified inline kfuncs (kinsn)\, which are kfun
 cs that BPF programs use like instructions\, and can be verified and inlin
 ed by the JIT. Each carries a short eBPF body that the verifier checks at 
 every call\, while the JIT inlines native code in its place. We can also f
 ormally verify the eBPF and the native code is same. Seven such operations
  speed up microbenchmarks by up to 24% and Cilium and Katran by up to 12%.
 \n\nkinsn fixes one operation at a time\, but registers\, branches and cal
 ls still slow down the whole program. What if the kernel just accepted nat
 ive code? Then we propose kprog: it runs the whole program as native code\
 , and the verifier checks an eBPF simulator of the native code\, instead o
 f JIT-compiling the eBPF into native code. You can then use any language o
 r any compiler to compile your kernel extensions. A simulator is far easie
 r and simpler to prove than an optimizing JIT\, and applications run up to
  1.83x faster.\n\nMany workload patterns appear only at run time. To use t
 hem\, we also propose Speculative ReJIT\, which rewrites an unmodified app
 lication's bytecode in userspace and resubmits it to the existing verifier
 \, so correctness stays in userspace and safety stays in the kernel.\n\nWe
  would like feedback on the kinsn interface\, on when an operation should 
 be applied at all\, and on whether kprog and Speculative ReJIT are worth p
 ursuing.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2445/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2449@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
SUMMARY:GCC Rust support for Linux
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · David Edelsohn\, Philip Herron · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2449/\n\nA conversation about the Linux k
 ernel requirements from GCC Rust and how to promote GCC Rust as another co
 mpiler for the Rust components of the Linux kernel.  The technical and com
 munity issue.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2449/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2352@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
SUMMARY:Firmware-mediated accelerators for edge AI
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Tomeu Vizoso · https://lpc.events/event/
 20/contributions/2352/\n\nA well-represented class of accelerators for AI 
 in edge deployments aren't programmed directly by the Linux kernel in the 
 host CPU\, but by firmware running on a companion core.\n\nDuring the firs
 t half of 2026 alone\, we have seen three different drivers submitted to t
 he mailing list for this specific type of hardware\, by their respective v
 endors or on their behalf (TI C7x\, NXP Neutron and Qualcomm QDA).\n\nThis
  talk will describe in detail a proposal that aligns with the upstreaming 
 requirements in the drm/accel subsystem and streamlines the functionality 
 that is needed inside the Linux kernel and the UAPI. The architecture will
  be described in detail: firmware\, kernel and userspace.\n\nAn important 
 benefit of the approach is that users will be able to accelerate their wor
 kloads without having to integrate any vendor BSPs or vendor-specific soft
 ware.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2352/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2589@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T172000
SUMMARY:Secure Boot: Getting your SHIM signed - The process and Its challen
 ges
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Sherif Nagy · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2589/\n\nThis talk walks through the end-to-end p
 rocess for Linux distributions to have their SHIM submitted for review and
  eventual signing by Microsoft\, surfacing the challenges faced by both di
 stribution teams and the SHIM review team.\n\nWe examine this process in d
 epth from dual vantage points: distribution maintainers navigating submiss
 ion requirements -- including SBAT entries\, CVE patching\, DBX and revoca
 tion policies -- and the SHIM review team evaluating submissions.\n\nWe al
 so address the Partner centre for Windows Hardware and what Linux distribu
 tion teams need to eventually get the SHIM signed by Microsoft.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2589/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2420@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
SUMMARY:Direct map fragmentation
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Kevin Brodsky · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2420/\n\nOn supported configurations\, the dire
 ct map is built using large\nPMD/PUD-level block mappings. This is expecte
 d to bring many of the\nbenefits of hugepages: improved TLB hit rate\, les
 s page table walking\,\nsmaller page table memory footprint.\n\nThis optim
 isation is most effective when the direct map gives access to\nthe entire 
 physical memory with uniform permissions. Unfortunately\, a\ngrowing numbe
 r of features need to remove pages from the direct map or\nmodify permissi
 ons/attributes at page granularity\, causing PMD/PUD\nblocks to be split d
 own to PTEs.\n\nAccording to results presented by Mike Rapoport at LSF/MM 
 2023 [1]\, the\nperformance impact of such fragmentation is negligible for
  data\naccesses. However\, recent measurements on x86 and arm64 suggest a 
 more\nnuanced picture\, and other factors such as power consumption should
  also\nbe considered.\n\nThis session will briefly summarise key results\,
  and then discuss\npossible ways to reduce fragmentation.\n\nQuestions for
  discussion:\n\n* Should direct map permissions be modelled as an allocati
 on property\n  in the buddy allocator\, or managed by higher-level interfa
 ces such as\n  execmem or secretmem?\n* Can x86 and arm64 use common logic
  for splitting and coalescing pages?\n\nA few interesting sources of direc
 t map fragmentation:\n\n* execmem (may use PMD-sized pools\; direct map PM
 Ds will still be split\n  if writing code)\n* secretmem\n* Confidential VM
 s including guest_memfd (encrypted/unmapped pages)\n* pkeys-based page tab
 le protection\n\nProposals to reduce fragmentation:\n\n* 2021/01 - PMD-siz
 ed pools for secretmem [2]\n* 2021/04 - Grouped vmalloc [3]\n* 2023/03 - `
 __GFP_UNMAPPED` based on dedicated cache [4]\n* 2026/02 - pkeys-protected 
 page table pools [5]\n* 2026/03 - `__GFP_UNMAPPED` based on migratype/page
 block [6]\n* 2026/06 - Collapsing blocks in secretmem [7]\n* 2026/06 - EXE
 CMEM_ROX_CACHE for arm64 with direct map PMD coalescing [8]\n\n[1] https:/
 /lwn.net/Articles/931406/\n[2] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20210121122723
 .3446-8-rppt@kernel.org/\n[3] https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20210405203711.
 1095940-1-rick.p.edgecombe@intel.com/\n[4] https://lore.kernel.org/all/202
 30308094106.227365-1-rppt@kernel.org/\n[5] https://lore.kernel.org/linux-h
 ardening/20260227175518.3728055-1-kevin.brodsky@arm.com/\n[6] https://lore
 .kernel.org/lkml/20260320-page_alloc-unmapped-v2-0-28bf1bd54f41@google.com
 /\n[7] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260603104624.36390-1-lance.yang@linux
 .dev/\n[8] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260611130144.1385343-1-abarnas@go
 ogle.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2420/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2623@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T172000
SUMMARY:VFIO PCIe TPH Userspace API & Progressive Security Policy
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Chengwen Feng · https://lpc.events/event/
 20/contributions/2623/\n\nPCIe TPH enables cache steering for high-perform
 ance P2PDMA\, RDMA and SDXI workloads. Today Linux only supports TPH opera
 tions within host kernel space\; userspace/VFIO passthrough environments l
 ack a standard interface to resolve and program steering tags.\nI’ve pos
 ted a complete patch series adding native VFIO TPH support\, aligned with 
 community incremental security policy design. The series provides unified 
 handling for CPU memory (via root port ACPI _DSM)\, dma-buf\, zero-tag and
  literal raw ST sources\, supporting cross-device P2P DMA scenarios. Three
  new VFIO_DEVICE_FEATURE ioctls cover feature opt-in\, multi-source tag re
 solution and batch ST table programming.\nCurrent upstream challenges incl
 ude:\nAutomated selftest coverage without physical TPH hardware\;\nLock gr
 anularity performance tradeoffs for batch ST programming\;\nSecurity bound
 ary definition for cross-device dma-buf TPH metadata access.\nThe core uAP
 I and security logic are stable and targeted for v7.3 merge window in late
  August. Post-merge follow-up plans include extending QEMU to emulate TPH 
 capabilities and implementing full guest passthrough support with VMM-mana
 ged _DSM emulation.\nThis session will briefly introduce the design\, then
  focus on resolving the outstanding upstream challenges and aligning on VM
  passthrough roadmap.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2623/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2594@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
SUMMARY:Emulating x86 atomics on arm64
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Gaming on Linux MC · André Almeida · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2594/\n\nAtomics can be challenging enough by themselve
 s\, and emulating them can be even worse. The lack of correctness will cra
 sh the application sooner or later\, and the lack of performance will be v
 ery noticeable.\n\nOne key difference between x86 and arm64 is how they ha
 ndle atomic operations on unaligned addresses. While on x86 such operation
 s are supported transparently\, on arm64 they raise a SIGBUS and the appli
 cation is terminated. This creates a problem when emulating x86 games on a
 rm64\, since we can’t modify their source code to avoid unaligned atomic
  operations. So\, we need a mechanism to handle such operations on top of 
 arm64.\n\nThis talk will introduce the topic and describe an implementatio
 n design\, benchmark results and how other OSes approached this issue.\n\n
 LKML link: https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20251117160841.334224-1-andrealmei
 d@igalia.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2594/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2519@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T172000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
SUMMARY:Two Years of Making PCIe Hotplug Reliable: What Landed\, What Didn'
 t\, and Where Do We Go
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Maciej Grochowski · https://lpc.events/ev
 ent/20/contributions/2519/\n\nIn 2024\, hotplug on our NVMe storage fleet 
 — NTB behind Microchip/Switchtec switches — was\nnot stable on mainlin
 e\, so we ran Keith Busch's out-of-tree bus-locking series on a private\nb
 ranch. It held. Then a new hardware generation arrived\, we needed a kerne
 l we could\nvalidate against\, and that meant mainline: 6.8\, now 6.17. Go
 ing upstream meant giving up the\nstructural work that had been keeping us
  alive\, and we started hitting problems again. This\nis the story of that
  round trip\, and where I think it should go next.\n\nThe good news is rea
 l\, so I lead with it: since Keith Busch's ALPSS 2024 talk "The Problem\nw
 ith PCI Subsystems\," the subsystem has closed most of what bit us. The ch
 ild use-after-free\nat `pci_dev_wait` is fixed (`11a1f4bc4736`\, v6.11). p
 ciehp suppresses reset- and DPC-induced\nlink events (v6.16). DPC recovery
  holds a port reference (`a1ed752bc7cb`\, v7.1).\n`reset_subordinate` is h
 otplug-safe (`8238cb69c01f`\, v7.1). DPC is decoupled from per-device\nAER
  binding for v7.3 (`97ca178c899d`)\, which resolves — properly — a lay
 ering hack I had\nbeen carrying in production. The point fixes landed.\n\n
 The structural work did not come with us\, and its absence is where the ne
 w problems live.\nHierarchy mutation still sits behind one global `pci_res
 can_remove_lock`\; Keith's per-bus\nlocking and subordinate-bus refcountin
 g are still unmerged\, two years on. Where exclusion\nbecame coordination\
 , the budgets do not match: `pci_dpc_recovered()` waits 4 s for a\nrecover
 y bounded at roughly 62 s. And a failure nobody was watching turned up —
  after a\nswitch-subtree reset\, 3 of 8 endpoints cannot place a 1 GiB BAR
  that boot places without\ncomplaint.\n\nI am not here to complain\; my cr
 ash was fixed and I will say so. I bring crash dumps\, source\nanalysis\, 
 a reproducible switch topology\, and fleet-scale test capacity — the val
 idation\nbandwidth the subsystem said it was short on — and three questi
 ons about the way forward. I\nwant to leave the room agreeing on which one
  is worth fixing next\, and who validates it.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2519/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2587@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T172500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
SUMMARY:Generic Boot Flow for Type 1 Hypervisors: UKI with systemd-stub
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Anushka Nabar · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2587/\n\nARM SystemReady defines a standard fir
 mware-to-OS boot path via UEFI\, but provides no standard mechanism for bo
 oting Type 1 hypervisors such as Gunyah or Xen. Platforms relying on Type 
 1 hypervisors to isolate security- or latency-sensitive workloads\, lack a
  standardized boot flow\, which creates fragmentation across mobile\, IoT\
 , and compute ecosystems.\n\nWe have implemented a solution for this acros
 s three areas. First\, we propose extensions to the UKI format to bundle a
  Type 1 hypervisor and its static guest VM images in a single UEFI secure-
 boot authenticated image. Second\, we discuss the required changes to syst
 emd-stub to parse the extended UKI to extract and pass configuration data 
 to the hypervisor at boot. Third\, we have prototyped support to the syste
 md-boot tooling to control and maintain this configuration.\n\nBy aligning
  with both the Xen and Gunyah communities\, the goal is to enable a vendor
 -neutral\, standards-compliant boot flow for any Type 1 hypervisor\, using
  UKI.\n\nTopics to be discussed:\n1. UKI extensions for Type 1 hypervisors
 \n2. Interface between systemd-stub and hypervisor\n3. Configuration data 
 passed to the hypervisor
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2587/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2407@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
SUMMARY:BPF\, sysctls and programmable kernel decision points
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Alan Maguire · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2407/\n\nTraditionally the kernel community have introduced new s
 ysctl tunables in places in the code where there is no "one-size-fits-all"
  answer. With BPF we have the opportunity to make such decision points pro
 grammable. A simple example of this is the kernel socket acceptq length\, 
 set via a combination of the listen() backlog and somaxconn sysctl.  While
  the traditional advice has been to set this to a very high value to allow
  for connection bursts\, doing so can lead to unacceptable connection late
 ncies for applications\, particularly when the application is doing CPU-in
 tensive processing that limits connection acceptance rate.  We only drop S
 YNs when the acceptq is full\, but in such cases it would be beneficial to
  drop earlier to allow the service to recover. Having a BPF program attach
 ed could detect such conditions and handle this case by dropping SYNs. Hav
 ing a dynamic response - potentially informed by wider system state - woul
 d be helpful here and in other cases. Wider discussion of decision points 
 like these for BPF programmability is explored.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2407/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2448@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
SUMMARY:Crossing borders between user space and kernel space: GDB/Valgrind/
 drgn close cooperation
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Alexandra Petlanova Hajkova · https://lpc.
 events/event/20/contributions/2448/\n\nWhile is GDB is primarily a  user s
 pace tool\, drgn is a kernel space one. Using them together may improve us
 er experience as drgn can help GDB to get access to various kernel structu
 res. Right now\, GDB can show what a thread is doing in user space\, drgn 
 can show what it is doing in the kernel — but the two tools do not talk 
 to each other. I would like to discuss what it would mean to cross that bo
 rder.\n\nThe crossing is technically straightforward: drgn is a Python lib
 rary\, and GDB already has a Python API\, making it natural to call drgn f
 rom GDB scripts. As a concrete demonstration\, I wrote a small GDB Python 
 command\, drgn_why_sleeping\, which retrieves the kernel stack of a sleepi
 ng thread via drgn and displays it alongside GDB's user-space backtrace 
 — showing the full kernel path where bt alone shows only a generic sysca
 ll frame.\n\nThis raises further questions: could drgn's per-frame registe
 r and local variable access allow GDB users to inspect kernel frames inter
 actively? Is a remote variant worth pursuing for kgdb setups? Could drgn h
 elp trace where in the kernel an error originates when stepping over a fai
 ling syscall? Valgrind could also benefit — since drgn does not use ptra
 ce\, it could attach to a Valgrind-instrumented process  simultaneously an
 d resolve invalid memory addresses to named kernel structures. And in the 
 other direction: could user-space tools like GDB or Valgrind offer anythin
 g useful back to drgn?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2448/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2417@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
SUMMARY:Accelerating Page Migration and Making the Migration Core Composabl
 e
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Shivank Garg\, Zi Yan · https:/
 /lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2417/\n\nAs the memory hierarchy deepen
 s at both ends\, with HBM adding a faster tier on top and CXL adding cheap
 er\, slower capacity below\, keeping hot data in the fast tier and sheddin
 g cold data downward makes page migration central to NUMA\, tiered-memory 
 and coherent CPU-GPU systems (where device memory is exposed as NUMA nodes
 ).\n\nProfiling move_pages(2) on EPYC Zen 6 shows that the folio copy domi
 nates migration (~96% of time for a 2MB THP)\, making it the primary scali
 ng bottleneck. Yet this path is largely sequential: folios are copied one 
 at a time by a single CPU\, while DMA engines\, idle cores and memory band
 width go unused.\n\nTo tap that idle hardware\, this work separates the fo
 lio content copy from the rest of migration and hands it to a pluggable mi
 grator. The batch path:\n - unmaps a batch of folios and flushes the TLB o
 nce\,\n - asks a migrator to copy the eligible folios\,\n - marks copied f
 olios so the move phase skips the per-folio copy\,\n - completes the move 
 through the existing flow [1].\n\nA faster copy is not the whole story: lo
 wer rmap overheads\, a simpler migration architecture and a composable cor
 e matter just as much. The work therefore proceeds in three directions: ac
 celerating the dominant copy phase\, batching the rmap walks and restructu
 ring the migration core so that these and other independent optimizations 
 compose instead of becoming special cases.\n\nAccelerating the copy\n-----
 ----------------\n\nThree complementary\, measured directions:\n1. dcbm - 
 a DMA-based migrator using dmaengine devices\, for bulk copy across multip
 le channels.\n2. mtcopy - multi-threaded CPU copy on idle cores\, a softwa
 re fallback where no DMA/offload engine is available [9].\n3. Bulk folio_c
 opy() for large folios: one copy over the contiguous range\, independent o
 f the batch and offload framework [2].\n\nMeasured with move_pages() on 1G
 B anonymous memory\, DRAM -> DRAM\, batch copy offload makes 2MB THP migra
 tion several times faster: ~6x with PTDMA (Zen 3\, 16 channels)\, ~3.5X wi
 th SDXI (Zen 6\, 1 channel) and ~3.8x with multi-threaded CPU copy (Zen 3\
 , mtcopy\, 8 threads).\n\nSeparately\, a related demotion effort uses non-
 temporal stores to reduce cache pollution and CXL-side read traffic [3].\n
 \nBeyond the copy: the rmap phase\n-------------------------------\n\nOnce
  the copy is offloaded\, rmap work dominates for PTE-mapped large folios (
 mTHP) because the unmap and restore walks handle each PTE separately. Batc
 hing those walks is the next lever\, and its payoff grows with folio size:
  on Zen 3\, a 1MB folio reaches ~5.6x over vanilla with DMA offload and rm
 ap batching\, compared to ~2x with offload alone [7].\n\nReworking the cor
 e so these compose\n-------------------------------------\n\nSeveral effor
 ts optimize different migration phases\, and each one adds flags or specia
 l cases to the current monolithic path [1][3][4]. They also run into the s
 ame limitation: ->migrate_folio() receives only enum migrate_mode\, which 
 describes the blocking semantics but not the migration intent (for example
 \, promotion versus demotion). So\, new behavior ends up either extending 
 migrate_mode or going through a side channel. For example\, non-temporal d
 emotion added MIGRATE_ASYNC_NON_TEMPORAL_STORES\, while the DMA offload re
 cords FOLIO_CONTENT_COPIED in migrate_info.\n\nTwo changes are posted as R
 FC[8]:\n\n1. Pass a small context struct (migrate_control) through migrate
 _pages() into ->migrate_folio()\, carrying mode\, reason\, and room for fu
 ture attributes. This separates blocking behavior from intent\, so the cop
 y phase knows why the folio is migrating and copy policy (cached\, non-tem
 poral\, offload\, and so on) can follow.\n\n2. Split the single list in mi
 grate_pages() into separate classes\, each with its own list and retries: 
 hugetlb folios\, movable_ops pages and LRU folios. This removes the type c
 hecks scattered through the shared batch path and makes the flow easier to
  follow.\n\nDiscussion\n----------\n\nI'd like to use the session to valid
 ate the direction of the migration core work and how ongoing efforts shoul
 d fit together.\n\nKey questions:\n\n- Interface: should migrate_pages() a
 nd ->migrate_folio() take a context struct? It touches every caller and ca
 llback. Is that one-time churn acceptable\, and is this the right shape?\n
 - Policy: Should each policy\, such as cache intent\, be stated explicitly
  by the caller\, or should the core derive it from the migration reason?\n
 - Core structure: is the per-class migration model right shape for maintai
 nability? movable_ops pages still use page->lru\, new_folio_t\, put_folio_
 t. What should a non-folio migration interface look like?\n- Synchronous f
 allback: after the async pass\, should the remaining folios be retried thr
 ough a direct single-folio path\, or should they keep reusing migrate_page
 s_batch() and avoid a second single-folio path?\n- Copy helpers: there is 
 no range version of copy_page() for bulk folio copy. memcpy() works\, but 
 without FSRM on x86 it falls back to an unrolled movq loop instead of copy
 _page()'s rep movsq. Should we add copy_pages()\, like clear_pages()?\n- E
 ngine selection: who should select the copy engine: the caller\, the provi
 der (as in the current RFC)\, or the core\, based on caller constraints an
 d provider capability? Should folio size\, batch size or topology drive th
 at choice?\n- Batching trade-offs: What is an acceptable trade-off between
  throughput and first-folio latency? Should the batch limit be a tunable p
 er platform?\n\nRoadmap:\n\nMigration core redesign and context passing\; 
 migration-side rmap batching\, bulk copy within large folios\, batch copy 
 and DMA offload\, Scatter-gather copy (DMA_MEMCPY_SG)\; SDXI migrator [6] 
 and DCBM driver\; topology-aware thread placement for multi-threaded copy 
 and CPU accounting for multi-threaded copy\; hot page promotion (pghot [5]
 ) reusing the offload path.\n\nReferences\n-----------\n[1] Migration batc
 h/offload\, RFC V6: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260630-shivank-batch-mig
 rate-offload-v6-0-da95d7e8b8a2@amd.com\n[2] Bulk folio_copy optimization\,
  RFC v1: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260427142036.111940-4-shivankg@amd.
 com\n[3] Non-temporal demotion\, RFC V2: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/
 20260730-rfc-nt-demote-v2-0-452dbe3b5073@zptcorp.com/#t\n[4] migrate_mode/
 migrate_folio() ABI extensibility (Memory Hotness and Promotion call):\n  
   https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/37010218-ecad-4f75-961d-686d51b6677d@am
 d.com\n[5] pghot\, V8: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260728054356.291998-1
 -bharata@amd.com\n[6] SDXI\, V3: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260605-sdxi
 -base-v3-0-4d38ca2bdffe@amd.com\n[7] Migration-side rmap batching\, RFC V2
 : https://lore.kernel.org/linux-mm/20260813-migrate-rmap-batch-v2-0-3c5424
 c555c7@amd.com\n[8] Migration redesign and policy\, RFC V1: https://lore.k
 ernel.org/linux-mm/20260902-migrate-refactor-shivank-v1-0-9dcca87669c4@amd
 .com\n[9] mtcopy RFC v3: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20250923174752.35701-
 7-shivankg@amd.com
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2417/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2402@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
SUMMARY:Beyond Coverage: Per-Task Function Boundary Extraction for Kernel C
 ontract Verification
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Toolchains Track · Yunseong Kim · https://lpc.events/event/20
 /contributions/2402/\n\nKCOV's trace-pc provides edge coverage feedback fo
 r kernel fuzzers\, but captures no data-flow context. Two syscalls hitting
  identical basic blocks with different argument values (e.g.\, vfs_open wi
 th O_RDONLY vs O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC) are indistinguishable to the fuzzer. This
  "semantic gap" causes coverage saturation on value-dependent state transi
 tions in complex subsystems (binder\, io_uring\, ksmbd).\n\nftrace functio
 n graph tracer support arg/ret values tracing during runtime. But their is
  a couple of challenge(FTRACE_REGS_MAX_ARGS 6) regarding architecture call
 ing convention from register\, and currently can't support structure and t
 heir members on the function.\n\nOn the other hands\, Rust For Linux kerne
 l modules can't supported by current tracing framework.\n\nWe need consens
 us on extending LLVM's SanitizerCoverage to capture function arguments and
  return values\, including automatic struct field decomposition from DWARF
  metadata\, with a kernel-resident per-task ring buffer.\n\n# What I Imple
 mented\n\nTwo new SanitizerCoverage modes in LLVM:\n\n## Emits LLVM RFC su
 bmitted\n1. `__sanitizer_cov_trace_args(arg_idx\, arg_size\, val\, offsets
 \, num_fields)` at function entry for each parameter.\n\n2. `__sanitizer_c
 ov_trace_ret(ret_size\, val\, offsets\, num_fields)` before each return.\n
 \nThe pass uses DICompositeType metadata to extract struct field offsets/s
 izes at compile time and emits them as global constant arrays. At runtime\
 , the kernel backend reads fields via `copy_from_kernel_nofault()` into a 
 per-task lock-free mmap'd buffer at `/sys/kernel/debug/kcov_dataflow`.\n\n
 A native rustc path (rustc built against the custom LLVM) enables Rust ker
 nel module instrumentation without a post-compilation pipeline. The only k
 nown method for capturing Rust function arguments at runtime given -O2 DWA
 RF elision.\n\n# Discussion Topics Needing Agreement\n\n1. **Callback ABI 
 stability**\nShould `__sanitizer_cov_trace_args/ret` be considered stable 
 SanitizerCoverage API\, or experimental? What's the path from `-fsanitize-
 coverage=trace-args` to an accepted upstream flag?\n\n2. **DWARF metadata 
 dependency**\nThe pass requires `-g` for struct expansion. Currently it gr
 acefully degrades: without `-g`\, `F.getSubprogram()` returns null and the
  function is silently skipped. With `-g` but missing type info for a param
 \, it records as scalar (offsets=NULL\, num_fields=0). Should this behavio
 r be documented as the contract?\n\n3. **Interaction with optimizations**\
 nAt -O2\, scalar arguments are spilled to `alloca` so the kernel callback 
 receives a uniform pointer. Should the pass mark these as side-effecting t
 o prevent DSE\, or accept the current behavior (compiler preserves them be
 cause the call itself is a side effect)?\n\n4. **Rust integration path**\n
 Currently requires building rustc against the custom LLVM (native path) or
  using a wrapper (rustc emit-llvm-ir then opt). Is there appetite for nati
 ve `-Zsanitizer-coverage=trace-args` support in upstream rustc? The kernel
 's Kbuild does not expose intermediate IR for external instrumentation\, m
 aking the native path mandatory for Rust kernel modules.\n\n5. **Kernel-si
 de callback design**\nThe kernel backend uses `copy_from_kernel_nofault()`
  for safe pointer reads and a bit-31 recursion guard (needed because `copy
 _from_kernel_nofault` itself is instrumented under INSTRUMENT_ALL). Should
  the LLVM pass remain fully target-agnostic\, or emit hints for the runtim
 e?\n\n6. **Scope control**\nPer-module opt-in (`KCOV_DATAFLOW_file.o := y`
 ) vs. global (`CONFIG_KCOV_DATAFLOW_INSTRUMENT_ALL`). The pass currently i
 nstruments all functions with DISubprogram. Should it support function-lev
 el filtering via attributes (e.g.\, `__attribute__((no_sanitize("dataflow"
 )))`)?\n\n7. **Performance implications**\nWith INSTRUMENT_ALL: +9.5% .tex
 t\, +133% syscall latency. Per-module: +8.3% on instrumented paths. The pe
 r-callback cost is ~27ns dominated by `copy_from_kernel_nofault`. Is this 
 acceptable for the SanitizerCoverage framework\, or should it be a separat
 e pass?\n\n# Why Rust Kernel Modules Need This\n\nExisting observability t
 ools completely fail on Rust kernel code:\n\n- **ftrace**: Cannot hook Rus
 t functions (no `__fentry__` prologue — rustc doesn't emit `-mfentry`)\n
 - **kprobes/fprobe**: Rust symbol mangling makes targeting impractical\; e
 ven with correct names\, only captures raw register values (no struct expa
 nsion)\n- **drgn/vmcore**: `rustc -O2` elides `DW_AT_location` for all par
 ameters — `frame.locals()` returns `{}`\n- **eBPF**: Requires manual str
 uct layout specification\; Rust `#[repr(Rust)]` types have no stable ABI\n
 \nOur LLVM pass operates on Rust-generated IR *before* codegen — the onl
 y point where both debug metadata and argument values coexist.\n\nVerified
 : Rust eight_struct_args_rust selftest produces correct output at -O2 on C
 I result: https://github.com/yskzalloc/kcov-dataflow/actions/runs/29659128
 011/job/88119068007\n```\ndo_el0_svc({0x4\, 0x4\, 0xffffffff\, 0x0\, 0x0\,
  0x0})\n0x0 = B...\nsb_start_write()\nfile_start_write()\n0x0 = _RNvCsdfZG
 IOKgjaD_22eight_struct_args_rust13write_handler() [eight_struct_args_rust]
 \n  0x11 = rsf_1(0x11) [eight_struct_args_rust]\n  0x33 = rsf_2(0x11\, {0x
 11\, 0x22}) [eight_struct_args_rust]\n  ...\n  0x30c = rnsf_8({0xa8\, 0x11
 \, 0x11\, 0x11\, 0x11\, 0x11\, 0x11\, 0x11\, 0x11}) [eight_struct_args_rus
 t]\n    0xaa = rsf_fwd_inner(0x11\, {0x11\, 0x22}\, {0x11\, 0x22\, 0x33}\,
  {0x11\, 0x22\, 0x33\, 0x44}) [eight_struct_args_rust]\n  0xaa = rsf_fwd(0
 x11\, {0x11\, 0x22}\, {0x11\, 0x22\, 0x33}\, {0x11\, 0x22\, 0x33\, 0x44}) 
 [eight_struct_args_rust]\n  rsf_ret_struct(0x0\, {0x11\, 0x11}\, 0x11) [ei
 ght_struct_args_rust]\n  0xfffffdffc0356e80 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_al
 ign(0x0\, 0x8\, 0x8\, 0xcc0\, 0xffffffff)\n  0x6f00000009 = rust_helper_kr
 ealloc_node_align(0x0\, 0x10\, 0x8\, 0xcc0\, 0xffffffff)\n  0xf200006e0000
 0011 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x0\, 0x18\, 0x8\, 0xcc0\, 0xffffff
 ff)\n  0x2500006e00000011 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x0\, 0x20\, 0
 x8\, 0xcc0\, 0xffffffff)\n  0xaa = rsf_heap(0x11\, {0x11\, 0x22}\, {0x11\,
  0x22\, 0x33}\, {0x11\, 0x22\, 0x33\, 0x44}) [eight_struct_args_rust]\n  0
 x0 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x11\, 0x0\, 0x1\, 0x0\, 0xffffffff)\
 n  0x0 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x11\, 0x0\, 0x1\, 0x0\, 0xffffff
 ff)\n  0x0 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x11\, 0x0\, 0x1\, 0x0\, 0xff
 ffffff)\n  0x0 = rust_helper_krealloc_node_align(0x11\, 0x0\, 0x1\, 0x0\, 
 0xffffffff)\n0x1 = _RNvCsdfZGIOKgjaD_22eight_struct_args_rust13write_handl
 er(0xffffa64d1231\, 0x1\, 0x0) [eight_struct_args_rust]\n...\n```\n\n# Imp
 lementation vs. ftrace funcgraph-args\n\nftrace's funcgraph-args (availabl
 e since 6.x) captures 6 raw register values but:\n- Requires `-mfentry` (n
 o Rust support)\n- No struct field expansion (just pointer addresses)\n- S
 hared per-CPU ring buffer with preempt_disable per event\n- Under load: ev
 ents silently overwritten/dropped\n- Stack-passed args (> 6) not captured\
 n\nkcov-dataflow:\n- Per-task private mmap buffer (no contention\, no pree
 mpt_disable)\n- Automatic struct field expansion from DWARF\n- All args in
 cluding stack-passed (alloca'd at compile time)\n- Works on both C and Rus
 t identically\n- Zero-copy consumer via mmap\n\n# Current Status\n\n- Repo
 sitory (kernel + LLVM + rustc + selftests + CI):\n  https://github.com/ysk
 zalloc/kcov-dataflow\n- CI (x86_64 + arm64\, 6 selftests):\n  https://gith
 ub.com/yskzalloc/kcov-dataflow/actions\n- Tested on linux-next 7.2.0-rc3 w
 ith custom clang/LLVM 23 and rustc 1.99-nightly\n- All selftests pass on b
 oth x86_64 (KVM) and arm64 (native) Github CI\n\n# Why This Needs Face-to-
 Face Discussion\n\n- This work crosses 3 communities (LLVM\, kernel\, Rust
 -for-Linux) that rarely meet simultaneously.\n- The callback ABI\, optimiz
 ation interaction\, and Rust pipeline decisions require input from all 3.\
 n- LPC Toolchains Track is the only venue where clang/LLVM developers\, ke
 rnel maintainers\, and Rust-for-Linux contributors are in the same room.\n
 \n# References\n\n- LLVM RFC: https://discourse.llvm.org/t/rfc-sanitizerco
 verage-add-fsanitize-coverage-trace-args-trace-ret/91026\n- LLVM PR: https
 ://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/201410\n- Kernel patch v3: https://lo
 re.kernel.org/all/20260902-b4-kcov-dataflow-rfc-v3-v3-0-824609f279f5@est.t
 ech/\n- arXiv paper: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.00455\n- LWN: https://lwn.
 net/Articles/1092429/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2402/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2355@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:20 years of pahole : alive and kicking!
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Alan Maguire\, Arnaldo Carvalho de Melo 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2355/\n\nThe first cset for p
 ahole is from October 24\, 2006\, a long time ago\, from the first goals o
 f helping reorganize the Linux kernel networking data structures\, that wa
 s done beyond my expectations\, helping countless open source projects to 
 view its data structures with great precision and flexibility\, to becomin
 g a swiss army knife tool to convert type information from DWARF to CTF an
 d\, crucially\, to BTF\, becoming part of the kernel build process to enab
 le BPF CO-RE\, it has kept earning its keep.\n\nRecent advances in DWARF t
 ag and language support\, plus features that exist but aren't well publici
 zed\, are subjects I want to communicate.\n\nCoverage analysis\, a quickly
  growing set of regression tests\, support for DWARF tags for C++ and Rust
  concepts\, support for DWZ\, partial units\, supporting modern DWARF pres
 ent in distro userlands are topics of recent improvement that\, by the 20t
 h anniversary\, will surely be ready to talk about.\n\nIntegration with pe
 rf\, namely in having pahole and perf work together in areas such as data-
 type profiling has been a perennial source of requests that\, by now\, wit
 h some help from new and controversial friends\, should finally become a r
 eality.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2355/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2590@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180500
SUMMARY:Booting Android with Linux EFI Boot Stub
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Yi-Yo Chiang · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2590/\n\nAndroid is evaluating the Linux EFI boo
 t stub to standardize OS handoff and reduce bootloader feature duplication
 .\nWe present blockers encountered with embedded hardware constraints\, an
 d aim to discuss potential solutions.\n\nTopics for Discussion:\n\n* EL2 E
 levation and KVM within an EFI context\n    * **The Problem:** KVM require
 s booting the kernel at EL2. Since Android firmware operates at EL1\, it m
 ust elevate to EL2 before kernel execution.\n    * **Discussion:** What ar
 e the options to elevate to EL2 before jumping to the kernel?\n        * O
 verride UEFI `ExitBootServices` to hijack the boot flow?\n        * Instal
 l a custom UEFI protocol callback that executes EL switch and takes over k
 ernel jump?\n\n* Physical Image Placement and PE/COFF Relocation\n    * **
 The Problem:** The UEFI PE/COFF loader copies and relocates the kernel ima
 ge to a dynamically allocated buffer address.\n    * **The Constraint:**\n
         * *Boot Latency:* Copying large kernel payloads increases boot lat
 ency.\n        * *Defeat Architectural Expectations:* Hardware platforms m
 ight require the kernel image loaded at specific address ranges. The PE lo
 ader moving the kernel image around defeats this.\n    * **Discussion:** H
 ow do we eliminate unnecessary allocations and respect physical image plac
 ement done by the firmware/bootloader.\n\nContact us: <android-gbl@google.
 com>
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2590/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2368@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:Updates and Future Directions for Resctrl RDT/MPAM/PQOS/CBQRI
LOCATION:Club B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Fenghua Yu\, Ben Horgan\, Reinette 
 Chatre · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2368/\n\nSignificant p
 rogress has been made on the resctrl subsystem recently\, driven by archit
 ectural evolutions across multiple hardware vendors. Key developments incl
 ude Intel's separate control and monitor domains\, ARM's IOMMU\, NVIDIA's 
 CPU-less MPAM support and MBW MAX hard limits\, AMD's monitor counter assi
 gnment\, and initial RISC-V inclusion.\n\nFollowing last year's highly pro
 ductive BoF\, which successfully guided upstream development over the past
  several months\, this session will bring the broader resctrl ecosystem to
 gether again—including maintainers and developers from Intel\, ARM\, NVI
 DIA\, AMD\, RISC-V\, Google\, Fujitsu\, and Alibaba. While we will briefly
  review recent milestones\, the primary focus of this session will be face
 -to-face discussion to address current architectural bottlenecks\, maintai
 n a clean cross-architecture abstraction layer\, and reach consensus on fu
 ture upstream design paths.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2368/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2508@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T181000
SUMMARY:Describing P2PDMA capabilities in ACPI
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Leon Romanovsky\, Lukas Wunner · https://
 lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2508/\n\nThe kernel uses a [whitelist][1
 ] do determine whether peer-to-peer DMA is supported between devices. But 
 constantly amending the whitelist is a maintenance burden.\n\nWe are propo
 sing to extend the ACPI HMAT table with bandwidth and latency characterist
 ics for P2PDMA traffic between PCI host bridges (and between devices below
  the same host bridge).\n\nThis will remove the need for a whitelist.\n\nI
 t also closes a gap in virtualization: VMs have no visibility of the physi
 cal PCI topology and thus cannot determine P2PDMA reachability. The propos
 ed HMAT extension solves this: VMMs such as qemu may present a synthesized
  HMAT table to VMs which reflects the physical capabilities. This replaces
  prior proposals such as Nvidia's [Virtual Peer-to-Peer Approval Capabilit
 y][2].\n\nLast not least\, the proposed HMAT extension allows users to pop
 ulate PCI slots for optimal P2PDMA performance.\n\nWe will briefly present
  the draft of this ACPI Code First ECN and explain how it fits into the Li
 nux P2PDMA model and path-selection algorithm. Afterwards we hope for a li
 vely discussion on its merits.\n\n  [1]: https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/
 v7.2-rc1/source/drivers/pci/p2pdma.c#L535\n  [2]: https://lists.gnu.org/ar
 chive/html/qemu-devel/2017-08/pdfUda5iEpgOS.pdf
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2508/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2444@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:eBPF Security: The Uneven Landscape
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Gürkan Gür\, Louie Wolf · https://lpc.events/e
 vent/20/contributions/2444/\n\nAlthough eBPF is widely used to extend the 
 Linux kernel\, running programs in kernel space introduces critical securi
 ty risks. Existing technical work often overlooks eBPF's entire security l
 ifecycle. This talk aims to address this gap by systematically analyzing e
 BPF vulnerabilities\, mitigations\, and architectural limits. By reviewing
  research papers and CVEs\, we will map real-world exploits to specific co
 mponents\, revealing that current defenses target isolated attacks rather 
 than systemic architectural risks. Finally\, we will present key takeaways
 \, outline open directions toward lifecycle-aware\, composition-safe secur
 ity models\, and highlight understudied non-verifier components.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2444/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2418@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:Tackling Isolation Issues Due to Memory Pressure
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Memory Management MC · Shakeel Butt · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2418/\n\nOn a densely shared host\, a routine ev
 ent breaks tenant isolation: a task holding a shared kernel lock (filesyst
 em metadata\, a control-plane mutex/rwsem) allocates *inside* the critical
  section\, the allocation falls into memory reclaim\, and until reclaim fi
 nishes every other task waiting on that lock — from any cgroup — pays 
 the stall. Shared kernel locks aren't mediated by cgroups or memcg limits\
 , so this is cross-tenant priority inversion\, and overcommit makes it wor
 se.\n\nIn this talk we will present the data we have gathered across the M
 eta fleet on this problem\, and share how we think the solution should tak
 e shape. Rather than a finished design or patch series\, the goal is to la
 y out the problem space\, the constraints any fix must satisfy\, our curre
 nt direction\, and the open questions and to get early feedback from the u
 pstream community.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2418/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2577@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T181000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:Arm DRTM beyond the reference model: dynamic launch on real platfor
 ms and Linux
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:System Boot and Security MC · Piotr Król · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2577/\n\nArm's DRTM architecture (DEN 0113) defin
 es a dynamic root of trust for measurement: a DCE-Preamble triggers a dyna
 mic launch event\, EL3 firmware (TF-A) measures a protected payload into a
  fresh chain of trust\, and the dynamically launched measured environment 
 (DLME) continues under a smaller TCB. Yet a working implementation exists 
 only against the Base AEM FVP TF-A's plat_drtm hooks (SMMU-based DMA prote
 ction\, the DRTM address map\, and measurement) live solely under the fvp 
 board\, and the Neoverse reference-design platforms that model real server
  silicon ship no DRTM support at all.\n\nThis session looks at what it tak
 es to move Arm DRTM off the reference model and make it useful to Linux: p
 orting the plat_drtm platform layer onto the Neoverse reference-design por
 ts\; delivering the DCE-Preamble as a UEFI/EDK2 application rather than a 
 bootloader patch\; measuring into PCR 17/18 with a DRTM event log\; and th
 e kernel-side plumbing to consume and attest the launch. Drawing on 3mdeb'
 s TrenchBoot work and years in the Arm DRTM ecosystem\, we want to map the
  upstreaming path across TF-A\, EDK2\, and the kernel\, and name the gaps 
 and their owners.\n\nDesired outcome: consensus on a minimal\, reproducibl
 e Arm DRTM reference that the community can build on FVP-first\, then real
  silicon\, and a shared list of the upstream changes needed to get there.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2577/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2384@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T181000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T183000
SUMMARY:Testing the Untestable: Using QEMU for Testing PCI Endpoint Framewo
 rk
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:VFIO/IOMMU/PCI MC · Manivannan Sadhasivam · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2384/\n\nThe Linux PCI Endpoint Framework provide
 s the software infrastructure for a Linux system to present itself as a PC
 Ie Endpoint device to an external Host. It consists of an Endpoint Control
 ler layer that abstracts the hardware\, an Endpoint Function layer that im
 plements device behavior\, and a ConfigFS interface for userspace to bind 
 them together. For testing\, the upstream pci_endpoint kselftests are used
 . But running this kselftest has always required two physical machines con
 nected by a physical PCIe link. This makes automated testing impractical a
 nd limits coverage to whoever has the right hardware.\n\n  This talk prese
 nts a software only test setup that removes the hardware requirement entir
 ely. Two QEMU instances communicate over a Unix socket\, one acting as the
  Endpoint Controller and the other as the Root Complex. A new generic EPC 
 driver on the Endpoint side talks to a virtual Endpoint Controller device 
 emulated by QEMU\, which forwards commands over the socket to the RC side 
 where QEMU emulates a PCIe Endpoint Function that the Host kernel enumerat
 es and tests against. The existing kselftests run unmodified against this 
 setup.\n\nThis talk will walk through how the two VM design works\, what t
 rade-offs were made in the socket protocol and what remains to be done for
  full CI integration.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2384/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-evening-20261005@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T193000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261005T223000
SUMMARY:Evening Event
LOCATION:TBA\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:https://lpc.events/event/20/timetable/#20261005.detailed
