END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2427@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T103000
SUMMARY:eBPF Research: What's Going On In Academia?
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:eBPF Track · Paul Chaignon · https://lpc.events/event/20/cont
 ributions/2427/\n\nThe body of academic work on eBPF is growing so large a
 nd scattered that it’s hard to see the forest for the trees. Papers span
  all kinds of topics and conferences\, vary wildly in quality\, and are of
 ten dense and hard to parse.\n\nThis talk will present the dominant trends
  of research. To that end\, we will first explain some heuristics to ident
 ify "high-quality papers"—it is not the number of citations!—and what 
 it means for a paper to be considered high-quality. We’ll then map these
  standout papers to some of the teams behind them.\n\nFinally\, we will se
 e that only specific research topics are leading to contributions upstream
  and explain why that might be. We will discuss how the kernel community c
 ould encourage research on specific problems\, should it choose to do so.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2427/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2373@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
SUMMARY:Kernel CVEs at AWS Scale: Two Years of Empirical Findings
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Kernel Summit Track · Dylan Johnson\, Justinien Bouron · http
 s://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2373/\n\nSince the Linux kernel proj
 ect became a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) in February 2024\, organization
 s maintaining custom kernels have faced a flood of CVE disclosures.\n\nWe 
 present empirical findings\, lessons learned\, and the key challenges that
  the kernel team responsible for Amazon Web Services fleet infrastructure 
 encountered over the following two years while handling the steady flow of
  kernel CVEs.\n\nWe summarize our approach to assessing kernel CVEs in a w
 arehouse-scale environment\, pruning our code base to reduce the attack su
 rface\, along with quantitative results from field data. Specifically\, we
  examine\n\n - how NVD/CVSS scores and third-party assessments correlate w
 ith our final in-house ratings\;\n - how community guidance\, including st
 able tree tracking and domain-specific risk assessment\, measurably improv
 ed our development velocity\, kernel maintenance\, and kernel adoption str
 ategy\; and\n - how backporting practices introduce regressions and compou
 nd risk.\n\nWe then present data on patching velocity and CVE-related tren
 ds across LTS versions. Although our data comes from a single environment\
 , we believe these findings generalize to any organization that maintains 
 in-house or custom kernels.\n\nWe will close with an open discussion on em
 erging developments (including where AI-assisted tooling fits into the CVE
  triage and patching pipeline) and invite attendees to challenge\, extend\
 , or contradict our findings with their own data.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2373/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2348@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
SUMMARY:RCU Pseudo-Transactions: Bridging the gap between RCU and STM
LOCATION:Small Hall\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Mathieu Desnoyers · https://lpc.events/e
 vent/20/contributions/2348/\n\nRCU data structures are notoriously complex
  to design mainly due to the\nneed to carefully manage how mutations are m
 ade observable to concurrent\nreaders.\n\nAs a general solution to this pr
 oblem\, I am proposing a novel\ntransaction-based synchronisation mechanis
 m: "RCU Pseudo-Transactions"\n(rcu_txn).\n\nIt applies both to userspace a
 nd kernel. It allows publishing complex\ndata structure mutations atomical
 ly to RCU readers\, and synchronizing\nupdates from multiple writers\, wit
 h minimal overhead on the read-side\n(low-bit pointer tag check\, predicte
 d branch on rcu_dereference)\, and no\nsize overhead on the data structure
  nodes. It is composable: an object\ncan belong to multiple transaction-aw
 are data structures\, and\ntransactions allow it to become visible (or hid
 den) atomically.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2348/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2514@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102000
SUMMARY:Probe events with typecast using BTF
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Masami Hiramatsu · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2514/\n\nBTF fetcharg has been introduced since v6.5 for fpro
 be and kprobe events for fetching function parameters by name\, and now we
  intrdouced typecast feature for BTF. This typecast feature is not only ca
 sting type\, but also\, the series supports nested typecasts\, container_o
 f()\, this_cpu_ptr()\, and "current" task structure access. With these fea
 tures\, we can access more context local data from dynamic trace events.\n
 I  would like to show how you can use these features and discuss what feat
 ures we can provide with BTF\, what limitations we have now etc.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2514/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2388@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102000
SUMMARY:DT bindings and sources shared with U-Boot: current status and ongo
 ing work
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Devicetree MC · Neil Armstrong · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2388/\n\nSince early 2024\, U-Boot has been synchronizing th
 e DTS and bindings of each Linux release into the U-Boot source tree using
  git subtree functionality. Since this transition\, some platforms like Am
 logic or Qualcomm have immediately switched\, and some older platforms hav
 e been actively migrating to upstream Linux DT sources and bindings while 
 maintaining minimal local U-Boot DTS modifications.\n\nNeil will summarize
  the current state of U-Boot's integration of these upstream DTS\, provide
  an update on ongoing migrations\, and outline the remaining work required
  to fully eliminate local U-Boot DTS modifications.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2388/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2409@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
SUMMARY:Kernel Sanitizers Office Hours
LOCATION:Club B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Aleksandr Nogikh\, Alexander Potape
 nko\, Dmitry Vyukov\, Justin Stitt\, Kees Cook\, Marco Elver\, Pimyn Girgi
 s · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2409/\n\nThe Linux kernel h
 as numerous tools to detect bugs\, among them a family of dynamic program 
 analysis called "sanitizers": Kernel Address Sanitizer (KASAN)\, Kernel Me
 mory Sanitizer (KMSAN)\, Kernel Concurrency Sanitizer (KCSAN)\, and the Un
 defined Behaviour Sanitizer (UBSAN). Although not a bug-detecting sanitize
 r itself\, we will also cover Kernel Coverage (KCOV)\, which utilizes simi
 lar compiler instrumentation to collect and expose execution feedback.\n\n
 Knowing when to apply which sanitizer in the kernel development process ma
 y not always be obvious: each sanitizer is dedicated to finding a differen
 t class of bugs\, and each introduces some amount of performance and/or me
 mory overhead. Not only that\, each sanitizer also provides a range of opt
 ions to tweak their abilities.\n\nThis session is dedicated to briefly int
 roducing each kernel sanitizer\, the bug classes they help detect\, and im
 portant gotchas when using them.\n\nThe rest of the session is dedicated t
 o answering questions around each of the sanitizers\, KASAN\, KMSAN\, KCSA
 N\, and UBSAN. Feel free to also share success stories that may give other
  attendees only starting out with some of the sanitizers ideas how to best
  apply them.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2409/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2552@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T103000
SUMMARY:mmu_notifier: can we use it better?
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:KVM MC · Claudio Imbrenda · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2552/\n\nKVM uses the mmu_notifier infrastructure to keep the gue
 st mapping up to date. After having completely rewritten the memory manage
 ment of KVM/s390 to use mmu_notifiers\, I have noticed some of the shortco
 mings in how KVM uses the notifiers.\n\nSome of areas where KVM's usage of
  the notifiers\, or the mmu_notifier infrastructure itself can be improved
 :\n\n - passing the reason code to the KVM callback when unmapping\n - mor
 e fine-grained reason codes\n - passing back flags from the notifier callb
 ack\n\nI will show how and why each of the above points would be useful fo
 r KVM with some concrete examples.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2552/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2645@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104500
SUMMARY:Sashiko current status
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:AI-Assisted Open Source Development MC · Roman Gushchin · htt
 ps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2645/\n\nCurrent status of Sashiko.
  Also in this block: #140 "Citation Needed: Treating the kernel's AI revie
 w context like code" (Fuad Tabba)\, and Chris Mason and others on review p
 rompts.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2645/
