BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2457@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T153000
SUMMARY:Modernizing NFS Direct I/O for PCI Peer-to-Peer DMA
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Networking Track · Pranjal Shrivastava\, Shivaji Kant · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2457/\n\nHigh-performance storage env
 ironments increasingly rely on direct data movement between PCIe  endpoint
 s\, such as NVMe Controller Memory Buffers (CMB) or BAR memory. Today\, NF
 S relies on standard page references (get_page) and doesn't support pinnin
 g pages for DMA\, making the subsystem  pin-unaware. Furthermore\, the leg
 acy iterator APIs used in NFS are incapable of passing the required capabi
 lity flags mandated by the GUP subsystem. Thus\, NFS remains incompatible 
 with P2PDMA due to this lack of pin-awareness.\n\n This talk walks through
  the design challenges in enabling P2PDMA for the NFS Direct I/O path usin
 g a two-phased approach. The first phase involves an architectural moderni
 zation\, i.e. migrating the Direct I/O path to use the modern iov_iter_ext
 ract_pages API. This transition introduces pin-awareness to the NFS reques
 t lifecycle. Additionally\, it migrates the NFS Direct I/O from pages to f
 olios. We have an on-going series posted upstream for the first phase:\n[h
 ttps://lore.kernel.org/all/20260603053033.3300318-1-praan@google.com][1]\n
 \n**Plumber's Discussion: Transport-Level P2PDMA Capability Detection**\nT
 he second phase of this modernization addresses the detection and propagat
 ion of P2PDMA capabilities based on the underlying transport. Since NFS co
 nnections can dynamically migrate across different hardware interfaces or 
 utilize multipathing (nconnect)\, P2PDMA support is a property of the acti
 ve connection rather than a static server capability. This session focuses
  on the following architectural challenges:\n\n - **Detection & Propagatio
 n:** Discuss & align on the proposed design for transport-level capability
  discovery. This would allow NFS to conditionally signal hardware P2PDMA s
 upport (via `ITER_ALLOW_P2PDMA`) to the GUP subsystem\, potentially on a p
 er-request basis.\n\n - **Architectural Implications:** Discuss the cross-
 subsystem impact of this transport-centric approach. Key areas include man
 aging state consistency across transport failover\, the implications of dy
 namic device rebinding during re-connections\, and the challenge of transp
 ort selection in multipath (nconnect) environments.\n\nThe talk is backed 
 by the ongoing NFS Modernization and P2PDMA series upstream:\n- RFC: [http
 s://lore.kernel.org/all/20260401194501.2269200-1-praan@google.com/][2]\n- 
 On-going Series: [https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260616134000.2733403-1-pra
 an@google.com/][1]\n\n  [1]: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260616134000.27
 33403-1-praan@google.com/\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/all/202604011945
 01.2269200-1-praan@google.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2457/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2354@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154500
SUMMARY:When Embedded Codecs Meet Graphics
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:LPC Refereed Track · Nicolas Dufresne · https://lpc.events/ev
 ent/20/contributions/2354/\n\nIn the current Linux kernel\, video codecs s
 plit into two categories. Accelerators built into GPUs are implemented as 
 thin drivers with userspace components exposing APIs such as Vulkan Video 
 or VA-API. Everything else falls under the Video4Linux kernel API. This fr
 agmentation adds complexity for both kernel and userspace developers. VA-A
 PI has stagnated and carries historical assumptions tied to Intel hardware
 \, while Video4Linux\, despite remaining relevant and widely deployed\, ha
 s an aging memory model and resource queue mechanism that limits modern us
 e cases. Vulkan Video\, by contrast\, is actively developed and designed w
 ith hardware-specific extensions in mind\, making it a strong candidate fo
 r embedded use cases.\n\nThis talk proposes introducing a new class of emb
 edded video codec drivers into Linux: thin\, vendor-specific drivers model
 ed after GPU drivers\, where the V4L2 kernel API is replaced by Vulkan Vid
 eo\, with the userspace driver implemented in Mesa. The goal is not to rei
 nvent the wheel\, but to replace an aging one. Such an approach would brin
 g better buffer management through existing DRM memory helpers\, modern ex
 plicit synchronization primitives already used by GPU drivers\, and reduce
 d kernel complexity by pushing codec-specific logic into userspace where i
 t belongs.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2354/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602a1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152000
SUMMARY:Backward Compactiliblity of Kernel Livepatching API
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · Petr Mladek · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2530/\n\nKernel livepatches are kernel modules which are abl
 e to modify the kernel behavior by redirecting kernel functions\, calling 
 pre/post patch callbacks\, and allocating shadow variables.\n\nThe interfa
 ce between the kernel and the kernel livepatch module is defined in `inclu
 de/linux/livepatch.h`.\n\nThe API has evolved over the years. But it has s
 tayed backward compatible since the commit 958ef1e39d24d6cb8bf ("livepatch
 : Simplify API by removing registration step") which was added in v5.1-rc1
  back in Jan 2019. This change was part of a patchset adding an **atomic r
 eplace** feature.\n\nThere are two patchsets which would want to break the
  backward compatibility again:\n\n - [Better integrate callbacks\, shadow 
 variables\, and states APIs][1]\n - [Replace set support][2]\n\nIt has bee
 n acceptable to break the API back in 2019. But the kernel livepatching ha
 s been a pretty new feature and had only few users back then.\n\nIt might 
 be more acceptable when the tools for creating livepatches can deal with i
 t. But is it enough?\n\nQuestion for discussion:\n\n - Is is possible to b
 reak the kernel livepatching API in 2026?\n - Is it enough to update tools
  for creating livepatches?\n - Is it needed and enough to update klp-build
 ?\n - How many old code streams people still support?\n - Should selftests
  stay backward compatible?\n\n  [1]: https://lore.kernel.org/all/202501150
 82431.5550-1-pmladek@suse.com/\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/all/2026060
 7131659.29281-1-laoar.shao@gmail.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2530/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602q1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T150000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152500
SUMMARY:Power Sequencing for Enumberable Busses - Driver Core Integrations?
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Chen-Yu Tsai · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2502/\n\nOn x86 / ACPI platforms\, devices on enumerable buss
 es can normally be seen directly by the OS. On device tree platforms\, the
 se devices sometimes require extra power sequencing like toggling regulato
 r supplies or GPIO lines. Over the years most of these cases have been sol
 ved\, but there are still some gaps.\n\nAs of kernel version v7.0\, suppor
 t for power sequencing generic PCI devices\, ones that have no extra toggl
 es that are not part of PCI specification\, and M.2 M-key slots is availab
 le [[link][1]]. v7.1 then adds support for the PCI part of E-key slots [[l
 ink][2]].\n\nSupport for the USB part of M.2 E-key slots is WIP by the aut
 hor [[link][3]]. Support for onboard USB devices and USB type A connectors
  is provided\nby the onboard device driver.\n\nThis session intends to giv
 e a quick overview of the current status and discuss whether parts of this
  could be moved or integrated at the driver core level. These include:\n\n
  - Bus code integration for acquiring power sequencers\n - Creating stub p
 latform devices to provide power control functionality\n   - USB onboard d
 evices\n   - PCI pwrctrl devices\n\nSuch mechanisms could then be reused f
 or the MDIO bus.\n\n  [1]: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-pci/20260107-pci-
 m2-v5-0-8173d8a72641@oss.qualcomm.com/\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/all
 /20260326-pci-m2-e-v7-0-43324a7866e6@oss.qualcomm.com/\n  [3]: https://lor
 e.kernel.org/all/20260916075745.3549953-1-wenst@chromium.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2502/
END:VEVENT
