SUMMARY:Intro / Welcome
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · https://lpc
 .events/event/20/contributions/2488/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2488/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602h1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T151000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T153000
SUMMARY:Improving TDX Linux integration: changes in TDX Migration and Attes
 tation
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Elena Reshetova · https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/20/contributions/2340/\n\nLive migration and runtime attestation
  are two critical features for Confidential Computing to get right\, not j
 ust in terms of fulfilling customer requirements\, but also correct integr
 ation into common Linux codebase and overall code simplicity and maintaina
 bility.  Based on Linux community feedback\, TDX architecture went through
  a few big changes last year wrt to Live Migration and Attestation to enab
 le simpler Linux integration.\n\nIn this short talk we will share the key 
 changes\, as well as our key learnings in this area that could be useful f
 or other architectures enabling these confidential computing features in L
 inux. We would also like to hear feedback from the community on other area
 s where such simplifications can make a great difference.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2340/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2482@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T151000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152800
SUMMARY:Bridging the VM Boundary: Scheduling Passthrough via pvsched and sc
 hed_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Josh Don\, 
 Vineeth Remanan Pillai · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2482/\
 n\nAs production workloads increasingly transition to virtual machines for
  security isolation and resource consolidation in multi-tenant environment
 s\, traditional CPU scheduling faces a critical M:N preemption challenge. 
 The host operating system schedules opaque virtual CPUs rather than the ac
 tual workload threads. Consequently\, the host scheduler remains blind to 
 the varying priorities and latency sensitivities of the guest threads runn
 ing inside the VM. This leads to severe priority inversion\; for instance\
 , the host scheduler cannot differentiate between a vCPU running a low-pri
 ority batch or kernel system thread and one executing a latency-sensitive 
 task (such as a critical helper daemon) nested within the same VM. Consequ
 ently\, critical latency-sensitive work is starved\, and physical resource
 s are wasted under host CPU contention.\n\nTo resolve this\, we propose a 
 bidirectional VM Scheduling Passthrough architecture to bridge the VM boun
 dary from a scheduling perspective. This model relies on cooperative\, par
 avirtualized communication to enable host-side awareness and control:\n1. 
 Guest-to-Host: The guest kernel exposes scheduling metadata (potential sig
 nals include thread-level priorities and latency requirements) to the host
 .\n2. Host-to-Guest / Host Control: The host leverages these guest signals
  to either (a) intelligently prioritize the execution of a given vCPU\, or
  (b) in a more complete solution\, directly select both the vCPU and the s
 pecific guest thread running on that vCPU to execute (this latter proposal
  is more aspirational\, and would effectively give the host full schedulin
 g control over the guest\, voiding the need for guest scheduling or load b
 alancing).\n\nImplementing such a model requires a clean separation of mec
 hanism and policy. In line with upstream maintainer feedback\, KVM should 
 remain policy-free\, acting purely as the communication conduit. We propos
 e sched_ext as the ideal framework to house the scheduling policy\, and pv
 sched as the communication mechanism to share data between host and guest.
  Running the policy in a host BPF scheduler allows for rapid iteration and
  workload-specific customization without modifying KVM or the core kernel.
 \n\nCurrent upstream efforts\, notably the IBM patch series "Introduce cpu
 _preferred_mask and steal-driven vCPU backoff"\, attempt to address host p
 reemption purely from the guest side. In this model\, the guest monitors h
 ypervisor steal time and flags heavily preempted vCPUs as "non-preferred" 
 to guide guest-side task migration. While this provides a defensive mechan
 ism\, it is unidirectional and reactive. Furthermore\, as highlighted by u
 pstream maintainers\, this approach faces significant limitations: it risk
 s embedding scheduling policy within the hypervisor\, and in high-overcomm
 it scenarios where steal time is elevated across all cores\, the guest-sid
 e mask degrades\, leaving the guest scheduler with no viable execution tar
 gets.\n\npvsched (https://github.com/pvsched/) has undergone some discussi
 on on the list already\, and v3 is being prepared.\n\nIn this session\, we
  want to discuss:\n- Interface Design: The updates in pvsched v3\n- sched_
 ext Integration: How sched_ext can ingest these guest signals to influence
  host scheduling decisions\, and conversely\, how it can export host sched
 uling desires back to the guest
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2482/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602b1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154500
SUMMARY:livepatch: Introduce replace set support
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · Petr Mladek\, Yafang Shao · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2526/\n\nMy employer relies heavily on livepat
 ch to rapidly experiment with new kernel features without interrupting pro
 duction workloads. Our use cases include:\n\n- Case 1: Deploying a livepat
 ch function as a stable BPF hook.\n   For example\, some proposals for suc
 h a use case has already been submitted upstream but has not yet been acce
 pted:\n  https://lwn.net/Articles/1054030/\n  https://lwn.net/Articles/104
 3548/\n  We also have another internal use case that we do not plan to ups
 tream:\n  https://lore.kernel.org/live-patching/CALOAHbDnNba_w_nWH3-S9GAXw
 0+VKuLTh1gy5hy9Yqgeo4C0iA@mail.gmail.com/\n\n- Case 2: Combining fleet-wid
 e cumulative livepatches with workload-specific livepatches.\n  For exampl
 e\, consider the VFS cache adjustment feature that we previously upstreame
 d:\n  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-fsdevel/20250511083624.9305-1-laoar.sh
 ao@gmail.com/\n  We initially deployed this feature as a livepatch before 
 upstreaming it. In the livepatch version\, we could not introduce a sysctl
  interface for per-workload tuning\, so we used a hard-coded default value
  and deployed it only to specific servers.\n\nIn Case 1\, the livepatched 
 BPF hook must remain stable. Otherwise\, existing BPF programs may become 
 invalid and need to be reloaded\, which could introduce operational risks.
 \n\nIn Case 2\, we currently need to release different cumulative livepatc
 hes for different workloads. However\, we would like to have a generic cum
 ulative livepatch combined with individual workload-specific livepatches. 
 This approach would significantly reduce the maintenance burden of managin
 g multiple livepatch variants.\n\nBased on these requirements\, we propose
 d a hybrid livepatch mode\, which allows multiple livepatches to coexist w
 ith different scopes. This proposal was later refined into the replace_set
  support proposal\, which has already been discussed on the livepatch mail
 ing list:\n\n  https://lore.kernel.org/live-patching/20260607131659.29281-
 1-laoar.shao@gmail.com/\n\nHowever\, several implementation details and de
 sign decisions remain open. We would like to discuss them at LPC and deter
 mine the best path forward.\n\nIn this presentation\, I will describe how 
 we use livepatch across our large fleet of production servers and discuss 
 the improvements needed to make livepatch suitable for a broader range of 
 use cases.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2526/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602r1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T155000
SUMMARY:Reviving early platform drivers
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Bartosz Golaszewski · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2503/\n\nCertain critical subsystems - clocks\, timers
  and interrupt controllers - sometimes need to be initialized before drive
 r core is made available in driver_init(). To that end\, we provide a set 
 of macros: IRQCHIP_DECLARE()\, CLK_OF_DECLARE()\, TIMER_OF_DECLARE() which
  allow the kernel to call initialization functions based on compatibles ei
 ther before reaching the point where actual platform devices matching thes
 e compatibles can be created or *instead* of creating them essentially byp
 assing the driver model entirely.\n\nThis results in the initialization ro
 utines not being able to use many APIs only available to real drivers and 
 - for many modules - never registering with the driver core.\n\nI've worke
 d on the idea of unifying these paths with a concept of "early platform dr
 ivers" back in 2018. That work never got anywhere but the "hacky" approach
  for early setup remains.\n\nI'd like to re-discuss the idea\, it's pros a
 nd cons and provide possible solutions for the main contention point raise
 d last time: the fact that my series did nothing to automatically convert 
 existing invocations of the _DECLARE() macros to using early platform driv
 ers.\n\n[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20180511162028.20616-1-brgl@bgdev.
 pl/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2503/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2483@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T152800
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154600
SUMMARY:BPF-based Composable Idle cpumask Selection
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Emil Tsalap
 atis · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2483/\n\nAll sched_ext s
 chedulers currently use kfuncs to manage their idle cpumask using a hardco
 ded policy provided by the kernel. This lack of configurability of the cur
 rent cpumask requires us to add policy through explicit masking operations
  directly in the scheduler code. This in turn leads to duplicating idle CP
 U selection logic across schedulers as it is difficult to factor it out.\n
 \nThis session discusses a new BPF-based subsystem for idle CPU mask selec
 tion. This subsystem abstracts the representation of the idle CPU set behi
 nd a common API that exposes explicit alternative policies to the user. Th
 e API is portable between schedulers and requires little code to adopt. In
 ternally\, the subsystem stores idle CPU information in arena-based data s
 tructures. Different structures (e.g.\, radix trees) provide different per
 formance/scalability tradeoffs and can be tailored to different idle CPU s
 election policies.\n\nTopics for this session:\n    - Overview of the idle
  CPU selection subsystem\n    - Data structures for representing the idle 
 CPU set\, tradeoffs for each\n    - Representing policy in a flexible\, ex
 tensible fashion through the idle selection API\n    - Using alternative C
 PU selection strategies as part of load balancing
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2483/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2464@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160000
SUMMARY:swiotlb Under Pressure: High-Speed NICs in Confidential VMs
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Networking Track · Dragoș Tătulea · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2464/\n\nSystems that treat devices as untrusted protec
 t themselves using confidential computing memory protection. Linux uses sw
 iotlb to handle the necessary buffer bouncing transparently to drivers. Fo
 r high-performance NICs operating at scale\, however\, the overhead of all
 ocating and managing bounce buffers can substantially exceed the cost of t
 he memory copies themselves.\n\nReceive queues create long-lived pressure 
 on the swiotlb pool. Pages are DMA-mapped when they enter a page_pool and 
 normally remain mapped while they are recycled\, often until they leave th
 e pool or the queue is destroyed. Across many receive queues\, these mappi
 ngs can retain a substantial portion of the available bounce-buffer memory
 \, even after the pool has been increased to the largest practical size fo
 r the system. As occupancy increases\, allocations search additional swiot
 lb areas and contend on their per-area locks. This primarily affects trans
 mission\, where an SKB’s linear area and fragments can each require a se
 parate bounce-buffer allocation\, copy\, synchronization\, and reclamation
  sequence.\n\nTo evaluate an alternative to per-mapping swiotlb bouncing\,
  we implemented a prototype mlx5e datapath based on preallocated DMA-coher
 ent staging memory. With 16 transmit-heavy TCP streams\, one per queue\, t
 he swiotlb datapath reduced throughput by 70% relative to a baseline witho
 ut buffer bouncing. The explicit staging datapath reduced this loss to 10
 –20%.\n\nIn this talk\, we will compare the generic swiotlb approach wit
 h the driver-managed bounce buffers\, present detailed measurements\, and 
 analyze the bottlenecks we identified. We will conclude by discussing what
  an upstream solution should look like: driver-specific staging buffers\, 
 a generic networking abstraction or improvements to swiotlb scalability.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2464/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602i1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T153000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160000
SUMMARY:Arm CCA Realm Live Migration
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Mathias Brossard · https://lpc.ev
 ents/event/20/contributions/2476/\n\nArm is developing Live Migration ABIs
  for the Arm Confidential Compute Architecture (CCA)\, with input and requ
 irements from ecosystem partners. The ABIs are provided by the Realm Manag
 ement Monitor (RMM)\, the trusted firmware component responsible for manag
 ing Realms.\n\nThe presentation will start with a short overview of the hi
 gh-level CCA Live Migration design and the end-to-end process. It will the
 n examine the assumptions and design choices underpinning the ABIs in thre
 e areas: core design\, platform preconditions\, and runtime mechanisms\, r
 elating each to the broader security\, correctness\, and practicality cons
 traints of confidential live migration.\n\nFinally\, the presentation will
  provide a basis for discussing how the emerging CCA Live Migration ABIs a
 nd Linux/KVM infrastructure for confidential-computing migration can infor
 m one another.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2476/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2367@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:FUSE mostly-passthrough filesystems
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Amir Goldstein · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2367/\n\nMany production FUSE filesystems do not 
 need to reimplement the full filesystem stack.  They need to mirror an exi
 sting directory with full fidelity while intercepting only a small subset 
 of operations for caching\, tiering\, HSM stub manifestation\, or auditing
 .\n\nTo make these filesystems easier to build\, we introduce [libfuse_pas
 sthrough][1] - a C++ library that handles all FUSE plumbing and exposes a 
 lightweight module API where only the intercepted operations need to be im
 plemented where everything else passes through automatically.\n\nThe libra
 ry makes use of upstream kernel FUSE read/write passthrough\, which allows
  open files to bypass the FUSE daemon entirely\, and provides an experimen
 tal playground for the development of [more FUSE kernel passthrough operat
 ions][2].\n\nWe invite contributors and users with FUSE passthrough worklo
 ads to learn about our experience\, discuss open problems\, kernel interfa
 ce requirements\, and help shape the future of FUSE.\n\n  [1]: https://git
 hub.com/amir73il/libfuse/tree/libfuse_passthrough/passthrough\n  [2]: http
 s://lore.kernel.org/fuse-devel/20260420221637.2631478-1-joannelkoong@gmail
 .com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2367/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602c1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:A Common Coexistence Model for Live Patching and Production Observa
 bility
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · Kris Van Hees\, Song Liu\, Yi Zhu · https:
 //lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2523/\n\n**Abstract**\nLarge Linux fle
 ets increasingly depend on always-on observability: BPF programs\, ftrace\
 , kprobes\, kretprobes\, and continuous profiling agents are part of the c
 ore production control plane. Kernel livepatching depends on some of the s
 ame low-level mechanisms\, especially dynamic ftrace-based redirection at 
 function entry. In large production environments\, we routinely observe fl
 eet rollout delays because critical security livepatches conflict with sys
 tem-wide tracing tools anchored to the same target functions.\n\nThe curre
 nt kernel documentation notes that kprobes\, ftrace\, and livepatch must n
 ot step on each other\, citing limitations like kretprobe conflicts. At fl
 eet scale\, these are not just corner cases\; they become rollout blockers
 \, visibility gaps\, or sources of dangerous runtime uncertainty.\n\nThis 
 session proposes an upstream discussion to define a predictable\, common c
 oexistence model. The goal is to identify the minimum kernel and tooling i
 nterfaces needed by livepatch builders\, BPF/tracing tool authors\, and fl
 eet rollout systems to gracefully handle instrumentation occupancy.\n\n**D
 iscussion Topics**\n\n 1. **Conflict Inventory:** Documenting attach-point
  collisions among livepatch\, ftrace\, kprobes\, and BPF trampolines.\n 2.
  **Occupancy Reporting:** Designing a per-function "patchability" report f
 or tools like klp-build and rollout agents.\n 3. **Deterministic Failures:
 ** Returning explicit conflict reasons when an attachment is rejected inst
 ead of opaque errors.\n 4. **Transition Progress:** Exposing standard trac
 epoints or counters for stuck tasks\, forced transitions\, and instrumenta
 tion blocks.\n 5. **Test Coverage:** Expanding kselftest to include negati
 ve tests for rejected\, unsafe instrumentation combinations.\n 6. **Policy
  & Chaining:** Debating safe chaining semantics and deciding if observabil
 ity should redirect to replacement functions.\n\n**Desired Outcome**\nAgre
 ement on where these mechanisms belong (kernel ABI\, sysfs/debugfs reporti
 ng\, kselftest\, or klp-build metadata). The discussion will involve livep
 atch\, ftrace\, kprobe\, and BPF maintainers alongside large-fleet operato
 rs.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2523/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2484@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T154600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160400
SUMMARY:Taming latency spikes caused by lock holder and lock waiter preempt
 ion
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Changwoo Mi
 n · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2484/\n\nPreempting a lock 
 holder — or failing to promptly schedule a just-woken\nlock waiter — e
 xtends the serialized critical section and produces severe\ntail-latency (
 P99) spikes: degraded server throughput\, frame-time\nspikes and dropped f
 rames in games. Applications hit this on both\nkernel-space locks and user
 -space primitives backed by futexes and SysV\nsemaphores. Existing techniq
 ues help but leave gaps: proxy execution\naddresses kernel-mutex priority 
 inversion but not user-space futex waiters\nor counting semaphores\, and t
 ime-slice extension protects a detected holder\nwhile doing nothing for th
 e delayed waiter.\n\nWe define lock waiter preemption (LWP) as the dual of
  lock holder\npreemption (LHP): the scheduling delay a woken waiter suffer
 s before it\nruns\, which we have measured at 2–16 ms in production game
  and server\nworkloads. We will present these measurements and a prototype
  LHP/LWP\nmitigation built in a production sched_ext scheduler\, then open
  three\nchallenges for community discussion:\n\n- efficiently identifying 
 lock-related preemption from BPF\;\n- exposing synchronization state betwe
 en libc and sched_ext\;\n- designing lightweight scheduler-assisted lockin
 g that improves latency\n  without sacrificing scalability.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2484/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602s1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T155000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T161500
SUMMARY:Evolving support for sync_state to subsystems beyond genpd
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Ulf Hansson · https://lpc.events/event/20/co
 ntributions/2504/\n\nAt last LPC in Tokyo we discussed about the limitatio
 ns of the sync_state support that quite recently was added to the generic 
 PM domain (genpd) subsystem. The conclusion was to mainly focus on making 
 it more fine grained\, as this should address most of the problems. Attemp
 ts to implement this has been submitted to LKML [1]. Discussion and iterat
 ions of the series are moving forward\, but a solution is yet to be landed
 .\n\nIn this regards\, we also have the need to extend the support for syn
 c_state to more subsystems beyond genpd\, to move away from the broken the
  "disable unused" features that each subsystem currently provides. Moreove
 r\, ideally we prefer the support for sync_state to be adopted on a subsys
 tem basis\, rather than relying on a per driver based implementation\, whi
 ch isn't scaling. Attempts have been made to add support to the regulator 
 and clock subsystems\, while the support in the interconnect subsystem nee
 ds improvements.\n\nLet's discuss these topics and in particular how we ca
 n make subsystem specific implementations to coexist and play along with e
 ach other.\n\n[1]\n[PATCH v3 00/13] driver core / pmdomain: Add support fo
 r fined grained sync_state\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/all/20260508123910.114
 273-1-ulf.hansson@linaro.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2504/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2462@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:KNOD: In-Kernel GPU Offload for Packet Processing
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Networking Track · Hoyeon Lee\, Taehee Yoo · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2462/\n\nAt LPC 2025\, we presented an early pro
 totype for running XDP programs directly on an AMD GPU from within the Lin
 ux kernel\, without CUDA\, ROCm\, or any userspace component in the data p
 ath.[1]\n\nSince then\, the project has evolved into knod\, an in-kernel n
 etwork offload device\, and an RFC patch set has been posted.[2] knod uses
  the GPU as a kernel-managed packet-processing accelerator: the kernel JIT
 -compiles an XDP program into GPU machine code\, the NIC places packets di
 rectly in GPU-accessible memory\, and the GPU processes them in parallel a
 nd returns XDP verdicts. The RFC extends the original XDP prototype into a
  generic offload-device model\, with RX IPsec as an additional use case.\n
 \nMajor changes since LPC 2025 include a redesigned NIC-to-GPU path that a
 llows the GPU to consume packets directly\, support for divergent control 
 flow in GPU-offloaded BPF programs\, RDNA2 support\, and optimizations to 
 batching\, dispatch\, occupancy\, and memory transfers. The current RFC re
 ports up to 70 Mpps for a Katran-derived XDP workload and 80 Gbit/s for RX
  IPsec.\n\nThis talk will walk through how knod has evolved since LPC 2025
  and discuss the remaining design and implementation challenges: the bound
 aries among the networking core\, bpf\, drm\, and accelerator drivers\; pe
 r-CPU map and queue-affinity semantics on accelerators\; further performan
 ce optimization\; support for additional GPU architectures\; and the Gener
 ic Netlink/YNL control plane and its integration with existing tools.\n\n[
 1]: https://lpc.events/event/19/contributions/2267/\n[2]: https://lore.ker
 nel.org/netdev/20260719175857.4071636-1-ap420073@gmail.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2462/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602j1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:CoVE in Action: the Landscape of Confidential VMs on RISC-V
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Ruoqing He\, Xiaoxia Cui · https:
 //lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2472/\n\nRISC-V CoVE (Confidential VM 
 Extension) brings confidential computing — hardware-enforced isolation o
 f tenant workloads from the hypervisor and cloud operator — to a fully o
 pen architecture.\nA lightweight TEE Security Manager (TSM) sits below the
  hypervisor and enforces per-VM memory isolation\, while tenants verify th
 eir environment through a standard IETF RATS attestation flow. The hypervi
 sor retains scheduling control — it simply cannot see inside a tenant's 
 TEE Virtual Machine (TVM).\nWe demonstrate CoVE Deployment Model 1 running
  end-to-end on real RISC-V hardware\, built on OpenSBI and integrated with
  Kata Containers and the confidential-containers project — bringing conf
 idential computing to container runtimes operators already use. No proprie
 tary extensions\, no special hardware — just standard RISC-V silicon.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2472/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2481@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T160400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T163000
SUMMARY:Making proxy execution compatible with sched_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Andrea Righ
 i · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2481/\n\nProxy execution al
 lows a waiting task (the "donor") to donate its execution context to a mut
 ex owner\, enabling the owner to continue running while the donor remains 
 eligible on the runqueue.\n\nToday\, proxy execution and sched_ext are mut
 ually exclusive build-time options: a kernel cannot be built with both CON
 FIG_SCHED_PROXY_EXEC=y and CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT=y.\n\nThis limitation is
  problematic for Linux distributions and anyone who wants to ship a single
  kernel image while selecting features at runtime.\n\nAn RFC series propos
 ing support for this integration has already been posted: https://lore.ker
 nel.org/all/20260506174639.535232-1-arighi@nvidia.com/\n\nHowever\, severa
 l design questions remain open. This session aims to discuss those issues\
 , reach agreement on the overall approach\, and define a concrete plan for
  moving the integration forward.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2481/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602t1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T161500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T164000
SUMMARY:Constification of sysfs attribute structures
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Thomas Weißschuh · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2505/\n\nSysfs attributes are used throughout the kernel
  to implement UAPI.\nSubsystems either use common attributes\, like `kobj_
 attribute` and `device_attr` or define their own wrapper structures.\nThes
 e structures are only descriptors defining the behavior of an attribute an
 d normally never change.\n\nHistorically the attribute however are not mar
 ked as `const` and could be modified through their various callbacks. Such
  modifications are inherently racy and therefore bug-prone or even a vecto
 r for attackers to redirect control-flow.\n\nFor some time I have been wor
 king on making it possible to mark all the different attribute structures 
 as `const` to fix these issues.\n\nAgenda:\n* Problem statement (see above
 )\n* Current state\n  * Which attributes *can* be marked `const` today?\n 
  * Which attributes are already converted.\n* Discussion (see below)\n\n* 
 Discussion\n\nDiscussion topics:\n* Which attribute types are still missin
 g?\n* How can subsystem maintainers convert their custom attribute types?\
 n* How to actually convert all the structure instances throughout the tree
 ?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2505/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2460@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173000
SUMMARY:PCI data path optimization with CQE coalescing and new ethtool para
 meters
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Networking Track · Haiyang Zhang · https://lpc.events/event/2
 0/contributions/2460/\n\nTo optimize the PCI data path on the Azure MANA N
 IC\, we implemented support for Completion Queue Entry\, or CQE\, coalesci
 ng. In the regular receive path\, each packet can generate a separate CQE 
 that must be processed by the driver\, increasing interrupt pressure and C
 PU & PCI overhead. CQE coalescing reduces this cost by **merging multiple 
 CQEs into one aggregated completion** that represents several received net
 work packets. This allows the driver to process more packets per interrupt
  and reduces the amount of per-packet overhead required in the fast path. 
 The improvement is especially important on ARM64 platforms\, where interru
 pt handling cost and cache efficiency can strongly affect networking throu
 ghput. In our measurements\, CQE coalescing provided a throughput enhancem
 ent ranging from 33% to 62% while also increasing the number of packets ha
 ndled per interrupt.\n\nWe also added **two new parameters to both the eth
 tool kernel API and the ethtool command line: ETHTOOL_A_COALESCE_RX_CQE_FR
 AMES and ETHTOOL_A_COALESCE_RX_CQE_NSECS** as suggested by netdev maintain
 ers. These parameters expose CQE coalescing through the standard Linux net
 working configuration model instead of relying on a driver-specific privat
 e flag. The frame-based setting controls how many packet completions may b
 e combined before a coalesced CQE is generated\, while the time-based sett
 ing controls how long the device may wait before reporting the aggregated 
 completion. Together\, they allow administrators to tune the balance betwe
 en throughput\, CPU utilization\, interrupt rate\, and receive latency for
  different workloads. Other NIC drivers that support similar CQE coalescin
 g behavior can adopt the same interface\, improving consistency across Lin
 ux networking devices.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2460/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2366@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T174500
SUMMARY:Defining the pkeys ABI
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Kevin Brodsky · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2366/\n\nWe have been supporting pkeys [1] on Linu
 x for 10 years now\, and they\nare no longer specific to x86: arm64 and po
 werpc support them too.\nUnfortunately\, important gaps remain in the kern
 el-user ABI\, making it\ndifficult to deploy pkeys robustly for key use-ca
 ses.\n\npkeys work in a fairly simple way: the user allocates a new pkey a
 nd\nthen assigns that pkey to a VMA. Access to that VMA is then restricted
 \nby a user-controlled register\, which defines RWX permissions for every\
 npkey.  The restrictions apply to both user and kernel accesses (uaccess\n
 and GUP).\n\nDifficulties arise when the kernel interrupts a user thread a
 nd then\naccesses its memory\, or invokes a signal handler. In those cases
 \, it is\nunclear which pkeys authority the kernel should use\, whether us
 erspace\nshould be able to configure it\, and how to avoid spurious crashe
 s or\nconfused deputy situations. This is what the current ABI is lacking.
 \n\nThis BoF is especially intended for arch/mm maintainers\, libc/runtime
 \ndevelopers\, and userspace developers already familiar with pkeys or\nde
 ploying pkey-based isolation. The goal is not to present a finished\ndesig
 n\, but to agree on the programming model that future ABI work\nshould fol
 low.\n\nA few important use-cases are currently difficult or impossible to
 \nimplement robustly:\n\n* Isolating the alternate signal stack: mapping i
 t with a non-default\n  pkey that only signal handlers are allowed to acce
 ss.\n\n* Sandboxing with pkeys: preventing some context from accessing\n  
 even the default pkey (0).\n\nProblematic situations include:\n\n* Signal 
 delivery [2]: signal handlers may be called asynchronously and\n  are conc
 eptually independent of the interrupted context.\n  Userspace may need the
  pkey register to be reset to a specific value\n  both for writing the sig
 nal frame and invoking the signal handler.\n\n* rseq [3]: the kernel write
 s to the registered struct rseq when context\n  switching\, which can happ
 en at any point. This is again independent of\n  the interrupted context\,
  which may not be allowed to write to that\n  struct.\n\n* io_uring worker
  threads: these are kernel threads that access user\n  memory asynchronous
 ly. They have their own pkey register value\n  restricting those accesses\
 , but userspace is not currently able to\n  configure it.\n\n* Other cases
 \, such as BPF helpers accessing user memory and\n  process_vm_readv(self)
  bypassing pkeys completely.\n\nAll these issues lead to a set of question
 s that we need to answer to\ncreate a consistent and unsurprising ABI:\n\n
 * Which contexts need a dedicated or userspace-configurable pkey\n  regist
 er value?\n\n* Are there accesses that should intentionally bypass pkeys\,
  and if so\,\n  how should that be documented?\n\n* Can these issues be ha
 ndled with targeted fixes\, or do we need a more\n  unified ABI design for
  asynchronous kernel access to pkey-protected\n  memory?\n\nAs Thomas Glei
 xner put it:\n\n> We really need to sit down and actually define a proper 
 programming\n> model first instead of trying to duct tape the current ill 
 defined\n> mess forever.\n\nThe intended outcome of this BoF is a shared d
 irection for documenting\nand extending the pkeys ABI\, so that pkeys can 
 be used reliably as\nintra-process privilege boundaries rather than only a
 s a best-effort\nhardening mechanism.\n\n[1] https://docs.kernel.org/core-
 api/protection-keys.html\n[2] https://inbox.sourceware.org/libc-alpha/fc31
 e639-f1eb-42d6-9dea-3665d9507f12@arm.com/\n[3] https://lore.kernel.org/all
 /87ikexhbah.ffs@tglx/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2366/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602d1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173000
SUMMARY:SFrame for Arm64 Reliable Stacktrace
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · Dylan Hatch · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2529/\n\nThe Livepatch consistency model [1] requires the ke
 rnel to provide reliable stacktrace in order to be fully supported. On x86
 \, the ORC unwinder provides these reliable stacktraces. However\, arm64 m
 isses the required support from objtool: it cannot generate ORC unwind tab
 les for arm64. Prior RFCs have proposed to add this support to objtool\, b
 ut feedback from the upstream community has indicated that a solution usin
 g data produced directly from the compiler would be preferred [2]\, [3].\n
 \nIn the v6.17 release\, the Arm64 kernel gained livepatch support\, but w
 ithout fully reliable stacktrace [4]. With this partial solution\, interru
 pt stacks cannot be reliably traced because the unwinder cannot tell if th
 e Link Register was current at the time the exception was taken.\n\nSFrame
  provides a generalized\, compiler-based solution\, inspired by the ORC un
 winder [5]\, [6]. Currently\, there's already an SFrame unwinder proposed 
 for userspace: [7].\n\nWe would like to propose similar functionality to p
 rovide reliable stacktraces within the Arm64 kernel: [8]. This patch serie
 s adds to and depends upon the exising userspace patches by factoring out 
 a common SFrame-lookup library which supports both kernel and userspace SF
 rame sections. Previous versions of this series implemented an SFrame-only
  kernel unwinder to replace frame-pointer unwinding entirely. However\, af
 ter receiving feedback\, the kernel unwinder now relies on SFrame only whe
 n unwinding across an exception boundary\, and uses frame pointers everywh
 ere else. This combined strategy enables the best of both worlds: the supe
 rior performance of frame-pointer unwinding is kept whenever possible\, wh
 ile the SFrame table allows for fully reliable unwind of a stack with inte
 rrupts.\n\nOngoing mailing list discussion draws into question SFrame's vi
 ability as a userspace unwinding solution [9]. This raises several questio
 ns around the in-kernel approach\, which we would like to bring forward fo
 r discussion:\n\n- What is the current pathway for support for SFrame gene
 ration within the LLVM toolchain?\n\n- The kernel unwind patches continue 
 to have a dependency on the userspace patches. From a development velocity
  perspective\, does this dependency make sense to keep in future versions?
 \n\n- For the purpose of unwinding across interrupt boundaries\, are there
  alternatives to SFrame that should be considered?\n\n[1]: https://docs.ke
 rnel.org/livepatch/livepatch.html#consistency-model\n[2]: https://lore.ker
 nel.org/live-patching/ZXxO43Xwn5GHsrO8@FVFF77S0Q05N/\n[3]: https://lpc.eve
 nts/event/17/contributions/1540/attachments/1356/2713/lpc2023-arm64-livepa
 tching.pdf\n[4]: https://lore.kernel.org/live-patching/20250320171559.3423
 224-1-song@kernel.org/\n[5]: https://sourceware.org/binutils/docs/sframe-s
 pec.html\n[6]: https://lwn.net/Articles/1029189/\n[7]: https://lore.kernel
 .org/all/20260505121718.3572346-1-jremus@linux.ibm.com/\n[8]: https://lore
 .kernel.org/lkml/20260519064950.493949-1-dylanbhatch@google.com/\n[9]: htt
 ps://lore.kernel.org/all/CAN30aBFVDxeoXApn_g_Hw0Ayhi4V=m7CcX8UDO6ZDTi6xA-3
 Pg@mail.gmail.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2529/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602k1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T172000
SUMMARY:SEV-TIO implementation challenges
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Alexey Kardashevskiy · https://lp
 c.events/event/20/contributions/2473/\n\nSEV-TIO is quite known by now\, t
 he upstream development is split in stages and continues. The current stag
 es are set to support basic functionality.\n\nThe talk will focus on exten
 ded features and how AMD hardware/firmware is going to implement these. Th
 is includes:\n- huge pages handling in IOMMU and KVM\, how RMP works and w
 hat TMPM does in PSMASH_IO.\n- IOMMU TLB flushing challenges: a hack for T
 urin CPU family and a proper fix in the Venice family (new behavior or inv
 lpgb) and what Venice will do in addition to that.\n- Cbit and vTOM and ho
 w we can allow simultaneous private and shared access for the same device 
 within the same VM + a (useless) way to implement “iommu=pt”-like beha
 vior in the guest.\n- sev-guest SNP platform device and using the DMA laye
 r for sharing memory with the hypervisor.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2473/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2485@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171800
SUMMARY:Stickiness: Keeping tasks cache-warm in work-conserving LAVD schedu
 ler
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Gavin Guo 
 · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2485/\n\nWork-conserving sche
 dulers prefer running tasks on idle CPUs\nimmediately\, ensuring no proces
 sing capacity is wasted while work is\nwaiting. In the lavd select_cpu pro
 cess\, when a task wakes\, the\nscheduler will do its best to seek an idle
  core and run the task over\nthere. However\, this idle-oriented CPU selec
 tion generally prioritizes\nidle cores over the cache-warm cores\, leading
  to more migrations\,\nmaking the tasks cache-cold\, and requiring L1/L2 c
 aches and TLBs to be\nrefilled frequently.\n\nThe work-conserving mechanis
 m works well in some scenarios. However\,\nthere are cache-sensitive workl
 oads such as edge routers using routing\ntables as in-memory KV stores. Th
 e migrations to the cold CPU have a\nglobal impact\, and the cache refilli
 ng evicts the other cache lines\nand pulls the new ones in\, making the ca
 che contention worse and further\nimpacting the tail latency.\n\n---\nWe w
 ill present our measurements and open the floor on topics\nincluding:\n\n-
  **Warmth estimation**: How long does L1/L2/TLB state realistically\n  sur
 vive on a CPU\, and could the kernel expose hardware signals\n  (e.g.\, PM
 U counters\, cache-occupancy registers) usable at\n  scheduling-decision f
 requency?\n\n- **The wait-or-migrate decision**: Waiting requires predicti
 ng when\n  a busy CPU will become available (remaining slice + queue servi
 ce\n  time\, or queue load?). Which metrics should a scheduler maintain to
 \n  make that prediction accurate?\n\n- **The scheduler/userspace interfac
 e**: Userspace structures (such\n  as allocator per-CPU caches) suffer hea
 vily from core migrations.\n  Should applications hint their locality need
 s to the scheduler\, or\n  should the scheduler expose warmth and migratio
 n state to userspace?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2485/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602u1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173500
SUMMARY:Hardware Cross-Dependencies - Solving the Unsolvable
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · AngeloGioacchino Del Regno\, Sebastian Reiche
 l · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2501/\n\nWe are seeing hard
  to solve cross-dependencies between different SoC subsystems (Rockchip\, 
 MediaTek\, etc.). For example a power domain needing an I2C regulator\, bu
 t the I2C regulator needing the I2C bus and the I2C bus driver needing a (
 different) power domain. This creates a cyclic dependency\, since the powe
 r domains (or clocks) are usually all behind a single device.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2501/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2486@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T171800
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173600
SUMMARY:HFI plug in to scx_ext
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Srinivas Pa
 ndruvada · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2486/\n\nThis presen
 tation explores how Intel HFI can be integrated with a sched_ext to improv
 e task placement and power-performance efficiency on hybrid Intel systems.
  HFI provides real-time hardware guidance on which CPUs are better suited 
 for performance- or efficiency-oriented work\, or which to avoid\, while s
 ched_ext like LAVD supplies an adaptive scheduling framework capable of us
 ing that guidance at runtime. By converting HFI output into scheduler-read
 able CPU hints\, the system can bias wakeup placement\, avoid degraded cor
 es\, and better match task type to CPU capability without hard pinning or 
 manual tuning.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2486/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602l1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T172000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T175000
SUMMARY:Extending Arm CCA Device Assignment to CXL Type-2 Accelerators
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Ankit Agrawal · https://lpc.event
 s/event/20/contributions/2474/\n\nWe are extending Arm CCA device assignme
 nt to CXL Type-2 accelerators so a Realm VM can receive such a device with
  a coherent device memory window. Standard RME-DA assigns a PCIe TDI to a 
 Realm through TDISP\, SPDM and IDE\, protecting CXL.io traffic. A CXL Type
 -2 device also exposes a coherent CXL.mem window through HDM decoders and 
 which confidential assignment must also secure.\n\nRMM v2.0 introduces a P
 DEV Stream object as a first-class handle for the device security channel 
 and a COH_CMEM stream type for coherent off-chip accelerators - the right 
 primitives to extend CCA to CXL Type-2. Several design problems remain uns
 olved: how the host TSM obtains and registers the CXL coherent memory rang
 e with RMM\, how the guest TSM attests that range\, and how to coordinate 
 device probe with stream connect so the coherent range is always registere
 d with RMM regardless of call ordering. This session will work through the
 se open questions to align on a direction for upstream development.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2474/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2459@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
SUMMARY:Coroutines for Linux kernel
LOCATION:South Hall 1 A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Networking Track · Marek Behún · https://lpc.events/event/20
 /contributions/2459/\n\nThere are multiple kernel drivers which could bene
 fit were it possible to somehow do coroutines in C. Coroutines would make 
 these drivers easier to comprehend\, extend with new code paths\, and debu
 g.\n\nThe most common use case lies in simplifying complex state machines.
  For example consider the main state machine `sfp_sm_main()` in `drivers/n
 et/phy/sfp.c`. Each invocation executes code for a specific state\, update
 s the state\, and returns. The current implementation even relies on goto 
 statements to jump between cases. As a result\, understanding the control 
 flow requires tracking disjointed code paths or drawing manual state diagr
 ams.\n\nUsing coroutines\, this logic can be transformed into a straightfo
 rward\, linear sequence using standard loop and conditional structures (`i
 f`\, `for`\, `while`). By leveraging GNU C computed gotos\, we can impleme
 nt mechanics similar to C++20's `co_await` and `co_return`. With some tric
 kery\, it is even possible to preserve variable values across suspension p
 oints.\n\nLet's explore hiding this underlying complexity behind clean\, m
 aintainable macros and discuss whether this approach can be safely impleme
 nted for production code.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2459/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602e1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
SUMMARY:Bringing klp-build to LoongArch: toolchain lessons for the next arc
 hitecture
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · dongtai guo · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2525/\n\nA series adding LoongArch support to objtool's `klp
  diff` subcommand (the\ndiffing engine klp-build invokes to generate a pat
 ch module) is under\nreview (v4:\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/all/202607241141
 28.31451-1-dongtai.guo@linux.dev).\nA v5\, with a reordering requested by 
 the LoongArch maintainer\, is in\npreparation. Most of the effort went int
 o the interaction between this\ntooling and the LoongArch toolchains\, and
  several findings generalize to\nany future architecture with aggressive l
 inker relaxation.\n\nThis topic walks through the klp-diff issues surfaced
  across the review\nrounds. They are not one bug but a family of toolchain
 -interaction\nfailures:\n\n- *PC-relative reachability.* A livepatch modul
 e maps farther than the\n  +-2GB a `pcalau12i/addi.d` pair can express\, s
 o a klp relocation\n  resolving to a vmlinux symbol overflows. Far calls a
 re already\n  redirected through a PLT stub by the module loader\, but fil
 e-local\n  static data is materialized inline\; a new arch hook rewrites t
 hose\n  PC-relative data references to GOT-indirect loads. This is not\n  
 Clang-specific: GCC emits the same form.\n- *Local-label anchors.* GCC/GAS
  on LoongArch anchor relocations to `.L*`\n  local labels to keep them rel
 axable\, where klp diff assumed a function\n  or section symbol plus adden
 d (LLVM folds these labels\, so this one is\n  toolchain-specific). Fix: n
 ormalize `.L*`-anchored relocations back to\n  their containing function\,
  fix annotation offsets in\n  `create_fake_symbols`\, and collapse `R_LARC
 H_ADD64/SUB64` pairs into one\n  PC-relative relocation via `arch_normaliz
 e_paired_reloc`.\n- *Section pairing and inline alternatives.* Keep both w
 ords of each\n  `-mannotate-tablejump` annotation entry\, and define\n  `A
 RCH_HAS_INLINE_ALTS` so the `.subsection 1` ALTERNATIVE() replacement\n  i
 s cloned\, mirroring arm64.\nOn the compiler side\, livepatch modules on L
 oongArch require -fPIC\n(GOT-indirect access instead of absolute addressin
 g)\, which collides\nwith the kernel's -fno-PIE defaults in non-obvious wa
 ys (last-one-wins\nflag ordering vs KBUILD_CFLAGS_KERNEL)\, and Clang reje
 cts GCC-style\n"awM" special-section flags\, requiring ANNOTATE_DATA_SPECI
 AL.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2525/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602v1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
SUMMARY:Synx: Cross-core synchronization
LOCATION:Club B+C\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Driver Core MC · Pravin Kumar Ravi · https://lpc.events/event
 /20/contributions/2507/\n\n## Abstract\n\nModern SoCs increasingly run par
 ts of a single pipeline (AI/ML\,\nvision\, camera\, graphics\, sensors) ac
 ross a mix of Linux drivers\nand firmwares on remote processors (NPUs and 
 AI processors\, ISPs\,\ncompanion cores). Coordinating that pipeline requi
 res\nsynchronization objects that can be created\, synchronously or\nasync
 hronously waited on\, signaled\, and released by *any*\nparticipant core\,
  Linux and non-Linux\, with lifetime tracked\nsomewhere no single core own
 s outright.\n\n`dma_fence` solves the local version of this problem well\,
  but its\nlifetime and callback model assumes a single kernel's view of th
 e\nworld. We've developed Synx\, a global handle/refcount table for\nthis 
 exact cross-processor case\, and posted an RFC to dri-devel\nand linux-arm
 -msm describing the model and the specific properties\ndma-fence doesn't c
 urrently cover (refer supporting links).\n\nLetting any two remote process
 ors signal each other directly\,\nwithout routing through the Linux host\,
  cuts latency and avoids an\nunnecessary CPU wake-up. The solution has sho
 wn power and\nperformance benefits in the last few generations of Qualcomm
 \nmobile and XR chipsets\, and is gathering more use cases\,\nspecifically
  ones involving AI pipelines.\n\nChristian König redirects us to solve re
 mote signaling in\nuserspace: a userspace fence/signaling point model base
 d on\ndma-buf\, citing XE's userspace wait support\, `eventfd`\, and ROCm\
 nevents as precedent\, with possible common ground centered around\n`event
 fd`.\n\n## Discussion points\n\n(Supposed to evolve as email thread develo
 ps)\n- the viability of a userspace vs. kernel-space fence model\n- the cu
 rrent remote-signaling solutions from other vendors\n- scope as standalone
  or extend current framework. Define\n  interfaces.\n- model the subsystem
  crash recovery and cleanup\n\n## Key people\n\n- Bartosz Golaszewski\n- D
 mitry Baryshkov\, Srinivas Kandagatla (Driver Core MC\; also\n  Qualcomm c
 olleagues who reviewed Synx internally)\n- Christian König (engaged on th
 e RFC thread)\n- Faith Ekstrand / Xe authors (cited by König)\n\n## Suppo
 rting links\n- https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/5f90bb35-994e-48bd-bf49-3
 001dcefa2ee@amd.com/\n- https://lore.kernel.org/linux-arm-msm/202608060519
 15.2234481-1-pravinku@quicinc.com/\n- formatted duplicate @ https://lore.k
 ernel.org/dri-devel/20260806051915.2234481-1-pravinku@quicinc.com/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2507/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2487@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T173600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180200
SUMMARY:Automatic placement of accelerator workloads
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:sched_ext: The BPF extensible scheduler class MC · Balbir Sing
 h\, Lee Trager · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2487/\n\nPropo
 sal\n\nOn modern multi-socket\, multi-GPU systems\, application performanc
 e is often limited not by compute availability but by poor CPU/GPU localit
 y. Today\, customers are frequently instructed to rely on strict node pinn
 ing (numactl) and disabling NUMA balancing in order to avoid costly cross-
 node memory accesses. While effective in some cases\, this approach can be
  suboptimal\, since it limits workloads to a subset of available resources
  and it also requires deep system knowledge from the user.\n\nIn this talk
 \, we will explore how GPU-aware auto-affinitization can be implemented di
 rectly in the Linux scheduler using sched_ext\, enabling dynamic and trans
 parent placement of CPU tasks close to the GPUs they actively use. We disc
 uss the challenges of integrating scheduler-driven task migration with NUM
 A balancing\, handling mixed CPU/GPU thread groups\, and avoiding resource
  over-concentration. Finally\, we present design principles showing how sc
 heduler-level techniques can outperform static affinity policies\, while s
 implifying the user experience.\n\nKey Challenges\n\nInteraction with NUMA
  Balancing\n\nNaively migrating a task closer to its GPU can backfire if t
 he task's memory remains allocated on a remote NUMA node. In such cases\, 
 migration may increase memory access latency instead of reducing it.\nKey 
 questions explored:\nHow can sched_ext and NUMA balancing cooperate rather
  than conflict?\nWhen should migration be deferred or paired with memory m
 igration?\n\nThread Aggregation and Shared State\n\nMany GPU-enabled appli
 cations use multi-threaded CPU components\, where:\nOnly a subset of threa
 ds directly interacts with the GPU\nOther threads share memory\, locks\, o
 r cache lines with GPU-driving threads\nMigrating only GPU-active threads 
 may introduce new inefficiencies due to cross-node communication among thr
 eads of the same process.\n\nWe need to explore strategies for:\n\nSelecti
 ve thread aggregation\, migrating related threads together\nAvoiding overl
 oad of a single NUMA node or LLC\nBalancing locality benefits against para
 llel resource contention\n\nResource Saturation and Fairness\n\nAutomatica
 lly clustering tasks near GPUs risks oversubscribing CPUs\, LLCs\, or memo
 ry bandwidth on specific NUMA nodes. The scheduler must therefore avoid ov
 erloading a certain LLC or NUMA node\, make optimal use of system resource
 s and maintain fairness.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2487/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2365@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T183000
SUMMARY:Virtio and Vhost-User Ecosystem for Production and Automotive Syste
 ms
LOCATION:South Hall 1 B\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Birds of a Feather (BoF) · Dorinda Bassey\, Albert Esteve\, St
 efano Garzarella · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2365/\n\nThe
  vhost-user protocol started as a simple QEMU feature\, but it has grown i
 nto a standard used across the entire Linux virtualization community. Toda
 y\, it powers production workloads in the cloud (e.g. QEMU and cloud-hyper
 visor)\, lightweight environments like libkrun\, and modern automotive sys
 tems.\n\nHowever\, as the ecosystem expands\, we are hitting new "plumbing
 " bottlenecks. For example\, the protocol specification is still stored in
 side the QEMU documentation\, which makes it harder for other projects to 
 participate in its growth. At the same time\, automotive deployments are i
 ntroducing strict new needs\, such as safety certification\, real-time per
 formance\, and long-term support\, that impact everyone.\n\nThis BoF will 
 bring developers and users together to address these challenges and other 
 emerging topics across the ecosystem. Our goal is to establish better coor
 dination\, align technical roadmaps for both cloud and automotive\, and bu
 ild shared tests and tools to ensure all implementations work together.\n\
 nKey Discussion Topics:\n - vhost-user Specification Governance: Discuss o
 fficial location for the vhost-user specification.\n - Automotive Virtuali
 zation Requirements & Reference Platforms: Coordinate deployment needs acr
 oss Automotive platforms [1]\n - Real-time performance needs (camera\, dis
 play\, CAN)\n - Status Update: Ongoing work on vhost-user device (virtio-M
 edia\, virtio-can\, virtio-rtc\, etc)[2][3]\n - Graphics Stack Alignment: 
 on roadmaps for shared dependencies like rutabaga_gfx across libkrun\, cro
 svm\, vhost-device-gpu [4]\n - Shared Virtio Message-Parsing Crates: Evalu
 ate creating unified Rust crates for Virtio device specs to share virtqueu
 e parsing logic across backends and VMMs.\n - Open questions about:\n     
 vhost-user Specification Refinements: Identify protocol gaps\, missing fea
 tures\, and necessary standard improvements.\n     New Virtio/vhost-user D
 evice Proposals.\n\n[1] https://source.android.com/docs/automotive/virtual
 ization/reference_platform\n[2] https://lore.kernel.org/virtualization/ab2
 FlQTWUxl0KmlT@fedora/\n[3] https://github.com/rust-vmm/vhost-device/pull/9
 44\n[4] https://github.com/magma-gpu/rutabaga_gfx/issues/24\n\nKey Partici
 pants:\n  - Albert Esteve aesteve@redhat.com\n  - Stefano Garzarella sgarz
 are@redhat.com\n  - Sergio Lopez slp@redhat.com\n  - Manos Pitsidianakis m
 anos.pitsidianakis@linaro.org\n  - Alex Bennee alex.bennee@linaro.org\n  -
  Matej Hrica mhrica@redhat.com\n  - Milan Zamazal mzamazal@redhat.com\n  -
  Gurchetan Singh gurchetansingh@google.com\n  - Jorge E. Moreira jemoreira
 @google.com\n  - Matias Vara Larsen mvaralar@redhat.com\n  - Dorinda Basse
 y dbassey@redhat.com\n  - Alberto Ruiz aruiz@redhat.com\n  - Erico Nunes e
 rnunes@redhat.com\n  - John Ferlan jferlan@redhat.com\n  - Michael Tsirkin
  mst@redhat.com\n  - German Maglione gmaglion@redhat.com\n  - Stefan Hajno
 czi stefanha@redhat.com\n  - Timos Ampelikiotis t.ampelikiotis@virtualopen
 systems.com\n  - Harald Mommer harald.mommer@oss.qualcomm.com
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2365/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602m1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T175000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T181000
SUMMARY:Deferring SWIOTLB Bounce to Reduce Heavy CPU Utilization
LOCATION:Club E\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Confidential Computing MC · Ryan Afranji · https://lpc.events
 /event/20/contributions/2475/\n\nCoco VMs rely on bounce buffering to use 
 the guest's vCPUs to encrypt and decrypt data for DMA. This memory copy is
  performed via the SWIOTLB bounce buffer.\n\nPersistent disk read operatio
 ns handle completions within their storage interface’s interrupt handler
 . When SWIOTLB is disabled\, the interrupt handler is invoked only after t
 he DMA data transfer is completed. This makes the handler only responsible
  for quick clean up operations. With SWIOTLB enabled\, the read data is cu
 rrently entirely copied by a vCPU during this interrupt handler which dram
 atically increases handling time.\n\nThe memory copy is expensive and shou
 ld not be done within an interrupt handler since it prolongs the time inte
 rrupting other tasks. This problem is exacerbated by each storage interfac
 e’s interrupt handler being pinned to run only a specific vCPU. Heavy di
 sk read workloads can cause these pinned vCPUs to spend the entire duratio
 n of the workload in the storage interface’s interrupt handler thus star
 ving other tasks. Google has seen cases of these workloads causing softloc
 kups on the pinned vCPUs.\n\nI have a prototype which defers the SWIOTLB
 ’s memory copy out of the interrupt handler and into a workqueue. This e
 liminates the softlockup problem. It also allows for the memory copy to be
  scheduled on other vCPUs\; not just the ones pinned to the interrupt hand
 lers. For VMs with a higher vCPU count\, this approach increases bandwidth
  and IOPS. Although\, deferring the work has the drawback of slightly decr
 easing bandwidth for VMs with a low number of vCPUs and slightly increasin
 g latency regardless of vCPU count.\n\nI would like to gather feedback for
  my approach and discuss questions I have regarding its enablement. Is thi
 s slight latency increase tolerable? Should this feature be enabled by def
 ault for VMs with forced SWIOTLB or should the user of the guest kernel be
  responsible for enabling it? If we decide to enable it by default\, shoul
 d we only enable it for VMs with a higher number of vCPUs? If we decide th
 e user of the guest kernel is responsible for setting it\, how should they
  be able to set it?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2475/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2602f1@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T132022Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261007T183000
SUMMARY:Testing klp-build: from unit tests to CI
LOCATION:Club A\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Live Patching MC · Joe Lawrence\, Song Liu · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2528/\n\n## Abstract\n\nWith klp-build now merge
 d into mainline\, establishing an automated test\nsuite is the logical nex
 t step.  Historically\, maintenance of\nkpatch-build\, a similar livepatch
 ing creation tool\, has shown that the\nobject diff and correlation layer 
 accounts for the vast majority of\nregressions.  Variations across compile
 r versions\, optimization levels\,\nLTO modes\, CFI\, and architecture-spe
 cific handling in objtool's klp-diff\nengine introduce a broad surface are
 a where regressions can easily go\nundetected.\n\nThe existing livepatch k
 selftests provide a proven model within the\nsubsystem.  Developed alongsi
 de core livepatching functionality\, they\nhave caught real bugs\, e.g. co
 vering shadow variables\, callbacks\, and state\ntransitions. All while re
 maining maintainable over time.  klp-build\nwarrants a similar testing dis
 cipline\, tailored to the toolchain layer.\n\nWe propose a two-tier testin
 g strategy designed for both local developer\nworkflows and continuous int
 egration:\n\n**Unit tests:** Directly exercise `objtool klp diff` against 
 minimal\n`.orig.o` / `.patched.o` object pairs.  Scripted assertions verif
 y\nsymbol correlation\, demangling\, checksumming\, special-section handli
 ng\,\nand relocations in seconds without requiring a full kernel build.  T
 o\nadhere to kernel repository standards and avoid committing binary\narti
 facts\, a CLI-driven runner regenerates test object pairs on demand\nfrom 
 module source and a kernel tree using the host/target toolchain.\n\n**Inte
 gration tests:** Execute the complete klp-build pipeline against\ndedicate
 d in-tree test infrastructure (`CONFIG_KLP_BUILD_TEST`)\,\nvalidating modu
 le post-linking and runtime livepatch loading on target\narchitectures.\n\
 nBoth tiers target a comprehensive architecture (x86_64\, arm64\,\nLoongAr
 ch) and toolchain matrix (GCC\, Clang\, ThinLTO\, CFI).  Our goal is\nto i
 ntegrate these test suites into upstream CI infrastructure (e.g.\,\nCKI\, 
 KernelCI) to provide continuous regression testing across\nconfigurations.
 \n\nWe invite discussion with the community on several key architectural a
 nd\nintegration questions:\n\n**Unit test granularity:** Should tests targ
 et individual objtool\ncomponents (e.g.\, checksumming\, correlation) inde
 pendently\, or is a\nsingle `objtool klp diff` invocation per test case th
 e appropriate\nabstraction level?\n\n**In-tree vs. out-of-tree structure:*
 * Should integration tests reside\nin-tree within kselftests while unit te
 sts remain out-of-tree -- and\nwhere should the test corpus generation too
 ling live?\n\n**Cross-version maintenance:** How can in-tree integration t
 est code be\nstructured to remain stable across kernel releases without in
 curring\nunnecessary maintenance overhead?\n\n**CI pipeline integration:**
  Should we integrate this this multi-arch\,\nmulti-toolchain matrix into c
 ontinuous integration platforms like CKI\nand KernelCI?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2528/
END:VEVENT
